(1, 'The Hurricane', NULL, 1, '2000-10-01', 5, '<img src=\'images/rev_hurricane.jpg\' width=\'150\' height=\'212\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'The Hurricane follows the true-life story of champion boxer Rubin \"Hurricane\" Carter\'s wrongful conviction for murder and subsequent jail-cell efforts to clear his own name. \r\n<P>\r\nThe story itself is very compelling. A crooked cop frames Carter for a series of crimes throughout his childhood culminating in a final frame-up pinning the murder of a number of patrons in a white bar on the black Carter. Once in prison, Carter refocuses his life and pens an inspiring \"I didn\'t do it...and now I\'ve come to a very spiritual place in my life\" autobiography. The book makes it into the hands of a black teenager struggling to find his identity while surrounded by his three white teachers -- a whole other story. \r\n<P>\r\nThe teen and his handlers throw themselves into the crusade to get Carter freed -- and eventually do. \r\n<P>\r\nThis is the kind of movie where you want to like it more than you do. Like I said, the story is very interesting. The problem, however, lies in the storytelling. The film is just way too slow. I\'ll tell you what, at times it felt like I was watching paint dry. That\'s not to take anything away from the actors themselves; there are strong performances all around. The storytelling was simply very ill-paced. \r\n<P>\r\nOverall, not a bad rental choice...but have your finger perched on the fast-forward button. ', 'movie', 'Denzel Washington', NULL, 1999, 1),
(2, 'What Women Want', NULL, 2, '2001-02-20', 1, '<img src=\"images/rev_whatwomen.jpg\" width=\"150\" height=\"233\" alt=\"\" border=\"0\">', '', 'There are two kinds of people who like Mel Gibson movies: Type A people watch Mad Max, Braveheart and The Patriot and say they admire Gibson\'s portrayal of a hero, someone who stands up for themselves and others to face of an unstoppable foe, usually played by some evil English dude or Tina Turner. They appreciate his calm demeanor in the most tense of situations. These people are women or just gay and they are lying. They are really just checking out his ass. The other group, Type B, are the people who watch Gibson movies and admire him for how well he can chop off someone\'s head and then give the corpse a \"Nyuk nyuk nyuk\" in honor of the late great Curly Howard of the Three Stooges. If you are a Type A person, you\'re dream has come true. Type B people, please forget this movie was ever released. \r\n<P>\r\nThe movie begins with promise. Gibson plays a lady\'s man by night and by day is this advertising guy who specializes in commercials for gin and pro wrestling. That\'s it, that\'s where the movie should end, but no, the title of this movie is What Women Want. \r\n<P>\r\nThe movie foreshadows it\'s going to pussy out when his estranged fifteen year old daughter moves in with him for two weeks. It gets even worse when the Queen of Wussy Movies, Alan Alda, makes an appearance as Gibson\'s boss who turns Mel down for a phat promotion and instead gives the job to Helen Hunt. Any other Gibson movie would have taken a dramatic turn and had Hunt develop a British accent and Alda an eye patch, then have the two kidnap Gibson\'s daughter. Mel would have 48 hours to lead a ragtag group of misfits who would rescue his daughter, liberate Scotland and foil a plan that would stop the flow of the Mississippi River into New Orleans. \r\n<P>\r\nBut no, as I mentioned once before, the title of this movie is What Women Want. Instead of Gibson driving a nuclear powered funny car across the Alkaline Salt Flats, he gets the ability to read the mind of any woman whom he encounters. Once again, instead of using this power to benefit mankind and help his friends get laid, Gibson helps womankind. He wins the love of his daughter, he convinces Marisa Tomei that she\'s not crazy, lands the womens division of Nike for the advertising company with an empowering commercial about women, and he stops some geeky broad from overdosing on sleeping pills. Pretty lame if you ask me. The only good part of the movie was when Marisa Tomei and Helen Hunt dyke out in the bathroom of the gun store while Gibson was shooting Alan Alda... oh wait... that\'s the movie I was thinking about while I was watching this crappy one. That one with Hunt munching Tomei\'s carpet was called What Men Want. I\'m sure that one will be in your local pornographic video and novelty store by Easter. \r\n', 'movie', 'Mel Gibson, Helen Hunt', NULL, 2001, 1),
(3, 'Heat', NULL, 3, '2001-06-24', 5, '<img src=\'images/heat.jpg\' height=\'175\' width=\'121\'>', '', 'The DVD version of Heat contains just one special feature:  a collection of three theatrical trailers.  The third is okay, the first is better, but the second is awesome.  Excellent action, superb score, and touches on all the best parts of the movie.  \r\n<P>\r\nLike most pre-DVD era movies, it\'s not a big surprise to see no other special features, but it sure would be nice to have them.  But that second trailer is pretty solid, and considering that the disc typically retails at around $15, it\'s a hell of a value for the price.\r\n', 'dvd', 'Al Pacino, Robert De Niro', '', 1995, 1),
(4, 'Shaft', NULL, 3, '2001-06-24', 8, '<img src=\'images/shaft2000.jpg\' height=\'170\' width=\'113\'>', '', 'Shaft\'s special features are very good.  There are some token cast and crew interviews, and a\r\nlargely forgettable \"making of\" featurette that really is just the typical 15-minute infomercial\r\npromoting the film, rather than really examining the creation process.  Its most redeeming\r\nmoments are a five minute segment offering a glimpse of Isaac Hayes in the recording studio as\r\nhe scored the film.  \r\n<P>\r\nAlso included on the disc is a \r\nmusic video for R. Kelly\'s song, \"Bad Man\".  The video is decent, but nothing special, and the\r\nmusic is, well, it\'s R. Kelly.  I\'ll let you make your own judgment regarding that.  Seeing as\r\nhow Busta Rhymes was in the movie, I\'d rather have seen a video from him. \r\n<P>\r\nBut where the special features really shine are in the theatrical trailer and the theme song \r\nvideo from Isaac Hayes.  Both ride far on the strength of the Shaft theme music.  The trailer\r\nis exciting, cool, and hella-funky, and the music video is pure \"sex machine to all the chicks\"\r\nattitude.  \r\n<P>\r\nThey may not be the best around, but Shaft\'s special features offer a good deal of bang for the\r\nbuck.  8 out of 10.\r\n<P>', 'dvd', 'Samuel L. Jackson', 'The cat that won\'t cop out.', 2000, 1),
(5, 'My Dog Skip', NULL, 4, '2001-06-25', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/mydogskip.jpg\' width=\'125\' height=\'185\'>', '', '\"No, not another animal movie!!\" Hollywood, in some bizarre marketing strategy, came to the conclusion many years ago that movies about animals were somehow entertaining.  From \"Project X\" to \"Free Willie\" to \"Doctor Doolittle,\" producers have sought to capture the non Homo-sapiens market segment.  Perhaps more canine viewers became enthralled; however, few human beings caught on to the craze.\r\n<P>\r\nNow enter \"My Dog Skip\", if you hadn\'t guessed by the overly-imaginative title, another dog tale (pun fully intended).  Although the title may be mediocre, very surprisingly, this movie is not.\r\n<P>\r\n\"My Dog Skip\" is an outstanding movie chronicling a young Southern boy\'s travels through childhood in World War II America.  The reason that this movie is so good is because it is true.  \"My Dog Skip\" is based upon the memoir of Willie Morris, a former Rhodes Scholar and editor of Harper\'s magazine.  In essence, this was not a creation of Hollywood big-wigs, but of a terrific writer\'s story of childhood.\r\n<P>\r\nThe wonderful narrative voice-- spoken by Harry Connick Jr.-- creates a feeling somewhere between \"Wonder Years\" and \"A River Runs Through It.\"  The narration is poignant and heart-felt, aptly demonstrating the marvels of Morris\' pen.   The score written by William Ross is rich with simple melodies, beautiful orchestral lines, and touches of Americana music that adds to the movie and heightens the senses during the moments of drama.  Frankie Muniz (of \"Malcolm in the Middle\" fame) gives a convincing performance as the nine-year old Willie Morris.\r\n<P>\r\nAlthough a few of the storylines reek of the animal movie \"cheesyness\" (i.e., a fight with moonshiners), for the most part \"My Dog Skip\" is a well-written tale that is deep and serious at its core.  The viewer fully accompanies Willie\'s journey as he learns of love, wisdom and heroism on his way to becoming a man.  \"My Dog Skip\" truly bucks the trend of mediocre, mass-produced animal movies.  Bottom Line: 8.5/10  ', 'chick', 'Kevin Bacon, Frankie Muniz', '', 2000, 1),
(6, 'Swordfish', '', 3, '2001-06-30', 8, '<img src=\'images/swordfish.jpg\' width=\'158\' height=\'233\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'From the producers of <i>The Matrix</i> comes <i>Swordfish</i>, the thrilling tale of an attempt to hack into and steal a long-forgotten cache of government money.\r\nJohn Travolta leads the cast as the just-crazy-enough ringleader of the group, with sidekick Halle Berry, and Hugh Jackman as the convicted hacker who\'s just looking for\r\na way to rescue his daughter from the sinister clutches of his ex-wife and her porn-producing husband.\r\n<P>\r\nTravolta fans can rejoice:  he has definitely returned from the brink of disaster that he so carelessly flirted with in making <i>Battlefield: Earth</i>.\r\n<i>Swordfish</i> isn\'t perfect, but it\'s a helluva solid action movie whose opening sequence features what could possibly be the greatest ten seconds\r\nin the history of cinematography.  Picture the 360 degree rotation scenes of <i>the Matrix</i>.   But instead of encircling a single kung-fu move, the shot\r\ndepicts the effect of a massive explosion on an entire intersection of the city.  Absolutely unbelieveable, and literally breathtaking.\r\n<P>\r\nAs I said, it\'s not quite perfect.  The opening sequence is great for drawing the audience into the movie, but then the film\r\njumps back several days in time, and on more than one occasion I felt like my knowledge of the future cheated me out of part\r\nof the suspense of later scenes.  A chronological presentation may have been more effective.\r\n<P>\r\nAlso, as one who works in the computer industry, I must say I was thoroughly <u>un</u>impressed by the scenes of \"hacking\" in the movie.\r\nIt consisted mostly of throwing around just enough buzzwords to make the audience think something of substance is being said,\r\ncombined with the rather standard Hollywood depiction of computers as having tricked-out graphic interfaces dedicated to modelling\r\nthe most mundane of tasks (see <i>Jurassic Park</i> and <i>Independence Day</i>).  But then I remind myself that this isn\'t a computer movie; it\'s an action movie.  Nobody wants to\r\nsit around watching Hugh Jackman type away at some blinking UNIX prompt, so I suppose I can forgive the glossy veneer.\r\nAnd in some ways, the film makes very effective use of Hitchcock\'s concept of the \"McGuffin\": a section of the plot that\r\nremains undescribed because its details are absolutely immaterial to the story (for example, a quest to retrieve secret documents.  What\'s in the documents?  Um, I dunno, spy stuff.  Who cares?).\r\n<P>\r\nThe rest of the action scenes aren\'t quite on par with benchmarks like <i>Face/Off</i> or the latest Bond movies, but\r\nthey\'re very good in their own right and inspire more than a few <i>\"whoa!\"</i> \'s.  They advance the story extremely well with\r\nonly a spot or two of predictability, but the great thing about this movie is that even when you think you\'ve got it\r\nfigured out, they\'ll throw one more curveball that you didn\'t see coming.\r\n<P>\r\nOverall, while <i>Swordfish</i> isn\'t as thought-provoking as <i>the Matrix</i> nor as beautifully choreographed as <i>Face/Off</i>,\r\nit will nonetheless stand as an extremely solid and unconventional action flick.\r\n', 'movie', 'John Travolta, Hugh Jackman, Halle Berry', '', 2001, 1),
(7, 'John Waters', 'A Director Who Has a Place in the World of Film, But I\'m Not Sure Where.', 2, '2001-07-04', 0, '<img src=\'images/watersjohn.jpg\' width=\'98\' height=\'150\'>', 'images/watersjohn.jpg', '    I enjoy senseless acts of violence.  I enjoy things that are counter-culture.\r\n  I even think cross dressers are funny.  But yet I don\'t like John \r\nWaters.  This whole article came about while I was watching the Independent \r\nFilm Channel not too long ago and came upon a docmentary on John Waters.  \r\nNot knowing very much about this director or any of his films, I decided to \r\nwatch.\r\n<P>\r\n    As I soon learned from the IFC documentary, Waters has directed such \r\ncult classics as Pink Flamingos, Hairspray and Pecker.  He\'s worked with \r\ngreat actors and actresses such as Johnny Depp, Kathleen Turner and even \r\nporn star Traci Lords.  Once again, I should be a fan of Waters and his \r\nfilms.  Yet, there was something that I just didn\'t like about them, and I \r\nthink I figured it out.\r\n<P>\r\n     It is the goal by Waters to gross out the audience and alienate himself \r\nby society.  That\'s cool.  I have seen only three Waters films, <i>Cry Baby</i>, \r\nstarring Johnny Depp, Traci Lords and Iggy Pop in a campy story about guy \r\nwho cries alot; <i>Serial Mom</i>, starring Kathleen Turner, who is, of all things \r\na mother/serial killer; and <i>Pink Flamingos</i>, which I\'m really not sure what \r\nit was about but it starred a 300 pound cross dresser named Divine who \r\ntipped over a Christmas tree on \"her\" parents and ate dog shit.\r\n<P>\r\n     So where is Waters\' place in film society ?  He\'s the Pope of Trash \r\nwho\'s directly influenced Tom Green and other performers who are out there.  \r\nI admire Waters much like I admire Larry Flynt and Moby.  I really don\'t \r\nlike their work but at least it gives other films and artists someone to \r\nlook down on.\r\n', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(8, 'Finding Forrester', '', 1, '2001-07-06', 7, '<img src=\'images/finding_forrester.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'214\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'With <i>Finding Forrester</i>, director Gus Van Sant adheres to the old \r\nadage: stick to what you\'re good at.  In 1997, Van Sant rode an unlikely \r\nhorse by the name of <i>Good Will Hunting</i> straight into the winner\'s \r\ncircle known as box-office and critical success.  In 2000, he tried to \r\nrecreate that success with Finding Forrester, a heart-warming tale of an \r\ninner-city smarty-pants who doesn\'t want to own up to his intellectual \r\nprowess because his basketball buddies will slag him for being a wuss.  Or \r\nsomething like that.\r\n<P>\r\nMr. Smarty McSmart Guy quickly meets up with a somewhat aged author William \r\nForrester (played by Connery).  Forrester agrees to help the young chap \r\nimprove his writing skills in order to succeed in his new private school -- \r\non one condition: the boy can\'t reveal the relationship or the fact that he \r\nknows Forrester at all.\r\n<P>\r\nOf course, like in any feel-good movie, troubles ensue -- at least in the \r\nmiddle of the movie.  Smart-Guy faces cheating accusations, Forrester faces \r\na crippling fear of leaving the musty confines or his apartment, etc.  Rest \r\nassured, however, that through Forrester\'s gritty Scottish wits and \r\nSmarty\'s street-wise irreverance, trouble has met its match.\r\n<P>\r\nDon\'t get me wrong, this is not a bad movie.  In fact, on its own merits, \r\nit\'s not too bad at all.  However, I know I would have liked it a lot more \r\nhad I not seen (and enjoyed) <i>Good Will Hunting</i>.  Stylistically, \r\nhowever, the two are just too similar.  And, no, going into it I didn\'t \r\nknow the director was Van Sant.  Two minutes in, I figured it out.\r\n<P>\r\nSo, if you enjoyed <i>Good Will Hunting</i>, go rent <i>Finding \r\nForrester</i>.  If you\'ve never seen <i>GWH</i>, go rent <i>Finding \r\nForrester</i> and enjoy it without having your experience pre-tainted by \r\nassociation.\r\n', 'movie', 'Sean Connery, Rob Brown', 'You\'re the man now, dog!', 2000, 1),
(9, 'Before Night Falls', '', 4, '2001-07-05', 1, '<img src=\'images/before_night_falls.jpg\' width=\'100\' height=\'150\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'Civil War, Persecution, Escape, Poetic Narrative, Powerful Previews, A True Story...  These were the qualities that drew me towards the critically-acclaimed movie, <i>Before Night Falls</i>.  \r\n<P>\r\nThe movie chronicles the true tale of Cuban poet,  Reinaldo Arenas (played by Javier Bardem), and his escape from Cuba.  After viewing previews of this movie that reminded me of scenes from The Shawshank Redemption, I knew this was a must-see.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nBoy, was I wrong.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nThis was the most raunchy and terribly boring movie I have ever seen.  I honestly do not care how many foreign movie awards this film has won--it was plain awful.  The scenes detailing homosexual encounters and chalked full of male nudity offended the senses.  The story dragged and was not exciting in the least.  The narration was indecipherable due to Bardem\'s thick Cuban accent.  Although the direction seemed decent--there were several well-thought, imaginative scenes-- director  Julian Schnabel didn\'t do anything special.  However, the most disappointing part of this movie was that the previewed scenes that I was captivated with stunk within the context of the movie.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nSean Penn and Johnny Depp made small cameo appearances that are worth noting.  The narration seemed to be the movie\'s strongest  point; however, like I said, it was not understandable most of the time.  Bardem does do a fine job playing the lead role and plays the part of the nymphomaniac-effeminate-homosexual-dying of AIDS-poet very well--he pretty much is the movie.  Apart from his performance, nothing else stands out (in a positive way). \r\n<P>\r\n\r\nBottom line: Please, please, please do not see this movie.  I wish I hadn\'t.', 'movie', 'Javier Bardem', '', 2000, 1),
(10, 'This is Spinal Tap', 'Special Edition', 6, '2001-07-06', 9, '<img src=\'images/spinal_tap.jpg\' width=\'151\' height=\'214\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'The loudest band on earth now has their very own DVD.\r\n<P>\r\nTwenty years after its inception, Spinal Tap is on what is to be its\r\nfinal tour.  Filmmaker Marty DiBergi follows the ever-changing group on\r\ntheir US tour, keeping them on schedule while filming this\r\nrockumentary.  According to one music critic, \"The musical growth of\r\nthis band cannot even be charted.  They are treading water in a sea of\r\nretarded sexuality and bad poetry.\"  To which the band replies, \"Well\r\nthat\'s just nitpicking, isn\'t it?\"\r\n<P>\r\nIf it\'s features you\'re looking for, you\'ve come to the right place. \r\nThere are over 100 minutes of extras, including over an hour of deleted\r\nscenes, much of which very easily could have been made it into the\r\nfinal cut; brand new commentary from the band; and an exclusive\r\ninterview called, \"Catching Up With Marty DiBergi.\"  If all that wasn\'t\r\nenough, the DVD also includes six television spots, the original\r\ntheatrical trailer, and four of Tap\'s music videos: Hell Hole, Listen\r\nto the Flower People, Big Bottom, and Gimme Some Money.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nOne warning for those who plan on renting or purchasing this DVD is\r\nthat there are two older versions.  The first is just the movie, like\r\nwhat people saw in the theaters way back in \'84.  The second, the\r\nCriterion Edition, released in \'94, includes some cut scenes,\r\ninterviews with the founding members of the band and the director, and\r\nthe 20 minute demo (about the only thing on this edition which doesn\'t\r\nappear on the Special Edition) which was used to pitch the film to\r\nexecutives at Embassy Pictures.  But for the most bang for your buck,\r\nmake sure you get the 2000 Special Edition DVD, which includes the most\r\nfeatures and highest quality picture and sound.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nIf you\'ve ever been in a band, wondered what it would be like to be in\r\na band, or seen a band, you\'ll enjoy This is Spinal Tap: Special\r\nEdition and all of its features.\r\n\r\n', 'dvd', 'Rob Reiner, Michael McKean, Christopher Guest, Harry Shearer', '\"It\'s such a fine line between stupid... and clever.\"', 2000, 1),
(11, 'The Score', '', 3, '2001-07-14', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/score.jpg\' width=\'145\' height=\'213\' alt=\'\' />', '', '<i>The Score</i> follows Robert De Niro as an aging master thief on a quest to complete one \r\nfinal heist before settling down and starting a family with stewardess girlfriend Angela Bassett.\r\nA relatively hackneyed story, and the introduction of a rash and inexperienced partner isn\'t\r\nparticularly new either, even when that partner is played by Ed Norton.  And of course, there\'s\r\ngot to be some big cheese financing the whole shebang; in this particular case, the head\r\nhoncho is Marlon Brando.\r\n<P>\r\nThere\'s a great deal of tension early on as misgivings about this new partner, the motives of his boss,\r\nand the location of the target cause De Niro\'s character to wonder whether he should continue with\r\nthe caper or not.  But of course we all know he will (we saw it in the previews), and eventually they\r\nget down to the brass tacks of the planning process.  \r\n<P>\r\nUp to and including this part, the film\'s pace is rather slow and methodical, but it doesn\'t drag \r\ntoo much.  And given the context of De Niro\'s patient nature, it\'s expected that such a \r\nperfectionist thief would take his time to get things right.  Also keeping things interesting are\r\nNorton\'s excellent portrayal of his retarded janitor alter ego, and Brando\'s performance as the\r\nman in charge.  Make no mistake: this is not a reprise of the wise and calculating \"Godfather\".\r\nBrando\'s character here is more eccentric, and comes across as being somewhat like your Great-Uncle\r\nLouie who\'s always betting too much money at the track.  I wasn\'t totally thrilled with the\r\ncharacter, but Brando does a good job with what he\'s given.\r\n<P>\r\nFinally the heist unfolds.  The complexity of the entry is rivalled only by <i>Mission Impossible</i>,\r\nand it would be disappointing to see anything less. The audience is treated to some excellent thrills \r\nas various parts of the plan fall into and/or out of place, and of course there\'s some tricks and turns and \r\ndouble-crosses.  As I mentioned, the pace of much of the movie is\r\nrather slow and methodical, but in the end this serves to highlight the frantic pace of the escape,\r\nwhich is well filmed with <i>NYPD Blue</i>-like handheld camera shots to increase the feeling of danger\r\nand instability.  And the ending is a pleasant surprise which actually elicted applause from the\r\ntheater in which I viewed the film.\r\n<P>\r\nAll in all, <i>The Score</i> is a very solid entry in the \"big heist\" arena and although it shares\r\nmany common components with its peers, the performances are top-notch and the overall execution is\r\nsufficient to lend a fresh take on the genre. ', 'movie', 'Robert DeNiro, Edward Norton, Marlon Brando', '', 2001, 1),
(15, 'Jinkies!', 'A Scooby Doo Movie? Please, Scooby, Don\'t.', 9, '2001-07-20', 0, '<img src=\'images/scoobphoto_small.jpg\' width=\'97\' height=\'150\'>', 'images/scoobphoto.jpg', '<div class=\'quote\'>Scooby Dooby Doo </div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'authorName\'>Posted by Greg 2001-07-13 14:51:36 </div>\r\n<P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>i see on the Internet Movie Database that there\'s a Scooby-Doo movie coming out in 2002 w/ Freddie Prinze Jr. and Sarah Michelle Gellar as Fred and Daphne, respectively. also, \"Stu\" from Scream will play Shaggy. the only other big star of note is Rowan \"Mr. Bean\" Atkinson, presumably as the villain. \r\n</div>\r\n<P><hr align=\'left\'><P>\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>Zoinks! </div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'authorName\'>Posted by Jesse 2001-07-13 20:21:26 </div>\r\n<P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>if that picture is any indicator, that movie will totally suck worse than anything has ever sucked before. somewhere, the real Shaggy is either rolling over in his grave or rolling a fatty to make him forget about this sure-to-be box office bomb. \r\n<P>\r\none question: will the real-life Harlem Globetrotters be in it? \r\n<P>\r\nwhat\'s next? live action Jabber-Jaws? or Grape Ape?  \r\n </div>  \r\n \r\n<P><hr align=\'left\'><P>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>More Movie Ideas...</div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'authorName\'>Posted by Matt 2001-07-13 21:14:36 </div>\r\n<P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>...or the live action HR Pufnstuf movie starring, perhaps, Brian Dennehy? \r\n </div>  \r\n\r\n<P><hr align=\'left\'><P>\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>PS: </div>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'authorName\'>Posted by Matt 2001-07-13 21:23:18 </div>\r\n<P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>That picture makes me laugh hysterically, but will more than likely give me nightmares tonight. \r\n</div>\r\n<P><hr align=\'left\'><P>', 'other', '', 'Click to see the whole frightening pic...', 0, 0),
(16, 'Legally Blonde', '', 8, '2001-07-31', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/legally_blond.jpg\' width=\'143\' height=\'212\' alt=\'\' />', '', '<i>Legally Blonde</i> is a fascinating drama, fusing gritty real-life court \r\nscenes with authentic details from the fashion world to produce a legal \r\nthriller worthy of Grisham.  OK, not quite, but it is a pretty funny movie, \r\nand it has a simple and hilarious David-and-Goliath plot that left me \r\nthoroughly satisfied in the end.\r\n<P>\r\nReese Witherspoon plays Elle Woods, a stereotypical blonde sorority queen.  \r\nA fashion-design major, her one ambition is to marry her wealthy boyfriend, \r\nWarner Huntington II (Matthew Davis II) and get an enormous diamond \r\nengagement ring.  Instead, he breaks off the relationship, feeling that a \r\nblonde sorority girl is below his social standing.  He heads for Harvard Law \r\nSchool, and Woods decides to enroll in Harvard to win him back.  She goes \r\nthrough all the trials and tribulations of a law school student; the LSAT, \r\nthe personal statement, and the rigors of the Socratic method.  All the \r\nwhile, she faces the stigma of being just a \"dumb blonde\".   To top it off, \r\nshe must compete with Vivian Kensington (Selma Blair), a snobby east coast \r\nelitist, to get her boyfriend back.  Slowly, her persistence and hard work, \r\ncoupled with her expert knowledge of fashion trends help her through the \r\nadventures of law school and win over her condescending classmates.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Legally Blonde</i> is typical of a mindless comedy.  It is littered with deus \r\nex machina plot twists and hilariously unbelievable court scenes, while \r\nspoofing both the college sorority scene and the Ivy League Establishment.  \r\nWitherspoon was excellent playing the air-headed underdog who takes on \r\nHarvard and its cynical, worldly students.  Overall, it’s a good, \r\nlighthearted comedy that should entertain you as long as you don’t think too \r\nmuch.\r\n<P>', 'chick', 'Reese Witherspoon', '', 2001, 1),
(17, 'Unbreakable', '', 7, '2001-07-31', 8, '<img src=\'images/unbreakable.jpg\'>', '', 'M. Night Shyamalan created quite a stir in 1999 when he shocked everyone with The Sixth Sense. His follow-up is just as stunning and artsy featuring another strong cast from Willis, Jackson, and Penn. From the onset you\'ll find that this film reflects the same vibe as Sixth Sense: dark, melancholy, and introspective. Willis\' character, David Dunn, is more mysterious and I have come to the conclusion that Willis always plays these confused/down-and-out/enigmatic characters so masterfully.<p> The basic premise of the movie: as the lone survivor of a train crash, Dunn is forced to consider the possibility that he is indestructible. A modern day comic book hero who is not the product of an imaginative animator, but placed smack dab in the real world. This notion is proposed by Jackson\'s character, Elijah, who is an expert comic collector with a rare bone disorder. The idea is brilliant and Shyamalan has once again created one of the most original films in recent years. His forward-thinking shot-making, color schemes, plot twists, and characterization make this film a jewel. Real critics have pointed out the slow pace of the movie as a sandbag, but in all honesty the film is short, clocking in under two hours. It is probably the relative lack of action and psychological nature of the film which gives it the perception of dragging. But the build-up and release of pivotal scenes are so worth it in the end. It is an incredible effort all the way around from Shyamalan-- a new 21st century directing talent in the making. ', 'movie', 'Bruce Willis, Samuel L. Jackson, Robin Wright Penn', 'Night\'s 2nd Thriller No Sophomore Jinx', 2000, 1),
(18, 'Barbarella', '', 3, '2001-08-02', 2.5, '<img src=\'images/barbarella.jpg\' width=\'166\' height=\'217\' alt=\'\' />', '', '<i>Barbarella</i> is set many years into the future, where weapons and war have been unheard of\r\nfor ages.  Jane Fonda plays a bootylicious secret agent from Earth, who is sent out in search of an insane doctor that has created a positronic ray gun and threatens to disrupt the harmony of the universe.  In order to find this crazed \"Dr. Duran Duran\" (I\'m serious; there\'s no way I could\r\nmake such a thing up), Barbarella enlists the aid of her ship\'s effeminate onboard computer, \r\na blind angel, a bumbling revolutionary, and some child-hunting ice pirate whose skin is hairier \r\nthan the fur coat he wears.\r\n<P>\r\nThe imagery of the late 60\'s is prevalent throughout the flick, as is its morality.  The cockpit of \r\nBarbarella\'s spacecraft is a fur box.  No joke.  The aforementioned revolutionary is named \"Dildano\".\r\nSeveral scenes of the ship maneuvering through space look remarkably like an egg cell floating\r\nin a petrie dish.  And of course as Barbarella gets into one jam or another, she learns to trade\r\nnookie in exchange for various favors and means of escape.  This is considerably difficult for her\r\nat first because on Earth, sex has been replaced by a ritual of pill-taking and joining of hands\r\n(didn\'t I see that in <i>Demolition Man</i>?).  But after a couple of times doing it like they do on \r\nthe Discovery Channel, she\'s practically begging for it at every turn.\r\n<P>\r\nFor those upset by creations like <i>Barb Wire</i> and <i>Coyote Ugly</i>, Barbarella reminds us that\r\nour wonderful country has a long and proud tradition of movies whose sole purpose was to show off \r\nsome T & A. Since I watched this on TV, I can\'t be sure whether viewers of an unedited version \r\nwould actually\r\nget to see Fonda shagging, but I\'m guessing even that couldn\'t save this movie.  \r\nWatch this one only if your only other choice is <i>Weekend at Bernie\'s Part II</i>.\r\n', 'movie', 'Jane Fonda, Marcel Marceau', 'Astronauts can only dream of this kind of Tang...', 1968, 1),
(19, 'Planet of the Apes', 'TFM Goes Ape', 9, '2001-08-03', 7.5, '<img src=\'images/planet_apes_2001.jpg\' width=\'145\' height=\'214\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'With members of <b>Top Five Movies</b> flung far and wide throughout the country, opportunities for us to watch movies together have\r\nbecome scarce.  All that changed this weekend as writers <b>Ryan</b>, <b>Greg</b>, <b>Nick</b>, and <b>Brian</b>\r\njoined up near Akron, OH for a special viewing of <i>Planet of the Apes</i>.  The outing was preceded by an exhausting game of \r\nfootball as well as numerous objections by Brian, who acknowledged a bias against the film\'s star, Marky-Mark Wahlberg.\r\n<P>\r\nWe nevertheless proceeded to the cinema and sat back to enjoy the film.  Along the way I\'m sure we annoyed some fellow patrons\r\nas we whispered our thoughts and theories throughout the proceedings, but our actions paled in comparison to the woman who \r\nreceived a cell-phone call in mid-movie.  The group consensus seemed to be that the movie was enjoyable and even satisfying,\r\nthough some felt the need to qualify that with the phrase \"... for a movie with talking monkeys.\"  Below are some comments\r\nfrom each member about the experience...\r\n<P>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'authorName\'>Ryan Says:</div><P>\r\n\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\nI went into \"Planet of the Apes\" expecting very little.  The combination of \r\na seemingly corny plot and Mark Wahlberg was destined for failure.  However, \r\nafter 2 hours of the epic human struggle against ape domination, I was \r\nconverted.  First, Tim Burton did for \"Planet of the Apes\" what he did for \r\n\"Batman\", creating a dark and disturbing feel for the movie through settings \r\nand costumes.  Second, Thade and Attar were masterful as the main \r\nantagonists.  Thade, a bloodthirsty general whose habit of hissing and \r\nsniffing made him completely revolting and evil.  I did think he was a \r\nfascist, but then fascists don\'t show as much deference to the Senate.  \r\nThade patiently waited for martial law to be declared before moving his \r\ntroops.  Attar was equally convincing.  I have been caught bellowing \"BOW \r\nYOUR HEADS\" not a few times since seeing the movie.  The third and most \r\nfascinating part was the ending.  The time travel issues involved are still \r\nunresolved and left me thinking about the movie for a long time afterwards.  \r\nAll in all, it was a great movie.  An 8.5/10.\r\n</div>\r\n<P>\r\n\r\n\r\n	<div class=\'authorName\'>Greg Says:</div><P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\n	The comparisons of this movie to the original are inevitable, but I\'d like to think that some of the film\'s lesser moments \r\nwere actually intentional bits of campiness intended to pay homage to its predecessor.  \r\nThis is clearly more of an action film than a thought-provoking\r\nsci-fi piece, but the issues of time travel and the creation of the planet\r\ninjected some intelligence into what otherwise might have disintegrated into yet another Hollywood blockbuster.\r\nThe aggressive animal nature of the apes in this film contrasts greatly with their civilized personalities in the \r\noriginal and is a bit unsettling at first, but I think Burton may have intended it as a parallel to mankind\'s own beastly\r\nnature.  Look for Charlton Heston in a well disguised cameo.  Overall I rate it 7 opposable thumbs up.\r\n</div>\r\n<P>\r\n\r\n	<div class=\'authorName\'>Nick Says:</div><P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\nA love triangle between two humans and an ape? I wasn\'t too sure about that.  The character development in this movie was \r\nzero as was the development of almost all of the relationships.  That being said, parts of this movie reminded me of \r\n\'Contact\' (a very good thing); and the ending was superb and basically saved the movie in my mind.  Bottom line: 7.0 rating\r\n</div>\r\n<P>\r\n\r\n	<div class=\'authorName\'>Brian Says:</div><P>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\n	I fought it hard. I swore I would not watch it. To be honest with you, I\'ve never been interested in the originals. \r\n	But to avoid being the party pooper, i \"strapped it on\" for the gang. I was pleasantly surprised by the film\'s depth \r\n	even though there were too many instances of enormous cheese-ity. To clarify: I HAVE NO PROBLEMS WITH MARK WAHLBERG. \r\n	This was a little misguided and rather a tendency to associate him with mass-audience films that cost a schluckazillion \r\n	bucks to make. This may not even be the case, just pure stereotyping on my part. Wahlberg turned in a solid performance. \r\n	Anywhoo, I thought that there were some interesting lessons to be drawn from the film regarding not only relationships \r\n	between man and animal, but man and fellow man. Its message was loud and clear, meant for the masses-- not sneaky \r\n	for the cerebral. And the ending would\'ve had Dr. Emmitt Brown hitting his head on the porcelain a couple more \r\n	times to figure out. The film was worth my time. \r\n</div>\r\n', 'movie', 'Mark Wahlberg, Tim Roth', '', 2001, 1),
(20, 'Snatch', '', 3, '2001-08-05', 6.5, '<img src=\'images/snatch.jpg\' width=\'149\' height=\'200\'>', '', '<i>Snatch</i> is the fast-paced tale of the fate of a rather\r\nlarge stolen diamond.  Naturally, a rock of such value attracts many\r\ninterested parties, each of which tends to maintain possession of the\r\njewel just long enough to celebrate their victory, before once again\r\nlosing it.  The film quickly becomes a frantic game of three-card \r\nmonte, and the viewer is forced to just sit back and wait to see\r\nwhere it all ends up.\r\n<P>\r\nAnd that\'s the primary problem with the movie.  There\'s not a whole lot\r\nof character development, so it\'s difficult to decide who we should\r\nbe rooting for in this tremendously complicated game of cat and mouse.\r\nThe first half-hour is especially tough because we have to very quickly\r\ndigest 15 or 20 different characters, most of whom speak in a very\r\nthick Cockney accent which further obfuscates their identities and \r\nmotivation.\r\n<P>\r\nDespite its shortcomings, <i>Snatch</i> is extremely well filmed and\r\nedited, and in all honesty the movie probably wouldn\'t have worked at\r\nall if director Guy Ritchie had done it any other way.  It\'s something\r\nof a mix between a dark comedy and an action film, along the lines of\r\n<i>American Psycho</i> meets <i>Reservoir Dogs</i>.  The aforementioned\r\nlack of character development leaves the ending somewhat unfulfilling;\r\nas if we just sat and watched this game of hot potato, but in the end\r\nhave nothing to show for it but 100-some minutes of elapsed time.\r\nStill, it\'s a moderately entertaining film with a few excellent\r\ncomedic moments and enough good cinematography to be worth a look.', 'movie', 'Brad Pitt, Benicio Del Toro', '', 2000, 1),
(21, 'Arlington Road', '', 8, '2001-08-08', 5.5, '<img src=\'images/arlington_rd.jpg\'>', '', 'Due to the high marks that <i>Arlington Road</i> received from fellow TFM\'ers, I \r\nfelt obligated to sit down and watch it.  I was sorely disappointed.  The \r\noverall idea was good, but the plot development was flawed and the casting \r\nwas questionable, rendering a potentially great movie little more than \r\naverage.\r\n<P>\r\nJeff Bridges plays Michael Faraday, a college professor whose wife was killed \r\nin the line of duty as an FBI agent.  Her death motivates him to study \r\nAmerican terrorists and extremist groups.  Naturally conspiratorial, Faraday \r\nbegins to develop suspicions about his new neighbors the Langs, played by \r\nTim Robbins and Joan Cusack.  As Faraday digs deeper, he discovers his \r\nneighbors are not who they appear to be.  Convinced that they have something \r\nplanned, he continues to investigate the Langs and their past, discovering \r\nties with some of the terrorists he has studied.  The movie climaxes as \r\nFaraday desperately attempts to expose his neighbors and their secret life.\r\n<P>\r\nPerhaps it\'s my own fault, but I couldn\'t look at Jeff Bridges without \r\nthinking of \"The Dude\" in \"The Big Lebowski\".   I felt that he gave a \r\nwhining, unstable quality to the character that didn\'t fit with the plot.  \r\nThe movie would have been better had he been more rational and professorial \r\nin the role.  My biggest complaint is that too much is left unexplained.  A \r\nmovie with radical and shocking plot twists must strike a delicate balance \r\nbetween giving adequate explanation in order to be believable, and leaving \r\nenough to the imagination to maintain the surprise element.  <i>Arlington Road</i> \r\nfailed to explain enough.  I had too many basic questions of \r\nbelievability at the end of the movie to be satisfied.  It was worth \r\nwatching, but not worth the accolades some irrationally exuberant TFM\'ers \r\nhave given it.  My rating 5.5/10.\r\n', 'movie', 'Jeff Bridges, Tim Robbins, Joan Cusack', '', 1999, 1),
(22, 'Jurassic Park III', '', 3, '2001-08-10', 4.5, '<img src=\'images/jurassicpark3.jpg\' height=\'200\' width=\'147\'>', '', 'It wasn\'t that bad.  It had a few decent moments.  And to be\r\nhonest, I <i>had</i> wanted to see it, but I\'d intended to wait for \r\nthe DVD release.  But when we arrived at the theater to discover\r\nthat the long awaited <i>American Pie II</i> was sold out, our choices\r\ncame down to <i>Jurassic Park III</i> or <i>America\'s Sweethearts</i>.\r\nWe chose the former, and I stand by that decision, given the \r\ncircumstances.\r\n<P>\r\nThe most enlightening thing I can say about this movie is that there\r\nwas more tension outside the theater between Brian and some big-mouthed\r\n(and equally big-boned) chick than there was on the whole island full \r\nof dinosaurs.  The whole movie feels very rushed.  There\'s some token\r\ndiscussion of raptors at the beginning, mostly just to remind us of\r\nhow scared we\'re supposed to be of them.  Yeah, yeah, we get it, they\'re\r\nsmart and scary.  Sorry, but no one\'s got the market on \"smart and\r\nscary\" cornered like Hannibal Lecter.\r\n<P>\r\nIt was obviously \r\nthe goal of the editing crew to get into the action as quickly as\r\npossible, probably to help cover up the flimsy story.  The plot,\r\nby the way, was apparently thrown together from the parts of \r\nMichael Crichton\'s books that didn\'t make it into the first two films.\r\nIn fact, it wouldn\'t surprise me if this movie was actually\r\njust footage salvaged from the cutting-room floor of those earlier\r\nflicks, spliced together with a few shots of Tea Leone and a new \r\nraptor that looked an awful lot like Stripe from <i>Gremlins</i>.\r\n<P>\r\nI also took issue with the soundtrack.  John Williams\' majestic \r\n<i>Jurassic Park</i> score is tossed \r\nin to try and force us into feeling wonderment towards the island\r\nand its inhabitants, but the simple fact is that we\'ve seen it all\r\nbefore and we\'re really just waiting to see some blood.  The use of\r\nthat once grand music here cheapens it immensely.  \r\n<P>\r\nAnd speaking of blood, don\'t blink or you\'ll miss it.  <i>Scream</i>\r\ntaught us that the death toll is supposed to go up in the sequels,\r\nbut I was hard-pressed to find much people-eating carnage here.  They\r\nreally dropped the ball on that one.  The whole time you feel\r\nlike you\'re watching an amusement park ride:  some people get bounced\r\naround and scream a lot, but we all know that in the end, they\'re\r\ngoing to be just fine.\r\n<P>\r\nAnd speaking of the end, let\'s not.  Frankly, it\'s pretty dumb.\r\nI\'m willing to cut the writers some slack since the rescuers could\r\nhave traced the location of the phone, even though that was never\r\nsuggested in the movie itself.  Basically, though, it\'s one of those\r\nendings you just accept and feel thankful that it did indeed end.\r\n<P>\r\nSo, here was a movie made just for the money and to use up the extra\r\nfilm from the first two movies.  But you knew that before you read\r\nthis, and so did I.  So I can\'t honestly say I was disappointed.\r\n<i>Jurassic Park III</i> is worth a glance, but any more than a glance\r\nwill just bore you with same stuff you saw in the first movie.  \r\nMostly, though, it\'ll make you wish more than ever that all sequels \r\nwould be outlawed, and that Sam Neill would find some work that\r\nactually puts his talents to good use.\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n', 'movie', 'Sam Neill, Tea Leone, William H. Macy', 'Will somebody please just nuke the place?  Sheesh...', 2001, 1),
(23, 'Memento', '', 2, '2001-08-13', 9.5, '<img src=\'images/memento.jpg\' width=\'100\' height=\'150\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'From the people who did not bring you such a summer flop as <i>Tomb Raider</i>,  a \r\nfilm that does not feature high priced actors like <i>The Mummy Returns</i>, a film \r\nthat no one seemed to herald because it was well written, well directed and \r\nwell acted in a summer of Check-Your-Brain-At-The-Door movies, comes \r\n<i>Memento</i>.\r\n<P>\r\nAlright this is the deal.  Guy Pearce (the skinny guy with glasses from \r\n<i>LA Confidential</i>) plays Leonard Shelby, a man avenging the death and rape of \r\nhis wife.  Seems simple, right?  Well, hold on, Laura Croft.  When Leonard\'s \r\nwife is attacked he is hit in the head causing a rare but very factual \r\ncondition that stops him from having any short term memory.  The last thing \r\nhe remembers is the incident and nothing that happens afterward.  To \r\nremember things he takes Polaroid pictures of things and tatoos information \r\non his body.  Rounding out the cast are Carrie-Anne Moss and Joe \r\nPantoliano both from the film <i>the Matrix</i>.\r\n<P>\r\nSo what\'s so great about this film?  Well other than there being no \r\napes, dinosaurs or Taco Bell promotional tie-ins, the film is shot from the \r\nend and runs backwards to the begining. So the first thing you see is Pearce \r\nshooting Pantoliano in the head and then taking a Polaroid of the dead body. \r\nNow the audience becomes Leonard Shelby, not knowing what happened before \r\nthat.  Brilliant.\r\n<P>\r\nAre there any negative points to the movie?  Only the fact that I had never even heard of it until a friend recommended it to me.  I hadn\'t seen any of the actors on the late night circuit or even seen the trailer on TV. Summer movies seemed to be reserved only for over budgeted crap or the typical stand-by, \r\nI-use-to-be-on-SNL-and-I-have-another-retarded-movie-where-all-the-funny-scenes-are-on-the-trailer movie. Memento isn\'t like that, lucky for me I caught it at the cheap theatre.\r\n<P>\r\nIn closing, I recommend all of you who didn\'t see <i>Memento</i>, which should \r\nbe just about everybody, to buy faster cars and go over to your local cheap \r\ntheatre and see this film before it leaves!', 'movie', 'Guy Pearce, Carrie-Anne Moss, Joe Pantoliano', '', 2000, 1),
(24, 'American Pie 2', '', 7, '2001-08-23', 7, '<img src=\'images/americanpie2.jpg\' width=\'156\' height=\'220\'>', '', 'OK, we all know that <i>American Pie</i> was the surprise hit of 1999. As for the second, no other sequel in recent years has conjured such anticipation. With the first the plot was fairly original, the characters timeless, and many scenes-- absolutely unforgettable. \r\n<P>\r\nAP 2 begins at the close of the first year at college. The original crew consisting of Jim, Oz, Finch, and Kevin, return home and quickly set up shop at a lakeside palace for a summer of fun and females. What ensues are equally memorable scenes such as encounters with \"lesbos\", porn mishaps, band camp blunders, and far eastern sex practices. \r\n<P>\r\nThere is Jim preparing for the arrival of Nadia with the help of band geek/sadomasochist Michelle, Kevin trying to make sense of a new relationship with Vicky, Oz patiently waiting for his girl to return from Europe, and Finch studying tantra awaiting the return of Stiffler\'s mom. I should mention that the legendary Steve Stiffler has been invited as a way to finance the excursion. Of course with him comes his classic party-boy-stiffmeister style. \r\n<P>\r\nWhat makes this sequel so worthy is the fact that EVERY siginficant character returns. Who could forget the cool and seasoned Jessy or Jim\'s HILARIOUS try-hard dad or Nadia or THE SHERMINATOR. Even the MILF guys are back!!! It is a film that masterfully builds on the riotous themes of the first. \r\n<P>\r\nMy advice to the directors though: STOP HERE. We all know Hollywood and my hunch is that these actors will need a paycheck down the road. Then again, Sean Penn got his start in <i>Fast Times at Rigdemont High</i> along with many other budding stars. <P>Do yourself a favor and see this movie, it will not disappoint.', 'movie', 'Many Actors/Actresses', '', 2001, 1);
INSERT INTO `Article` (`ID_`, `Title`, `Subtitle`, `Author_ID_FK`, `Article_date`, `Rating`, `Image`, `Image_fullsize`, `Text`, `Type`, `Starring`, `Caption`, `Release_year`, `QA`) VALUES
(25, 'American Pie 2', '', 2, '2001-08-25', 8, '<img src=\'images/americanpie2.jpg\'>', '', '   <I>American Pie 2</I> would be just like a sequel to <I>The Big Chill</I> if Kevin \r\nCostner didn\'t die and had sex with a pie instead.  I\'m pretty sure at least, \r\nI never finished watching that movie, I kept falling asleep everytime that \r\nJeff Goldblum guy started talking about the dinosaurs eating flies or \r\naliens or something.\r\n<P>\r\n    Anyway, here\'s the skinny on <I>American Pie 2</I>.  It\'s been one year since \r\nKevin (Thomas Ian Nicholas), Jim (Jason Biggs), Paul Finch\r\n(Eddie Kaye Thomas)  and Oz (Chris Klein) went on a crusade to lose \r\ntheir virginity at the senior prom.  They\'ve all gone to college and reunite \r\nfor summer vacation at a phat beach house.  Sexual deviance and hijinks \r\nensue.\r\n<P>\r\n    Is this a good film? Yea, I think so.  There are some funny scenes that \r\nthe trailers didn\'t give away, like other movies.  The film also brought \r\nback just about everybody from the first one too.  Everyone from Stiffler, \r\nThe Sherman-ator, Nadia and even the M.I.L.F. dudes.\r\n<p>\r\n    Were there any negatives of the film?  Yea, it seemed too rushed.  The \r\ndirector, James B. Rogers, tried to give everyone equal screen time.  It \r\nwent from Jim trying to hook up with Nadia to Finch going on about Stiffler\'s \r\nmom to Stiffler doing something homoerotic.  The plot seemed to jump around \r\nalot.\r\n<P>\r\n     Another negative was that <I>American Pie 2</I> had to live up to the \r\noriginal.  The first one had so many classic scenes.  This one had some \r\nfunny stuff, but not like the first one. Nadia got topless in the first \r\none, I expected to see her box in this one and I was let down.  Oh well.\r\n<P>\r\n    Overall this is a good movie to see.  It\'s good for a few laughs and \r\nit\'ll be a good drinking movie went it comes out on DVD.\r\n', 'movie', 'Jason Biggs, Shannon Elizabeth', '', 2001, 1),
(26, 'Jay And Silent Bob Strike Back', '', 2, '2001-08-25', 9, '<img src=\'images/jay_silent_bob.jpg\'>', '', '  The year 1994 brought the world <I>Clerks</I>, Kevin Smith\'s first film.  Critics \r\nand movie-goers alike embraced two convenience store drug dealers named Jay \r\nand Silent Bob.  Now it\'s seven years later and the two pimped out Jedi \r\nKnights have their own film.\r\n<P>\r\n  Here\'s the story, Jay and his heterosexual life partner, Silent Bob, find \r\nout that the comic book based on them is being turned into a movie. That \r\nsounds good.  Until they find out that punks on the internet were talking \r\ntrash about them.  Nothing is more important that protecting your rep.  So \r\nthe two go to Hollywood to wreck the film.\r\n<P>\r\n  This is the movie I have been waiting for all summer.  Did it live up to \r\nits expectations?  I think so.  I am a die hard Kevin Smith fan and as I \r\nunderstand it, this will be the last film with Jay and Silent Bob and the \r\nentire Clerks Universe.  They did lots of <i>Star Wars</i> jokes, spoofed other \r\nclassic films and brought back other Kevin Smith movie alumni like Jeff \r\nAnderson and Joey Lauren Adams.\r\n<P>\r\n  The problem with the film is that if you weren\'t a fanatical <I>Clerks</I> fan \r\nyou\'d probably spend the entire time wondering why that one guy in the front \r\nwith the trench coat and Daredevil shirt is laughing so hard.  A casual fan \r\nwouldn\'t have gotten the Walt Flannagan\'s dog joke or understood why Holden \r\nwished he was still dating a lesbian.  Okay, some things are universal.\r\n<P>\r\n   All in all, I liked the film. There was a lot of funny stuff and some \r\ngood spoofs.  Charlie Average Movie-Go-Seer might not give <I>Jay & Silent Bob</I>\r\nthe high rating I did, but they probably feel bad for any independent \r\ncontracter who was working on the reconstructed Death Star in <I>Jedi</I>.\r\n', 'movie', 'Ben Affleck, Shannon Elizabeth, Jason Mewes', '', 2001, 1),
(27, 'Almost Famous', '', 3, '2001-09-14', 9.5, '<img src=\'images/almost_famous.jpg\' width=\'148\' height=\'215\'>', '', '<i>Almost Famous</i> is the story of fifteen-year-old William Miller\'s quest to become a successful rock journalist.  William\'s life is a constant conflict between the rebellious undertones of the rock \'n roll he adores, and the love and sense of obligation he feels towards his oppressive and utterly square mother.  In the consequence-free atmosphere of the early 70\'s, her constant smothering and admonitions against sex and drugs could easily push him away.  But keeping William on the straight and narrow path is the realization that no amount of hard partying will ever make him cool.  He\'s destined to always be a relatively mature mind trapped in a youngster\'s body, hopelessly isolating him from both his peers and his idols.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nSeeking to break into the exciting world of rock journalism, William finds literary guidance from Lester Bangs (Philip Seymour Hoffman), editor of the underground rock mag \"Creem\" and fellow un-hipster.  While pursuing a story for Lester, William meets up with the entrancingly beautiful \"band-aid\" Penny Lane (Kate Hudson) and the upstart rockers \"Stillwater\", fronted by Jeff Bebe (Jason Lee) and Russell Hammond (Billy Crudup).  He connects well with the band and when Rolling Stone comes calling for his services, William pitches a Stillwater article to the mag.  Soon he\'s on an all-expense paid trip around the country, documenting the band\'s triumphs and struggles.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nI can only think of one significant complaint about this movie:  there was way too much Elton John music.  It seemed like every tender moment was punctuated by Elton John.  I can tolerate one or two such tunes, but this one had too many. \r\n<P>\r\nOther than that, <i>Almost Famous</i> is one of those movies that just seems to have it all.  In fact, it\'s difficult to find anything much to say about it at all because the film does so many things so well, that it\'s difficult to even know where to begin.  It can move from extremely funny to deeply touching and back again in the span of several minutes.  Like <i>Spinal Tap</i>, this is a must-see for anyone who\'s been in a band, or has ever been moved by music at all.  Which should be just about everyone.  Look for a great bit part by SNL\'s Jimmy Fallon.', 'movie', 'Billy Crudup, Kate Hudson, Jason Lee', '', 2000, 1),
(28, 'Rock Star', '', 3, '2001-09-22', 7, '<img src=\'images/rockstar.jpg\' width=\'152\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', '', '<i>Rock Star</i> brings us Marky-Mark Wahlberg as Chris Cole, an 80\'s high-school grad who fronts a tribute band for the fictional group, \"Steel Dragon\".  Cole\'s insistence on taking the songs too seriously soon leads to his ejection from his own band.  But wonder of wonders, he suddenly gets a call from Steel Dragon\'s guitarist who\'s looking to replace their band\'s singer.  Cole is quickly snapped up in his dream come true, only to find that it\'s not all it\'s cracked up to be.\r\n<p>\r\nAnd that\'s where it gets predictable as hell.  Did any of us really expect him to live happily ever after?  Nope.  This movie is one of those films that looks like a nice, juicy steak on the outside, but when you bite into it, you find sugar and spice and all the other elements of a typical chick-flick.    There are some very mild rockstar-type orgy scenes that are only there to keep the men interested, while the director is really advancing his insidious relationship-oriented themes.  In fact, the sudden discovery of a male \"member\" in a place you wouldn\'t expect (thankfully it\'s not seen, only implied) will change your perspective on earlier scenes and cement the fact that your girl will take more home from this flick than you will.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nAs the movie progresses, we also are subjected to a number of \"recurring themes\" that besides being easily forseen, left me expecting Elton John to suddenly burst onto the screen singing \"The Circle of Life\".  Throughout the film, I couldn\'t get over the whole \"it\'s all been done\" feeling.  You\'re not going to find any new insight into the rock \'n roll lifestyle here.  <i>Rock Star</i> should have been made five years ago, before we all burned out on \"Behind the Music\" stories.\r\n<p>\r\n\r\nBut like <i>Planet of the Apes</i>, Marky-Mark\'s got one last rabbit in his hat, and the wry \"poetic justice\" ending of <i>Rock Star</i> almost makes up for the other shortcomings in the movie.  Again, the basics of the finale aren\'t particularly surprising, but there\'s a little twist in the details and circumstances that when you think about it, is pretty clever.  So I\'ve gotta give some props there, but it\'s still only enough to raise this film\'s rating by about half a point.  So that computes to a 7.0 for gratuitous mascara and nipple piercing.\r\n\r\n', 'movie', 'Marky-Mark Wahlberg, Jennifer Aniston, various actual rock stars', '', 2001, 1),
(29, 'Rosie O\'Donnell', 'An Actress Who Has a Place in the World of Film, but I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2001-09-24', 0, '<img src=\'images/odonnell.jpg\' width=\'97\' height=\'129\' alt=\'\' />', 'images/odonnell_big.jpg', '     When I did the Top Five Tom Hanks movies I came to the dilemma of which film to put first.  I really liked Hanks character in <i>A League of Their Own</i> \r\nand his portrayal of Joe in <i>Joe versus The Volcano</i>.  I went with the latter \r\nbecause of my dislike for Rosie O\'Donnell and my like for volcanoes.\r\n<P>\r\n    Then I began to think, as I often do, about why I dislike Rosie \r\nO\'Donnell.  Yes, she does have a huge head.  Yes, she is hate monger who \r\nbrought the Koosh balls into pop culture.  But that wasn\'t it.\r\n<P>\r\n    I\'ve narrowed down my dislike for her to two reasons.  The characters \r\nshe plays are anti-male and the Betty Rubble incident.  Yes, I have only two \r\nreasons for not liking someone.  That\'s two more than most.\r\n<P>\r\n     Rosie O\'Donnell always plays the role of women who men hate in real \r\nlife.  Who is the arch enemy of any man at any bar ?  The fat friend of the \r\ngirl you\'re trying to hook up with that night.  Rosie O\'Donnell has made a \r\nbrilliant career of this.  Example:  <i>Beautiful Girls</i> ( 1996 )  Rosie \r\nO\'Donnell doesn\'t play one of the two love interests of Matt Damon nor does \r\nshe portray the 14 year old girl whom Tim Hudson has a major jones for.  \r\nInstead, Rosie O\'Donnell plays the fat beautician who tells all the women in \r\nthe movie that men are scum. How many times has this happen to men in real \r\nlife.  Rosie O\'Donnell doesn\'t know you, but she is sure she knows your \r\ntype.  Whatever that means.  Is she being type cast ?  Yes, but she could \r\nbreak out of that mold by  portraying a door to door dildo saleswoman \r\nwho gets all the chicks in the movie to dyke out or something.  Hollywood is \r\nfilled with roles like that.  She should really talk to her agent.\r\n<P>\r\n      Secondly, and this one hurt the most, Betty Rubble.  Growing up I \r\nwatched the Flintstones religiously.  The Flintstones had that Honeymooners \r\nconnection except for one.  Fred Flintstone was definitely Jackie Gleason.  \r\nHe talked loud and was boisterous but was always good to his wife.  Barney \r\nRubble was Art Carney, the quirky best friend.  Wilma Flintstone was \r\ndefinitely Alice.  But it was Betty who didn\'t act like Trixie.  Betty \r\nRubble didn\'t nag Barney. She looked good on the cartoon.  Her jet black \r\nhair, her 36-24-36 measurements all beneath a blue mastodon fur skirt.  She \r\nseemed like the type of wife that would greet her husband with a beer and a \r\nhummer when he came home from work.  The perfect chick. In 1994 when it came \r\ntime to create a live action Flintstones movie, I was excited.  John Goodman \r\nas Fred, Rick Moranis as Barney and Carmen Electra as Betty.  This could be \r\ngreat, but no. Enter Rosie O\'Donnell.\r\n<P>\r\n    Rosie O\'Donnell has 22-48-62 measurements, no mastodon hide could be \r\nlarge enough to cover her fat Oreo-eating ass.  I was destroyed.  I watched \r\n<i>The Flintstones Movie</i> in horror.  Rick Moranis seemed to cower in fear \r\nevery time he had a scene with Rosie for fear that her giant head would crush \r\nhim.  I feared for his life too.  I feared that Rosie O\'Donnell\'s portrayal \r\nwould bring brimstone and locusts.\r\n<P>\r\n     So that\'s it.  That\'s why I dislike Rosie O\'Donnell so much. Where is \r\nher place in world of film ?  As a fat man hater who quite possibly ruined \r\nthe sexiest role in film ever.', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(30, 'Steve Buscemi and Peter Lorre', 'Two Actors With a Place in the World of Film, But I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2001-10-10', 0, '<img src=\'images/buscemi_lorre.jpg\' width=\'156\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', 'images/buscemi_lorre_big.jpg', 'He\'s thin and creepy looking to begin with. In films, he always plays the bad guy\'s sidekick\r\n that seemed to be on his own side the entire time. He was the character \r\n    that the good guy can\'t trust but needs to in order to survive. He always got \r\n    his ass kicked but always got away with the goods. Who am I talking about? Not \r\n    one, but two of the most gifted character actors of the early and later 20th \r\n    century: Peter Lorre and Steve Buscemi. Two of the greatest actors who ever \r\n    played crooked thieves. \r\n	<P>\r\nFor those of you who don\'t know Peter Lorre go to \r\n    your local grocery store cereal aisle and pick up a box of Boo-Berry\'s. \r\n    Remember the commercial? That throaty-voiced cartoon ghost is based on Peter \r\n    Lorre. Lorre played the side kick to Sydney Greenstreet in <i>The Maltese Falcon</i> (1941) and <i>Casablanca</i> (1942).\r\n	  In both films Lorre was the lacky who was \r\n    willing to turn on his boss for the right price.  Later in his career, Lorre \r\n    made a few comedies and did an episode of Alfred Hitchcock\'s weekly television \r\n    show.  But Lorre\'s bad guys of the 1940\'s film noir were the finest of his \r\n    career.\r\n	<P>\r\nThe 1990\'s brought film noir back.  Quentin Tarantino\'s film \r\n    <i>Reservoir Dogs</i> (1992) gave the world Mr. Pink, a cockroach-like diamond \r\n    thief who gets beat up by Harvey Keitel but lives through a shootout.  The \r\n    Coen Brothers made <i>Fargo</i> (1997) and cast Buscemi as an unlucky bank robber \r\n    who gets shot in the face and then goes through a wood chipper.  \r\n	<P>\r\nSo what place in the world of film do Lorre and Buscemi have? An important one.  The \r\n    good guy in a movie is only as good as the villain.  The villain is only as good as his \r\nhenchmen.  If Tom Hanks is the new Jimmy Stewart, then Steve Buscemi is the new encarnation of Peter Lorre.  Peter Lorre \r\nis the ancestor of Steve Buscemi.  If I had a working Way-Back Machine like Mr. Peabody and his boy \r\nSherman had and I was to recast <i>The Maltese Falcon</i> with Buscemi or bring Lorre to the 1990\'s and cast him for the role of\r\n Mr. Pink then the same thing would happen.  Wonderfully acted creepy characters.', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(31, 'Mulholland Drive', '', 3, '2001-10-30', 9.5, '<img src=\'images/mulholland.jpg\' width=\'148\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'Director David Lynch delivers another gem with <i>Mulholland Drive</i>.  This is arguably\r\nhis best work, effectively melding the saturated Americana color tones of <i>Blue Velvet</i>, the quirky humor\r\nof <i>Twin Peaks</i>, and the brooding soul-examination of <i>Lost Highway</i>.  <i>Mulholland Drive</i> is \r\nan examination of love and jealousy, and is at times a biting commentary on the world of Hollywood.\r\n<P>\r\nBe forewarned:  if you have no prior familiarity with the work of David Lynch, DO NOT see this movie.  Lynch is\r\nan acquired taste, and this is arguably his most sophisticated creation to date.  If you\'re interested in getting\r\nto know the world of David Lynch, you need to start small and work your way up.  Check out the <i>Twin Peaks</i> series and \r\n<i>Blue Velvet</i>, then when you\'ve got those mastered, you can graduate to <i>Lost Highway</i> and <i>Mulholland Drive</i>.\r\n<P>\r\nLynch is a master of, shall we say, \"unconventional\" storytelling.  Like <i>Lost Highway</i>, <i>Mulholland Drive</i> is not\r\na straight-forward Hollywood fable with clear winners and losers.  It is a puzzle, an intricately woven web of fantasy and \r\nreality.  It is a dream captured on film.  It is an abstract sculpture crafted of celluloid.  It requires intense examination\r\nof both the film and the viewer\'s own soul before its full meaning will even begin to become clear.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Mulholland Drive</i> continues this tradition perfectly.  Its opening is mysterious, yet soon brightens to match\r\nthe demeanor of the naive character Betty, played by Naomi Watts.  At first I found her character\'s cheery nature to\r\nbe irritating and poorly played, but as the film progressed and Watts displayed her true ability, I realized that the \r\nearlier happy-overkill was probably deliberate.  Laura Harring is excellent as Rita, the fearful amnesiac, and Justin\r\nTheroux brings well-placed humor to his role as the struggling filmmaker Adam.\r\n<P>\r\nLongtime fans will enjoy the contributions of Lynch regulars like composer Angelo Badalamenti and Michael J. Anderson,\r\nthe backwards-talking \"Man From Another Place\" from <i>Twin Peaks</i>.  Also watch for cameos by Billy Ray Cyrus and\r\nAussie hottie Melissa George.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Mulholland Drive</i> should probably be avoided by viewers who like cliched plots and tidy endings, but those who appreciate\r\nthe abstract art of David Lynch will be greatly pleased by this latest offering.', 'movie', 'Justin Theroux, Naomi Watts, Laura Harring', '', 2001, 1),
(32, 'From Hell', '', 3, '2001-11-05', 5.5, '<img src=\'images/from_hell.jpg\' width=\'146\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '<i>From Hell</i> stars Johnny Depp as Inspector Abberline, whose drug-induced visions give him mysterious insight\r\ninto brutal crimes.  His talents are called upon to solve a series of mysterious slayings at the hand of the man\r\nwho would be known as Jack the Ripper.  Heather Graham plays Mary Kelly, a street prostitute and potential\r\ntarget for the Ripper, with whom Abberline falls in love and must save from certain death. \r\n<P>\r\nI was pretty disappointed with this film.  It\'s one of those movies that fills time without really accomplishing\r\nanything.  It wasn\'t scary, it wasn\'t funny, it wasn\'t romantic or tragic, it didn\'t have any social commentary.\r\nIt was just <i>there</i>.  There is no palpable chemistry between Depp and Graham; although we can observe that he\r\nhas some love for her, the audience never really <i>feels</i> it.\r\n<P>\r\nAlso troubling is the often blatent disregard for historical truth.  True, the film never makes any claim to be\r\naccurate, but I\'m sure that many moviegoers will leave this film believing that the Ripper was in fact an instrument\r\nof a sinister conspiracy involving the monarchy and the Freemasons.  In reality, this idea is based on flimsy,\r\nunsubstantiated theories and \"evidence\" that was, in some cases, conjured out of thin air.  Those who have seen the\r\nfilm may be interested in <a href=\'http://www.casebook.org/dissertations/dst-fromhellfact.html\' \r\ntarget=\'_blank\'><b>this evaluation</b></a> from <i>Casebook: Jack the Ripper</i>.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>From Hell</i> is a pretty unremarkable film filled with historical inaccuracies, and containing very little to \r\nsustain one\'s interest to the end.  Rather than being an fascinating look into the mind of a serial killer, or at the\r\nvery least, some romantic thriller, it is neither; just another cop-out conspiracy theory with little suspense.\r\n<i>From Hell</i> only accomplishes two things.  First, it will teach you to beware strangers offering grapes.  And\r\nsecond, it will give you a hearty laugh at its ridiculous use of the eulogy, \"Good night, sweet prince.\"  Don\'t rush out\r\nto see this one anytime soon.  <i>From Hell</i>\'s mediocrity will leave you suspecting it actually came From Purgatory.', 'movie', 'Johnny Depp, Heather Graham', '', 2001, 1),
(33, 'Jack Black', 'An Actor Who Has a Place in the World of Film, but I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2001-11-09', 0, '<img src=\'images/black_jack_small.jpg\' width=\'97\' height=\'150\' alt=\'\' />', 'images/black_jack.jpg', '   There was this unwritten rule that the only comedians to make movies \r\nwere to have been on network television.  Aykroyd, Murray, Chase then came \r\nSandler, Schneider, Carey, and Myers.  Now there seems to be an odd man \r\nbreaking the trend who goes by the name of Jack Black.\r\n<P>\r\n    At first I thought Jack Black the actor was the guy from the Pixies, but \r\nhe wasn\'t.  Then I thought he might be the long lost grandson of British \r\nactress Karren Black, no I was wrong again.  Just who was this Jack Black?  I \r\ndid myself a bit of detective work.\r\n<P>\r\n     Jack Black is the singer from Tenacious D, a comedy musical act who had \r\ntheir own HBO televison show and who appeared in a Foo Fighters video.  They \r\ncurrently have an album out featuring a hit single,\r\n\"Wonderboy\".  Jack Black is not just a singer, he\'s an actor too, thus \r\nfitting perfectly into my catchy title.\r\n<P>\r\n    Black has appeared in such films as <i>Cable Guy</i> (1996), <I>Enemy of the \r\nState (1998)</i> and <i>Mars Attacks</i> (1996).  In these films he played small \r\nbit parts without much dialog.  In 2000, Black appeared in <i>High Fidelity</i> as \r\nBarry the store clerk.  His sarcastic wit and singing of Marvin Gaye\'s \"Let\'s Get It On\" \r\nmade the movie.  The very next year Black appeared in \r\n<i>Saving Silverman</i> as JD McNugent, Jason Bigg\'s bumbling former Subway \r\nemployee.  Now Black has the lead role in the Farrelly Brothers\' new film \r\n<i>Shallow Hal</i>.\r\n<P>\r\n   So where does Jack Black fit in the Hollywood scheme?  This guy will \r\nmake films that will be considered cult favorites.  I see him as the next \r\nSteve Martin of the early to mid 80\'s.  Black plays roles that fit him and \r\ngive him a chance to make the role funny and not vice versa.  I see big \r\nthings for Black.\r\n', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(34, 'Hulk Hogan', 'An Actor With A Place in the World of Film, But I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2001-11-28', 0, '<img src=\'images/hogan.jpg\' width=\'136\' height=\'90\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   I\'m a big fan of professional wrestling.  I\'m a big fan of film.  When \r\nyou cross film into the world of pro wrestling you get quality documentaries \r\nlike <i>Beyond the Mat</i> ( 1999 ) or <i>Brett Hart: Wrestling with Shadows</i> ( 1998 ). \r\n<P>\r\n  When you cross pro wrestling into the world of film you get such crap as \r\n<i>Ready to Rumble</i> ( 1999 ) starring David Arquett and a host of bumbling WCW \r\nwrestlers and <i>The Mummy Returns</i> ( 2001 ) starring Dwayne Johnson a.k.a The \r\nRock.  Who is to blame? None other than Hulk Hogan.  Brilliant as a pro \r\nwrestler but terribly retarded as an actor.\r\n<P>\r\n    Hulk Hogan began his film career innocently enough as little more than \r\nan extra in <i>Rocky III</i>, portraying a pro wrestler going against Rocky, a \r\nboxer, in a charity match.  Hogan than stretched his acting skills to \r\nportraying a pro wrestler who wrestled other pro wrestlers in matches where \r\nhe got paid in the major motion picture <i>No Holds Barred</i> ( 1989 ). <i>No Holds \r\nBarred</i> lasted at the box office no longer than a Ernest P. Worrell film \r\nfestival at the Apollo.  Most common people would chalk up this failure as a \r\nlearning experice and continue doing what they do best in life.  Not Hogan, \r\nhe would further violate the world with his offensive acting skills. Enter \r\n<i>Suburban Commando</i>.\r\n<P>\r\n    So here\'s the premise, an alien bounty hunter lands in middle class \r\nAmerica in search of an evil alien who wants to take over Earth. Sounds good \r\nso far?  You\'ve thought too quick, the bounty hunter, played by Hogan, \r\nbefriends the family and hilarity is allegedly to ensue, but it never does. \r\n<P>\r\n  Hogan basically repeats the same joke over and over again: he doesn\'t know \r\nhis own strength and something that\'s not suppose to get broken, gets \r\nbroken. Car doors,  front doors, neighborhood dog\'s legs it was all the same \r\njoke.  Afterwards Hogan always has the same dumb look on his face as if he \r\njust got kicked in the balls.  Yet again, a terrible, terrible film.  Normal \r\npeople would be discouraged, but then again, pro wrestlers aren\'t normal \r\npeople.\r\n<P>\r\nSince <i>Suburban Commando</i>, Hogan has defiled the world several times with \r\nsuch films as <i>Mr. Nanny</i> ( 1993 ), <i>3 Ninjas</i> ( 1995 ) and the worst film ever made\r\n(<a href=\"topfive_56.html\">according to me</a>), <i>Santa With Muscles</i> ( 1996 ).\r\n<P>\r\n        So where does Hulk Hogan belong in the world of film?  No where!  \r\nNot any place!  He shouldn\'t be in films as an actor, producer or director, \r\nhe shouldn\'t even be allowed to have a video rental membership for fear that \r\nhe might contaminate good films with his mere pressance.  Words can not \r\ndepict how terrible an actor Hogan is, only nightmares of children who have \r\nlived through a holocaust can.  As human beings it is our duty to buy faster cars so we can drive to \r\nchurch quicker to pray that God will never let Hulk Hogan make another movie \r\nagain.\r\n\r\n', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(35, 'Harry Potter and the Sorcerer\'s Stone', '', 2, '2001-12-11', 2, '<img src=\'images/potter.jpg\' width=\'148\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '     For the love of all that is good and holy, please let the magic end in a firey \r\ncrash of brimstone and hellfire.  The only reason I saw this film was to \r\nmake fun of it.  I\'ve never read a Harry Potter book, I didn\'t buy any of \r\nthe toys and I didn\'t anxiously wait for this crappy film to come out.\r\n<P>\r\n      Here\'s the pathetic plot.  Harry James Potter, played by\r\n\"the lucky young boy who auditioned out of the thousands\", Daniel Radcliffe,\r\nis an orphaned boy who lives with his aunt, uncle, and dickweed of a cousin.  \r\nHe gets a mysterious letter inviting him to go to the prestigious yet \r\nmysterious Hogwarts School of Witchcraft and Wizardry.  Soon after, I myself \r\nbegan wondering if such a school existed.  If so, I would like to learn how \r\nto cast a spell on Radcliffe to magically teleport him to a jail cell with \r\nthree sex starved inmates in South Carolina.  I then began feeling rather \r\nhappy and content.  Then I looked up at the movie screen again and realized \r\nthe film hadn\'t ended.  More crap.  Harry Potter meets two other misfits in \r\nretard school, Ron Weasley, played by Rupert Grint and Hermione Granger, \r\nplayed by Emma Watson.  They stumble upon an evil plot by their teacher, \r\nSeverus Snape, played by Alan Rickman, to steal something guarded by a three \r\nheaded giant dog named \"Fluffy\".\r\n<P>\r\n    Stop right there.  Where the hell did they come up with these names? \r\nSeverus Snape? Hermione Granger? The damn stupid computer dog doesn\'t even \r\nhave a cool name.  They couldn\'t have made at least something good?\r\n<P>\r\n    From there the movie drags on and on.  Kid lacks self esteem, does \r\nsomething no one expected him to do, yada yada yada...enter the ending plot \r\ntwist to set up for the sequel.\r\n<P>\r\n    This mockery of a film was directed by Chris Columbus, who gave the \r\nworld such cinematic headaches and <i>Mrs. Doubtfire</i> and <i>Home Alone</i>. As far as \r\nChris Columbus\'s direction of this film goes, I hope that his movie \r\ndirector\'s license gets revoked and he is forced to take a job as a guy who \r\nhas to clean up vomit on school buses.\r\n<P>\r\n    However, for the amount of negativity I\'m showing towards this film, \r\nthere were high points.  The special effects were entertaining.  It amazes \r\nme how much computer-designed special effects work into films today and how \r\ngood it looks compared to five years ago.  Secondly, nowhere was there any \r\nsign of Hulk Hogan (see <a href=\'other_34.html\'>my last article on the Hulkster</a>).  Finally, the \r\nfilm\'s running time was short enough that I was able to make it to the bar \r\nand begin drinking in hopes of forgetting what a terrible, terrible film \r\nthis was.\r\n', 'chick', 'Daniel Radcliffe, John Hurt, Alan Rickman', '', 2001, 1),
(36, 'Vanilla Sky', '', 3, '2001-12-18', 7, '<img src=\'images/vanillasky.jpg\' width=\'149\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'Director Cameron Crowe brings us <i>Vanilla Sky</i>, a fairly successful retelling of the 1997 Spanish film, <i>Abre los Ojos</i>.  Publishing heir and playboy extraordinaire David Aames (Cruise) decides to cast aside his friend and lover Julie Gianni (Diaz) in favor of a new and irresistably attractive dancer named Sofia (Cruz, in a role she also held in the original film).  Julie turns out to be a psycho hose beast and begins to stalk David, turning to desperate measures in an attempt to ensure the two will be together forever.  In the aftermath of the incident, David struggles to return his life to normalcy, and soon descends into a state of mental breakdown.\r\n<P>\r\nYou may have heard that this movie twists dramatically towards the end, and indeed it does.  So much so, in fact, that the film\'s trailer really only describes the first 45 minutes to an hour of the movie, from which it begins to veer towards an entirely different conclusion.  The ending is one which you either accept or you don\'t; there is little room for fence-sitting.  Fellow TFM contributors Ryan and Brian hated it.  I, on the other hand, found the conclusion interesting, though perhaps a bit \"tidy\" and in some ways reminiscient of a certain Arnold Schwarzenegger flick.  It is nevertheless unconventional, most likely due in large part to the foreign origins of the story.  American viewers are often confused or dismayed by the storytelling conventions of other cultures, but Crowe does a good job of preserving the evidence of the film\'s foreign origins, while translating the details to an Americanized version.\r\n<P>\r\nRegardless of one\'s acceptance of the story\'s plausibility, it is difficult to dismiss the all-around superb performances of the cast.  Tom Cruise is excellent as the charming pretty boy and tortured soul, and Cameron Diaz brings just the right amount of sexiness and psychotic intensity to her role as the jilted lover.  But the star of the show is Penelope Cruz, who is absolutely breathtaking as Sofia.  She effectively combines wit, cuteness, and exotic flair in a way that is simply irresistable.  As a matter of fact, I think I\'m in love with her.  I have no doubt that she will readily leave that flake Tom, and come join me for a lifetime of gettin\' it on.  Don\'t worry, you\'re all invited to the wedding.\r\n<P>\r\nTwo more highlights of the movie are the surprising shots of New York city completely empty and silent, as well as Cameron Crowe\'s trademark use of superb musical accompaniment.  At the end of the day, any assessment of this movie will be dominated by one\'s opinion of the film\'s conclusion.  While not as thought-provoking or utterly brilliant as noir pieces like <i>Mulholland Drive</i>, <i>Vanilla Sky</i> is nevertheless an entertaining journey, a great character study, and a showpiece of brilliant acting.', 'movie', 'Tom Cruise, Cameron Diaz, Penelope Cruz, Jason Lee, Kurt Russell', '', 2001, 1),
(37, 'Lord of the Rings: The Fellowship of the Ring', '', 2, '2002-01-02', 7.5, '<img src=\'images/lord_of_rings.jpg\' width=\'172\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    High above the mucky-muck in the longy-long ago the Great Writer of \r\nDorky Fantasy books wrote the Magic Ring Book.  The Great Writer spawned a \r\nclan called the Weirdos-Who-Refuse-to-Deal-with-the-Real \r\n-World-and-Wear-Leather-Trenchcoats who worshiped the Great Writer and his \r\nMagic Ring Book.  The Weirdos-Who-Refuse-to-Deal-with-the-Real \r\n-World-and-Wear-Leather-Trenchcoats formed an allegiance with Lord Khan the \r\nKapitalist to join more people into their clan.  The people that \r\nWeirdos-Who-Refuse-to-Deal-with-the-Real \r\n-World-and-Wear-Leather-Trenchcoats wanted to join their clan were the \r\nMainstreamers, a race of people who did not cut their hair in the shape of \r\na mullet and had women who weighed less than 300 pounds.  \r\nWeirdos-Who-Refuse-to-Deal-with-the-Real \r\n-World-and-Wear-Leather-Trenchcoats wanted to be like the Mainstreamers.  \r\nLord Khan wanted the gold from The Mainstreamers.  But the Mainstreamers shun \r\nthe Weirdo\'s-Who-Refuse-to-Deal-with-the-Real \r\n-World-and-Wear-Leather-Trenchcoats, for their ways were odd and pooftery. \r\n<P>\r\nLord Khan devised a diabolical plan with the Slime People of Hollywood, \r\nprobably led by the Crap Monster Hulk Hogan,  to turn the Lord of the Rings \r\ngame into not one, but three major motion pictures to draw the Mainstreamers \r\ninto his green shadow and take their gold.  The Mainstreamers fought off \r\nLord Khan, but they were deceived by the good King of Burgers who began \r\ngiving away Great Glass Collector Items.  The Mainstreamers were sucked into it.  \r\nThey went to Lord Khan\'s movie and sat for three hours watching Unholy \r\nBoredom.   Just when the Mainstreamers believed they had defeated Lord Khan, \r\nthey were deceived again.  Lord Khan had under his power the strength of the \r\nScribes who placed a clever plot twist to trick the Mainstreamers into \r\nseeing the sequel.\r\n<P>\r\n        Yup, that pretty much sums it up.  <i>Lord of the Rings: Fellowship \r\nof the Ring</i> was the first of three crappy three-hour movies brought to us by \r\nCapitalists and Hollywood bastards, led by none other than Hulk Hogan.  \r\nThe Hulkster did not appear in the film and was not involved in the \r\nproduction of it, but he was behind it... somehow.\r\n<P>\r\n      There were some good points to the film, I guess.  The special effects \r\nwere great.  The monsters were very scary and the cities were nothing short \r\nof bitchin\'.  The acting wasn\'t too bad, Elijah Wood was very good as the main \r\ncharacter of Frodo.  Other people in this film who turned it out were Liv \r\nTyler, who played an elf, Ian McKeller, who played a magician, and Sean \r\nAstin, who played a dyslexic yet spunky Hobbit who wanted to play football \r\nfor Notre Dame.\r\n<P>\r\n	The thing that I really didn\'t like about the film was the fact that it was \r\nthree hours long.  The kid has a ring, he\'s being chased by bad guys, he\'s \r\nabout ready to get killed and someone comes out of nowhere and saves him and \r\nthey run from the bad guys again. Over and over and over again.  Same bad \r\nguys, same stupid kid, different people saving his monkey-ass and then running \r\nfrom the bad guys.       \r\n<P>\r\nThe other thing I didn\'t like about the film was \r\nthe laborious use of obscure phonetic sounds put together to create names of \r\npeople, places, and things.  Even this is just a personal preference.  I never \r\nread any of J.R.R. Tolkien\'s books nor am I really even a fan of the genre of \r\nfantasy games.  My sister, who saw the film with me and is a fan of the \r\nsubject matter, was not phased by the gratuitous use of the dialog being \r\nspoken by Otter Pub of Mead Ville who spoke to Mork of Ork about the sacred \r\nSpray Bottle of Febreze that could magically lift the Stains of Darkness from \r\nHeavyweight Fabrics, Carpets, and Upholstery.\r\n<P>\r\n     How do I rate this film?  It\'s 9.5 out of 10 if you like this genre. It \r\nprobably would have been a 10 out of 10 if David Bowie and some Muppets were \r\nin it.  If you\'re a Mainstreamer like me, I give it a 7.5 out of 10.  It\'s \r\nnot a bad film, but it\'s too friggin\' long!', 'movie', 'Elijah Wood, Ian McKeller, Liv Tyler', '', 2001, 1),
(38, 'Reservoir Dogs', '', 2, '2002-01-01', 3, '<img src=\'images/reservoir_dogs.jpg\' width=\'153\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', '', '     Santa Claus left a DVD player beneath the Christmas tree at my house \r\nthis year. I had no DVDs so I went out to National Record Mart here in \r\nMeadville, which sad to say has gone out of business, and bought some DVDs. \r\nNow comes the question, if you were to rebuild your film collection from \r\nscratch, what would be the first on your list?  <i>Reservoir Dogs</i> was the first \r\none on my list.\r\n<P>\r\n    If you haven\'t seen Quentin Tarantino\'s first masterpiece, then shame on \r\nyou.  This is arguably the greatest new American gangster movie ever. \r\n<i>Reservoir Dogs</i> begins with a simple plan, six guys busting in and busting \r\nout of a diamond wholesaler in broad daylight.  Things go awry when the heist \r\nis botched and the cops show up.  A brilliant film in every way, the dialog, \r\ncinematography and the cast.\r\n<P>\r\n   As far as the DVD\'s special features go, there really isn\'t anything \r\nexciting.  Most DVDs feature outtakes or special commentary by the \r\ndirector.  <i>Reservoir Dogs</i> features the widescreen and standard view and \r\nFrench or Spanish subtitles.  Nothing all that exciting, but it was cool \r\nreading the \"Like a Virgin\" scene in Spanish.\r\n<P>\r\n    All in all, if you\'re like me and you\'re building your DVD collection, \r\nthis is a must.  If you\'re not like me, go buy this one anyways.  The movie itself is a 9.5 rating, but the special features on the DVD only rate a 3.', 'dvd', 'Harvey Keitel, Tim Roth, Steve Buscemi', '', 1992, 1),
(39, 'Joe Dirt', '', 2, '2002-01-01', 2, '<img src=\'images/joedirt.jpg\' width=\'146\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    I bought this DVD for my mother for Christmas.  She nearly disowned me.  \r\n<i>Joe Dirt</i> is one of the worst films I have ever seen.  This is \"Hulk Hogan\" \r\nbad.  <i>Joe Dirt</i> is how the United States would have been if the South \r\nhad won the Civil War: Guys in mullets driving crappy cars and \r\nlistening to Molly Hatchet.\r\n<P>\r\n    David Spade plays Joe Dirt, a luckless and witless janitor in search of \r\nhis parents whom he lost at the Grand Canyon when he was 8. Dirt gets in \r\nmisadventures throughout the rural South trying to find his parents, \r\nhilarity supposedly ensues, but never does.  Instead of something funny, the \r\nstory jumps around; Dirt finds an asteroid which turns out to be a frozen \r\nlump of shit, Dirt meets up with a Native American fireworks salesmen, Dirt \r\nhas sex with someone whom he believes is his sister.  There\'s also a scene \r\nthat deals with Buffalo Bob from <i>Silence of the Lambs</i>, which only fizzles and \r\nnever comes though as being funny.\r\n<P>\r\n     The cast was an all-star lineup that would have been great in any \r\nother film together.  Christopher Walken, who is creepy, yet did a good job \r\nplaying a janitor, Brittany Daniel, who is hot and did an excellent job of \r\nplaying a hot chick and even Kid Rock, who is white trash, did an amazing \r\njob playing a dude who is white trash.  If Spade would have played his old, \r\nsarcastic character, instead of someone named Joe Dirt, the name says it \r\nall, the film would have been great.\r\n<P>\r\n     The special features on the DVD include a direct link to the website \r\n(http://www.this_movie_sucks_my_white_ass.com) and outtakes and bloopers, \r\nwhich aren\'t that different from the actual film.\r\n<P>\r\n      This is by far, one of the worst films I have seen on DVD.  The only \r\nhope is that Spade becomes heavily involved in the art of voodoo and \r\nresurrects the spirt of Chris Farley to make more movies with him.', 'dvd', 'David Spade, Dennis Miller and Brittany Daniel', '', 2001, 1),
(40, 'Pi', '', 3, '2002-01-01', 9, '<img src=\'images/pi.jpg\' width=\'145\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'With the recent theatrical release of <i>A Beautiful Mind</i>, I thought it would be a good idea to go\r\nback and take a look at another tale of a mathematical genius tortured by his own mind and obsessions.\r\nSpecifically, I sought out <i>Pi</i>, the independent film by writer/director Darren Aronofsky, and\r\nwas not the least bit disappointed.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Pi</i> is the story of Max Cohen and his quest to find mathematical patterns throughout the world,\r\nand especially in the stock market.  He is convinced there is order behind the chaos, and his life is\r\nutterly consumed by this search.  Cohen isolates himself in his apartment for days at a time, accompanied\r\nonly by his mainframe computer \"Euclid\", the insects that infest the building, and massive mind-bending\r\nheadaches.  He emerges only to seek the wisdom of his former teacher Sol, or to read the paper at a nearby\r\ncoffee shop and confirm the predictions made by his computer, all the while taking great pains to avoid\r\nany other human contact.  Eventually, however, a Jewish mystic and a representive of a number-theory\r\nthinktank force themselves into Cohen\'s life and provide the final clues he needs to unravel the secrets\r\nof the universe.\r\n<P>\r\nNow, I haven\'t yet seen <i>A Beautiful Mind</i> so I don\'t want to unfairly prejudge it, but I have a\r\nfeeling that it couldn\'t possibly be as edgy and mindblowingly thought-provoking as <i>Pi</i>.  This is\r\na tremendous film.  First, the cinematography is excellent.  The movie is filmed in black and white, with\r\nsuperb editing and sound that really immerse the viewer in Cohen\'s tortured mind.  We really feel his\r\nparanoia and isolation, and share in his sense that enlightenment is just around the corner.\r\nWhat really made me love this movie, though, actually took several hours to sink in.  The way that\r\nAronofsky weaves a connection between mathematics, God, economics, the Hebrew language, and the Japanese game \"Go\"\r\nis simply amazing.  Without giving away any important plot details, I can\'t even begin to discuss the \r\nnumerous ways that I\'ve been thinking about this movie for the last 12 hours.  Suffice it to say, one\r\ncan take the ideas and themes presented and run with them in any number of philosophically intriguing\r\ndirections.\r\n<P>\r\nIf you\'re looking for a creative movie that you can become completely immersed in, and come away with\r\nenough ideas to leave you thinking for days, I highly recommend ordering up a serving of <i>Pi</i>.', 'movie', 'Sean Gullette, Mark Margolis, Ben Shenkman', '', 1998, 1),
(41, 'Cameron Diaz', 'An Actress with a Place in the World of Film, But I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2002-02-09', 0, '<img src=\'images/mary.jpg\' width=\'163\' height=\'207\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    Cameron Diaz seems to have a knack for playing roles of chicks that \r\nwould talk to guys who write for topfivemovies.com.  Seriously, if I was in a \r\nbar and I saw Cameron Diaz, I would probably go up to her and say, \"Hello, \r\nhow are you, can I buy you a drink?\"  Then I\'m sure her bodyguard would \r\nthrow me outside or her middle-aged pony-tailed fruit producer boyfriend \r\nwould mace me, but I\'d still talk to her.  She seems cool, I don\'t think I\'d \r\nbe embarasssed to fart in front of her.\r\n<P>\r\n    Cameron Diaz is a good actress too.  Everyone knows her from <i>There\'s \r\nSomething About Mary</i> ( 1998 ) or by playing the voice of the princess in \r\n<i>Shrek</i> ( 2000 ).  Diaz has made better films however.  She played a showgirl \r\nin <i>The Mask</i> ( 1994 ), an abused girlfriend in <i>Feeling Minnesota</i> ( 1996 )and \r\nshe was caught in a love triangle in <i>She\'s the One</i> ( 1996 ).  By the way, \r\n<i>She\'s the One</i> was an excellent soundtrack, too bad <a href=\"http://petty.cjb.net/\" target=\"_blank\">Tom Petty is dead</a>.\r\n<P>\r\n    The thing that appeals to the common man about Cameron Diaz is that \r\nshe\'s not the sterotypical Hollywood blonde.  She\'s doesn\'t have the dumb \r\nblonde label that Marilyn Monroe had or being helpless like Mimi Van Dorn \r\nwas.  Diaz lost that image whenever she began kicking ass in <i>Charlie\'s \r\nAngels</i> ( 2000 ).  Do you really think Myrna Loy would have shot Alexander \r\nSess in <i>Buckets of Blood</i> ( 1948 ) ?  Jesse might think so.\r\n<P>\r\n    So where is Diaz\'s place in the world of film?  I don\'t see her \r\nreplaying the role Ingrid Bergman played in Casablanca, unless Sam tells \r\nBogey that if he doesn\'t clean his pipes before seeing her that he\'s walking \r\naround with a loaded gun.  I see Diaz continuing to make the romantic comedy \r\nthat American women drag their boyfriends to.  While women weep as Richard \r\nGere is in a hospital bed about ready to go through a dangerous, yet life \r\nsaving, rectal gerbil removal operation, Cameron Diaz can play his devoted \r\ngirlfriend. Everyone would be happy then.\r\n<P>\r\n    Diaz, on a serious note, should also stick with doing to occasional \r\nindependent film.  She is a great actress who can stretch herself and play \r\nsome very dramatic characters, <i>Being John Malkovich</i> ( 1999 ) is an excellent \r\nexample, where she played a veterinarian turned lesbian.  In conclusion, \r\nDiaz, a great actress who plays roles that guys and chicks can get into.', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(42, 'Clay Pigeons', '', 2, '2002-02-02', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/clay.jpg\' width=\'100\' height=\'140\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    I saw this movie at the cheap theatres when I was in college.  I really \r\nliked it.  I then saw it a few months ago on USA, network TV does not do \r\nthis film justice.  So I got it on DVD and I was happy again.\r\n<P>\r\n    Here\'s the story.  Joaquin Phoenix plays the main character, Clay who is \r\nhaving an affair with his best friend\'s wife Amanda, played by Georgia Cates. \r\n  The best friend goes nuts, kills himself and makes it look like it was \r\nClay\'s fault.  Clay asks Amanda for help and she tells him no, just like a \r\nwoman would.  Clay manages to clear himself from any wrongdoing and then \r\nAmanda wants to hook up with him again, just like a woman would.  Clay kicks \r\nher to the curb and in the process meets Lester Long, played by Vince \r\nVaughn.  Lester and Clay become fishing buddies and keep finding dead \r\nbodies.  To investigate the newly found dead bodies, in comes none other than Dale \r\nShelby, played by Janeane Garofalo, who is on the search for a serial killer \r\nwho might be Clay or Lester.  Hilarity ensues.\r\n<P>\r\n    The actors in this film are great.  Phoenix plays the caught in the \r\nmiddle character of Clay perfectly.  Garofalo\'s scarcasm comes through \r\nwonderfully as an FBI agent belittling the pratices of the small town police \r\nforce.  Above all is Vince Vaughn. I\'m a big Vaughn fan, <i>Swingers</i> has \r\nalways been one of my favorite films and I tolerated the new version of \r\n<i>Psycho</i> only because of him.  Vaughn\'s character of Lester Long is \r\noutstanding.  Vaughn is scary and yet very charming.  An odd combination, \r\nbut very truthful.\r\n<P>\r\n     The soundtrack also kicks ass.  Odd little songs of the southwest, the \r\nfilms setting, blend in the tranquilty of death.  Plus the Refreshments are \r\non it, who RULE!\r\n<P>\r\n      As far as the DVD itself goes, it\'s okay.  It has the standard \r\ninteractive features such as trailers, cast bio\'s and wide screen versions.  \r\nI wish it would have had outtakes or bloopers or something.  Oh well.\r\n<P>\r\n      In conclusion, rent or buy this DVD.  It\'s a funny Hitchcock-esque film \r\nthat will keep you entertained and leave you quoting Garofalo\'s line, \"Barney, please don\'t poke the dead body with a stick.\"', 'movie', 'Vince Vaughn, Janeane Garofalo, and Joaquin Phoenix', '', 1999, 1);
INSERT INTO `Article` (`ID_`, `Title`, `Subtitle`, `Author_ID_FK`, `Article_date`, `Rating`, `Image`, `Image_fullsize`, `Text`, `Type`, `Starring`, `Caption`, `Release_year`, `QA`) VALUES
(43, 'Snow Dogs', '', 2, '2002-02-16', 6, '<img src=\'images/snow_dogs.jpg\' width=\'164\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   I\'m not going to entirely tear up this film like I do to most I review.  \r\n<i>Snow Dogs</i> is an entertaining family film.  If you want to take your kids to \r\na silly family film or you want to see some breathtaking shots of the \r\nVancover wilderness, go see this movie.\r\n<P>\r\n  But if you want to see Cuba Gooding Jr. in an Oscar winning performace, \r\nthen don\'t go see <i>Snow Dogs</i>.  I know what <i>Snow Dogs</i> is, it\'s a light-hearted \r\nfamily film.  Gooding did a great job at this, he kept it fun and I am glad \r\nto see that he is willing to act in a variety of roles.  But for Christmas \r\nSake, how many times do we need to see him slip on the ice and fall on his \r\nass?  Once it was hilarious, twice it was funny, but after the tenth time \r\nof Gooding slipping on the ice, I kind of wish he would have cracked his \r\nhead open and the film would end.\r\n<P>\r\n    Here\'s the plot, Gooding plays Ted, a dentist in Miami who finds out \r\nthat he was adopted and his recently deceased biological mother was a dog \r\nsled racer in Alaska.  Gooding travels to Alaska for the reading of the \r\nwill.  There he meets the gruff Thunder Jack ( James Coburn ), the \r\nultra-hottie Barb the bartender ( Joanna Bacalso ) and his mother\'s rag-tag \r\nmisfit sled dog team.  Hilarity ensues.\r\n<P>\r\n    Notable performances include Nichelle Nichols, Star Treks Lt. Uhura, \r\nwho plays Gooding\'s adoption mother; Bryan Doyle-Murray, who plays the town \r\ndrunk, and Sisqo, Goodings dental partner.  I\'m not a fan of the Thong Song \r\nMan, but he did do a good job playing a poofter.      The dogs in this film \r\nwere the canine version of the Seven Dwarfs.  There was the leader, the dumb \r\none, the one who liked to eat, the one who liked to sleep and so on and so \r\nforth, fairly formulaic. They don\'t like Gooding in the begining of the \r\nfilm, the leader bites Gooding in the ass about nine times, Gooding earns \r\ntheir trust then  they do heroic shit that no one expected them to do to \r\nsave Gooding.  Pretty simple.\r\n<P>\r\n      If I could have directed this film and improved it, I would have \r\nchanged the whole PG rating and done some crazy Cujo shit.  One dog was bad \r\nass, but imagine seven.  I would have totally cut out the whole Gooding \r\nadoption part and focused on the dogs flipping out and terrorizing the town. \r\n  Sisqo and Gooding are still dentists, but get turned into FBI \r\ndentists/rabid dog hunters and have to fight these dogs before they take \r\nover all of Alaska.  First scene, the dogs attack Sisqo and the lead dog \r\nmakes him his bitch.  Maybe some aliens, but definitely big rocket-powered \r\ndog sleds.  I also would have made the dogs have British accents and eye \r\npatches.  The final scene would be on top of this snow covered mountain with \r\nGooding fighting the lead dog and throwing him down a deep crevass and \r\nsaying, \"When you get to doggy hell, tell \'em The Dentist sent you.\"  Just \r\nan idea.', 'chick', '  Cuba Gooding Jr., James Coburn, and a bunch of dogs', '', 2002, 1),
(45, 'Ghost World', '', 2, '2002-03-07', 6, '<img src=\'images/ghost_world.jpg\' width=\'153\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    This movie was mildly entertaining, so accordingly I gave it 6 stars or \r\npears or whatever the hell Greg uses to rate movies.  Recently I\'ve been \r\ntaking a lot of flack from the knuckleheads at IMRA (Internet Movie Raters \r\nAssociation) on how I rate films and DVD\'s.  So to please the masses and \r\nnot get blacklisted from yet another organization (I\'m still bitter about \r\nthe letter I got from the American Communist Association.  Who gets blacklisted \r\nby other Commies?) I\'ve decided to show step-by-step how I came to \r\nthe exact number of points <i>Ghost World</i> gets.\r\n<P>\r\n     I took 2 points away from <i>Ghost World</i> because of its title after I saw \r\nthe film.  There are no ghosts in <i>Ghost World</i>.  <i>Ghostbusters</i> had both the \r\nghosts and the busters.  The movie <i>Ghost</i> had Patrick Swayze playing the \r\nrole of a ghost.  <i>Space Ghost</i> is a ghost from space.  <i>Ghost World</i>, no ghost.\r\n<P>\r\n      The premise is confusing at best, so I took 4 points.  The main \r\ncharacter Enid and her best friend Rebecca graduate high school and want to \r\nget an apartment.  Along the way they are apathetic to everyone in their \r\ntown and get mixed up with a lonely record collector named Seymore.\r\n<P>\r\n     What was best was the dynamite actors and actresses.  It starred Thora \r\nBirch (American Beauty) who plays the ultra moody, Buddy-Holly-glasses-wearing\r\nEnid.  Enid\'s best friend is Rebecca, played by the ultra hottie \r\nScarlett Johansson (Home Alone 3, like anybody saw that one).  They \r\nboth interact with a dude named Seymore, played by the Peter Lorre of our \r\ngeneration, the ultra creepy Steve Buscemi.  I gave 8 points for the use \r\nof Johansson and Buscemi, but took 3 for the fact that the chicks didn\'t \r\nget naked and tag team on Buscemi at some point in the film.  I gotta look \r\nout for my boys.\r\n<P>\r\n      This film was originally based on the comic book <i>Ghost World</i> by Daniel \r\nClowes, who also wrote the screenplay.  I gave 2 points for Clowes putting \r\ndown the tracing pen and writing the screenplay, but took 1 point because \r\nthe comic book didn\'t involve any archcriminal trying to poison the city\'s \r\nwater supply.\r\n<P>\r\n     The DVD scored this film some bonus points.  It had some behind the \r\nscenes footage and an alternate ending.  I like it when DVD\'s go the extra \r\nmile, 3 points.\r\n<P>\r\n      To sum it all up, that\'s 8 + 2 + 3 - 1 - 4 - 2 = 6.\r\n', 'movie', 'Thora Birch, Scarlett Johansson and Steve Buscemi', '', 2001, 1),
(46, 'Jimmy Neutron: Boy Genius', 'A dorky cartoon kid inventor, a robot dog, and a kid with asthma', 2, '2002-03-28', 2, '<img src=\'images/jimmy_neutron.jpg\' width=\'145\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   Animated films can be really great, Walt Disney\'s <i>Fantasia</i> showed the \r\nworld what only had been seen by hippies on LSD.  Nickelodeon, back in the \r\nday, was a great network for children\'s programing.  Who can forget seeing \r\nAlanis Morrisette getting slimed on \"You Can\'t Do That On Television\" or \r\nseeing reruns of SCTV.\r\n<P>\r\n    Nowadays Nickelodeon has become yet another television tyrant, much like \r\nMTV, sodomizing America with boardroom-produced pop culture.  How many \r\ntimes have we seen a flash-in-the-pan pop singer in front of 20 adolescent \r\nkids just to pimp their crappy cd or sweatshop produced merchandise?\r\n<P>\r\n    The most recent atrocity to the world of animation is <i>Jimmy Neutron: \r\nBoy Genius</i>.  The writers took a good long 15 minutes to write this one.  \r\nJimmy Neutron is a child inventor who wishes his parents weren\'t around to \r\nmake him follow rules.  Jimmy\'s wish comes true when a fleet of aliens \r\nkidnap all of the neighborhood parents and take them to their home world to \r\nbe sacrificed by a giant chicken.  Jimmy, his robot dog and a ragtag group \r\nof lovable misfits defy the odds to rescue their parents with inventions \r\nthat Jimmy creates.\r\n<P>\r\n    The film was done with computer animation, which was cool for the first \r\n6 minutes, then got boring.  There were only two celebrity voices, Patrick \r\nStewart was the voice of the not-so-scary despot King Goobot, and Martin \r\nShort played his brother, Oobler.\r\n<P>\r\n    As far as the soundtrack goes, it could have been the only thing the \r\nmovie had going for it if it was used correctly.  Tracks by the Ramones, The \r\nGo-Go\'s and The Backstreet Boys played throughout the film at inopportune \r\nmoments.  It\'s a good thing Johnny Ramone is dead, because he would have \r\ngone into an epileptic seizure if he saw these fruity cartoon characters eat \r\ncotton candy and pee in the shower to \"Hey Oh Let\'s Go\".\r\n<P>\r\n     The reason this film got one star is for the animation.  This film \r\ncouldn\'t have taken a long time to make, therefore the Gorillaz animated \r\nfilm can\'t be that far away.  Hopefully there will be a scene where Murdoc \r\nruns Jimmy Neutron over in his Geep.  A man can dream, can\'t he?\r\n', 'chick', 'Patrick Stewart, Martin Short', '', 2001, 1),
(47, 'Suicide Kings', '', 2, '2002-04-02', 9, '<img src=\'images/suicidekings.jpg\' width=\'97\' height=\'140\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   <i>Suicide Kings</i> was very similar to <i>Things to Do in Denver When You\'re \r\nDead</i> ( 1997 ).  Not only were both movies made in 1997 and starred \r\nChristopher Walken; both films were dark comedies in the genre of film noir \r\nand neither one got very much press when released. Both films also had a \r\nlarge cast of notable actors who played small parts but played them very \r\nwell.  Christopher Llyod and Steve Buscemi were both in <i>Things to Do in \r\nDenver When You\'re Dead</i> while Dennis Leary and Johnny Galecki were both in \r\n<i>Suicide Kings</i>.  I think it was the small role actors that made both films so \r\ngood.  Both Leary and Lloyd added a dark humor to both films which offset \r\nthe bleak world of organized crime.\r\n<P>\r\n     Suicide Kings is about Avery, played by Henry Thomas, whose sister gets \r\nkidnapped by mobsters and ransomed for two milion dollars.  In order to get \r\nher back Avery and his friends played by Jay Mohr, Sean Patrick Flannery and \r\nJeremy Sisto, kidnap a notorious crime boss played by Christopher Walken to \r\nget her back.  The plan seems simple, but goes awry when the possibility of \r\nsomeone setting up the group is detected by Walken.  Can the group trust \r\nWalken or can they trust only themselves?\r\n<P>\r\n     The film is great.  Walken is the second creepiest guy in Hollywood and \r\ndoes a great job in this film.  Leary is also funny, especially when he \r\ntakes the bucket from the homeless guy. The way the story is told keeps the \r\naudience guessing throughout the film.\r\n<P>\r\n    As far as the DVD goes, I was equally empressed.  First of all it was \r\nonly $7.99 at Wal*Mart.  Second of all it included alternate endings and \r\ncommentary by the director.  It was a very good DVD all around.\r\n', 'dvd', 'Christopher Walken, Sean Patrick Flannery and Jay Mohr', '', 1997, 1),
(48, 'Heartbreakers', '', 2, '2002-04-02', 7, '<img src=\'images/heartbreakers.jpg\' width=\'133\' height=\'200\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   <i>Heartbreakers</i> is about Max Conners (Sigourney Weaver) and her daughter \r\nPage (Jennifer Love Hewitt), who con rich men out money by marrying them.  \r\nThis is not a new premise, this happens to millions of men each day, women \r\nconning them for drinks, dinner and bus fare.  Anyways, Weaver and Hewitt are \r\nlooking to score phat money in Palm Springs and rook cigarette mogul William \r\nB. Tensy (Gene Hackman).  Along the way Hewitt meets a ruggedly handsome bar \r\nowner played by Jason Lee, who she begins to con but then falls in love with.\r\n<P>\r\n    This film reminds me of <i>Dirty Rotten Scoundrels</i> ( 1988 ), but I think \r\nHewitt has a much better ass then Steve Martin.  The unsung heroes of this \r\nfilm are Ray Liotta and Jason Lee.  Gene Hackman got top billing, but his \r\ncharacter wasn\'t that great.  Lee, known for his work in Kevin Smith films, \r\nwas sarcasticly funny.  Liotta, <i>Goodfellas</i> ( 1990 ), was also great as a car \r\nrepossessor/ jilted lover.\r\n<P>\r\n     The DVD had some good features.  There were two documentaries on the \r\nmaking of the film, deleted scenes and director\'s audio commntary.\r\n<P>\r\n     <i>Heartbreakers</i> isn\'t <i>Momento</i>, but it is an entertaining film.  Check it \r\nout to at least see Hewitt and Weaver.\r\n', 'chick', 'Sigourney Weaver, Jennifer Love Hewitt, and Gene Hackman', '', 2001, 1),
(49, 'Sorority Boys', '', 2, '2002-04-08', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/sororityboys.jpg\' width=\'149\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    <i>Sorority Boys</i> is somewhere in the middle of great college comedies.  It \r\nwasn\'t as funny as <i>Animal House</i> (1981), but much better than <i>PCU</i> (1994).  \r\nIt is definitely an entertaining film and will be one of those great DVD \r\npurchases.\r\n<P>\r\n     Let me break it down for all of you who went to good colleges and have \r\ntrouble following plots.  Barry Watson, Michael Rosenbaum and Harland \r\nWilliams are members of the KOK frat, the number one party frat in college.  \r\nThey are also the head of the social committee.  When the Booze and Cruise \r\nmoney goes missing they are kicked out.  In order to clear their names and \r\nget good jobs they dress up like sorority sluts and sneak into a party.  When \r\nthey are caught they are taken in by the Delta Omega Gamma a.k.a. DOG \r\nsorority. Hilarity ensues as the three guys dress like girls to get back \r\ninto their frat.\r\n<P>\r\n    There are many great parts to this film.  Number one would be all \r\nthe scenes with boobs.  Lots and lots of boobs.  Second would be the fight \r\nscene between Barry Watson and Michael Rosenbaum sword fighting with dildos \r\nwhile dressed in drag.  The third greatest part of the film would be the \r\nfact they show lots of boobs.\r\n<P>\r\n    Harland Williams was very funny and Melissa Sagemiller shows off her \r\nboobs.  There is also a lesbian scene but I\'m not sure if it really counts \r\nbecause it was between Melissa Sagemiller and Barry Watson who was dressed \r\nas a chick.  Still highly erotic because the illusion of two lesbians making \r\nout is very strong.\r\n<P>\r\n     In conclusion this was a great flick.  Go see it with someone who likes \r\nboobs.', 'movie', 'Barry Watson, Michael Rosenbaum, and Harland Williams', '', 2002, 1),
(50, 'Jim Carrey', 'An Actor with a Place in the World of Film, But I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2002-04-11', 0, '<img src=\'images/carrey.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'207\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    Jim Carrey is probably the worst marketed actor of all time.  Films in \r\nwhich he is supposed to be serious get billed as comedies and then flop.  \r\nFilms that are meant to be family comedies or dark comedies are billed as \r\nthe average run-of-the-mill comedy, get lots of hype, then flop.  It\'s only \r\nwhen Carrey gets no press that he does well.\r\n<P>\r\n     Jim Carrey\'s history is well known.  He grew up poor in Toronto, sent \r\nhis resume into The Carol Burnett Show at age 10, had a series of miserable \r\nfailures but paid his dues to be a star.  What I find remarkable enough is \r\nthat, to steal a line from Cracker, is that he was better when no one knew \r\nhis name and he was pompous.  Carrey\'s early stuff is great.\r\n<P>\r\n     <i>Earth Girls are Easy</i> (1989), <i>Once Bitten</i> (1985), <i>All in Good \r\nTaste</i> (1983), and <i>Ace Ventura: Pet Detective</i> (1994) are all very funny.  \r\nCarrey isn\'t over the top in these films like he was in <i>Liar, Liar</i> (1997), \r\nnor did he try to do too much like in <i>The Truman Show</i> (1998).\r\n<P>\r\n     Carrey is also best when the attention isn\'t all upon him.  In Living \r\nColour was great because it was a variety show, Carrey got his time and was \r\nvery funny.  <i>The Cable Guy</i> (1996) was a great dark comedy because it \r\nwasn\'t about him, it was about Matthew Broderick\'s character.  In that \r\nregards that\'s why <i>Dumb and Dumber</i> (1994) was great while <i>Me, Myself and \r\nIrene</i> (1999) blew.\r\n<P>\r\n    What about Carrey as a serious actor?  I believe he can do it.  I \r\nthought he was great in <i>Man on the Moon</i> (2000) but I never really liked \r\nAndy Kaufman.  <i>The Truman Show</i> sucked because Carrey tried too hard to win \r\nthe Oscar.\r\n<P>\r\n     Jim Carrey\'s place in the world of film? He\'s not a legend, sorry to \r\nsay, not now at least.  He\'s not Buster Keaton or The Three Stooges.  He \r\nneeds to focus on one thing.  He\'s either a comedian or a serious actor.  \r\nTom Hanks pulled it off because Tom Hanks never put himself in the box of \r\ncomedian, but yet he made some very funny movies.\r\n<P>\r\n      Will there ever be a movie based on Carrey\'s life like the one based \r\non Kaufman? I think so.  Give it twenty years after he dies.  I think it \r\nwould be a very good film about a guy no one quite understood.\r\n', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(51, 'Don Knotts', 'An Actor with a Place in the World of Film, But I\'m Not Sure Where', 2, '2002-04-12', 0, '<img src=\'images/knotts.jpg\' width=\'144\' height=\'197\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    Long before The Rock was dressed up as a Egyptian mercenary, there was \r\nanother pimped out pharaoh, The Knotts, except he wasn\'t known as The \r\nKnotts, but more commonly known as Don Knotts.  Knotts started his film \r\ncareer with <i>No Time For Sergeants</i> (1958), a play that he had performed on \r\nBroadway in his earlier years.  This comedic <i>Full Metal Jacket</i> of its time \r\nintroduced Knotts to Andy Griffith, who later made Knotts an Emmy winner \r\nwith The Andy Griffith Show.  But Knotts wasn\'t content with just being an \r\naward winning television star.  The bright lights and silvery dreams of \r\nHollywood called out to Knotts like the voice of God to the prophets of \r\nsome ancient desert tribe.  In 1966 Knotts once again showed the world that \r\nJerry Lewis wasn\'t a comedic genius and starred in <i>The Ghost and Mr. \r\nChicken</i>, where he had to spend the night in a suspected haunted house to \r\ngain an outrageous fortune from some dead uncle.\r\n<P>\r\n   The 1970\'s allowed Knotts to display his acting wonders not only on the \r\nsmall screen with his own variety show and playing the adhesive Mr. Furley \r\nin Three\'s Company, but Knotts also appeared in Disney films.  Yes, Disney. \r\nBefore it was a capitalist movie company that belched out disease-ridden \r\ncartoon lions and lesbian mermaids, Disney made quality films starring the \r\nlikes of Don Knotts and housebroken shaggy dogs. In 1977 Don Knotts played \r\nthe pre-cursor to Chewbacca in <i>Herbie Goes to Monte Carlo</i>.  Knotts was the \r\nmechanic as some dude pretended to be Speed Racer and chase down some \r\ndiamond thieves and win some crazy race through Monte Carlo.  Meanwhile \r\nKnotts is suping this Volkswagon Beetle that is possessed by the ghost of \r\nJunior Wells.\r\n<P>\r\n     The Herbie saga wasn\'t the end of Knotts\'s kung fu grip on the \r\nHollywood scene.  Out of the west came a story about two American \r\ndesperados that fit the personalities of Knotts and Tim Conway.  The film \r\nwas <i>The Apple Dumpling Gang</i> (1975).  Sure there were kids and some goofy \r\nsongs, but Knotts and Conway were the heart of the story:  two rugged \r\nfrontiersmen living out their dreams of Manifest Destiny.  The film received such praise that \r\nthere was a sequel, <i>The Apple Dumpling Gang Rides Again</i> (1979).  This time \r\nConway and Knotts are two outlaws on the wrong side of the law and are \r\ntrying to clear their good names and see that justice is done.\r\n<P>\r\n     But Don Knott\'s greatest cinematic achievement of the 1970\'s \r\nwould be a film entitled <i>Gus</i> (1976).  At this time American heroes were no \r\nlonger pure:  Dirty Harry, Rocky, Santa Clause.  Then along came a mule that could \r\nkick field goals.  The day Gus came to the masses, fascism died.  In Gus was \r\nthe embodiment of the American spirit. Something that should not be was \r\ngranted an opportunity to breathe.  Not only did it breathe, but it survived \r\nand conquered.  Gus, the field goal kicking mule, that son of a bitch that \r\neveryone said would fail, did it.  It kicked a field goal and won the \r\nfucking game.  But it was when the camera got a tight shot of Don Knotts \r\nwith a small tear beginning to form from his eye and he began to cheer at the \r\nvictory, it\'s not what he said.  It was what he showed.  It was as if to \r\nsay, Gus the field goal kicking mule wasn\'t the main character of this \r\nstory, neither was I but it was the people.  It was the working class heroes \r\nthat this story was about.  He was right.\r\n', 'other', '', '', 0, 1),
(52, 'Deliverance', '', 3, '2002-04-25', 8, '<img src=\'images/deliverance.jpg\' width=\'157\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '<i>Deliverance</i> follows the harrowing adventures of four Atlanta city slickers who decide to get back to nature and take\r\na canoe trip down Georgia\'s Cahulawassee River.  They proceed with the expedition despite grave warnings from the locals\r\nthat the river is not to be trifled with, but they soon discover that the rapids are the least of their troubles.\r\n<P>\r\nThis is one of those classic movies that you often hear about, but never get around to watching.  Many people have some\r\nvague awareness of the film as a warning against getting mixed up with hillbillies in the impoverished backwoods of the South.\r\nI was one of these semi-informed masses, and after many months of trying to rent the movie, I decided to just buy the DVD.  And\r\nI\'d say it was definitely worth it.\r\n<P>\r\nThe outing starts off rather innocuously, with the standard horseplay and bravado one would typically expect from four guys\r\ntrying to prove their manhood to nature and themselves.  Their initial encounters with the rapids are tough but exhilirating,\r\nand their first night is spent drinking and eating their fill of fish.  But soon after the journey resumes, the quartet runs\r\nafoul of the locals, and the events that follow are equal parts darkly humorous and deeply disturbing.\r\n<P>\r\nThe performances by Jon Voight and Ned Beatty are very good, and Burt Reynolds was surprisingly great as the headstrong leader\r\nof the group.  I guess <i>Deliverance</i> was filmed back when Reynolds was actually an actor, and not just a washed-up \r\ncaricature of himself.  At any rate, all the participants very accurately portray the initial excitement, then growing anxiousness\r\nand fear of the river journey.  Filmed on location, the river too plays its part very well, and illustrates very well its dual\r\nnature as both a safe harbor from unwanted human contact, and a dangerous force all on its own.\r\n<P>\r\nI\'d rate <i>Deliverance</i> very high, with good marks for sequences of whitewater, kung-fu, shotgun-fu, bow-and-arrow-fu,\r\nand compound fractures.  I thought the very ending was a little fractured and drawn out, but I suppose it did mirror the fact\r\nthat these men will never forget the torment to which they\'ve been subjected.  Highly recommended viewing for anyone thinking\r\nof taking a road trip through the southern states.\r\n', 'movie', 'Jon Voight, Burt Reynolds, Ned Beatty', '', 1972, 1),
(53, 'Donnie Darko', '', 3, '2002-04-28', 9, '<img src=\'images/darko.jpg\' width=\'157\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'High school student Donnie Darko\'s world is going to end in twenty-eight days.  How does he know this?  Because\r\na vision of Frank-- a man in a very twisted-looking rabbit suit-- told him so.  And Frank has some other things \r\nto tell Donnie, too, things that he\'d better make \r\nsome sense of before it\'s too late. Sound ridiculous?  Maybe.  But to a paranoid schizophrenic like Donnie Darko, \r\ndaytime hallucinations are a very real part of his world.  And as Donnie\'s world begins to unravel more and more,\r\nit\'s going to take every friend he has-- both real and imaginary-- to unlock the secret of saving himself and his\r\nloved ones before he completely loses his mind.\r\n<P>\r\nFirst-time writer/director Richard Kelly has created a gem in <i>Darko Darko</i>.  Themes of philosophy, psychology,\r\ntime travel, relationships, religion, and all the best and worst elements of humanity come together in a very\r\nimaginative story that is truly mindbending.  The casting is excellent, the setting and atmosphere are extremely\r\nimmersive, and the soundtrack is superb.  I felt a very <i>American Beauty</i>-like vibe to the film, a sense of\r\nhope and redemption even in tragedy.  Universal themes of changing the world and sacrificing oneself for their loved\r\nones are here, but Kelly weaves them together in an intricate fashion to create a very original story.\r\n<P>\r\nIf you like neat, tidy Hollywood endings, you\'ll be very confused by this film.  But if you thrive on the kinds of\r\nmomentous pictures that hold you spellbound throughout and leave saying \"whoa...\" at the end, then don\'t you dare\r\nmiss <i>Donnie Darko</i>.\r\n', 'movie', 'Jake Gyllenhaal, Jena Malone, Drew Barrymore, Noah Wyle', '', 2001, 1),
(54, 'The Scorpion King', '', 2, '2002-04-29', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/scorpionking.jpg\' width=\'150\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '  <i>The Scorpion King</i> is the greatest prequel to a sequel of a remake.  Why? \r\nBecause I think it\'s the only one.  But more than that, <i>The Scorpion King</i> is \r\na very entertaining film.  It\'s not going to lead an Egyptian independence \r\nmovement like <i>Braveheart</i> did in Scotland, nor win any Oscars.  However, <i>The \r\nScorpion King</i> will give the Rock a better acting reputation than other pro \r\nwrestlers, like perhaps, Hulk Hogan.\r\n<P>\r\n  Here\'s the story.  The Rock plays Mathayus, an assassin sent to kill the \r\nsorceress of an evil king who is conquering all the tribes of the Nile \r\nregion.  Instead of killing her, he gets captured, but the sorceress saves \r\nhis life.  The Rock now vows to kill the evil king, but in the process bone \r\nthe sorceress.\r\n<P>\r\n   A very simple story, which is good, because there wouldn\'t be enough room \r\nfor the non-stop action. Lots of fight scenes, jumping through fire scenes, \r\nand various other death-defying scenes.  Scorpion King reminded me alot of \r\nConan The Barbarian, except more fight scenes.\r\n<P>\r\n  How did the Rock do? I wasn\'t really surprised that the Rock could make \r\naudiences believe that he was going to fight someone, win, and challenge \r\nsomeone else to fight. That\'s what he\'s been doing in the WWF for years now. \r\nThe fights scenes with the evil king, Memnon, played by Steve Brand were \r\nvery well done and very entertaining. He also had good chemistry with his \r\nco-stars as well which included the sorceress, played by Kelly Hu and the \r\nleader of another tribe, Balthazar, played by Michael Clarke Duncan.\r\n<P>\r\n  It wasn\'t cheesy and filled with WWF Rock jokes, but Dwayne Johnson gave \r\n<i>the Scorpion King</i> a character of his own. A very entertaining film that \r\neveryone in the family could enjoy.\r\n', 'movie', 'The Rock, Michael Clarke Duncan, and Kelly Hu', '', 2002, 1),
(55, 'Star Wars: Attack of the Clones', '', 3, '2002-06-01', 8, '<img src=\'images/starwars2.jpg\' width=\'160\' height=\'196\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'George Lucas has learned his lesson.  Nearly all of the mistakes that made <i>Episode I</i> so dreadful are thankfully absent in the \r\nlatest installment of the Star Wars saga.  The story has gotten more interesting, the effects are much improved,\r\nand the irritating Jar Jar Binks gets very little screen time.  <i>Attack of the Clones</i> is not only better than the pitiful <i>Phantom Menace</i>,\r\nit\'s arguably even better than <i>Return of the Jedi</i>.  Lucas seems to have written this Episode to parallel <i>The Empire\r\nStrikes Back</i>, and it works very well.\r\n<P>\r\nThat\'s not to say there aren\'t still shortcomings.  Even with the addition of a co-writer, Lucas still struggles with dialogue.  The\r\nfirst half-hour is especially clumsy.  I think George still needs to understand the difference between pedantic dialogue vs. action\r\ndialogue.  Too often he has the Jedi speaking in a very rigid, formal structure right in the middle of a lightsaber battle.  I know\r\nthe Jedi are supposed to be calm in every situation, but save the formalized structure for teaching, and use something more realistic\r\nduring battle.  Especially bad are some of Yoda\'s lines.  When he\'s imparting wisdom to a young Jedi, Yoda\'s reversed grammar is\r\nhella-cool.  But he sounds downright silly using the same structure to issue battle orders: \"Around the survivors a perimeter create.\"  \r\nLame.\r\n<P>\r\nLucas also seems to have failed poli-sci.  Portman\'s Queen Amidala character is now only a Senator, because her two terms of eligibility\r\nfor election have expired.  What the hell is up with that?  Kings and Queens exist only in a monarchy, and are granted their status\r\nfor life based on birthright.  Any student of <i>Monty Python and the Holy Grail</i> can tell you that you don\'t vote for kings.  C\'mon,\r\nGeorge.  It\'s obvious that he can\'t decide whether <i>Star Wars</i> is supposed to be a fairy tale, with kings and princesses and\r\nevil wizards, or a lesson in the importance of democracy.\r\n<P>\r\nAnother problem is Lucas\' apparent inconsistencies with his own story.  The overall plot is right, but the details seem screwed up.  \r\nLike C-3PO calling Anakin \"Master Ani\".  I can\'t imagine a protocol droid ever foregoing that etiquette to call its own creator by\r\na nickname.  Furthermore, at one point in the film, Anakin and the droids visit Tatooine where they meet up with Anakin\'s stepbrother\r\nOwen, the same Uncle Owen who will later be guardian of Luke.  But in Episode IV, Owen has no memory of ever stumbling across these\r\nsame droids before.  Also troubling is that C-3PO spends considerable time in the company of characters who are discussing Obi-Wan \r\nKenobi, so he is obviously aware of his existence.  Yet in Episode IV 3PO accuses R2-D2 of being mental when he says he has a message\r\nfor Kenobi.  Why would a droid \"forget\" one of his past contacts?  And the whole idea of Anakin meeting Owen is problematic, too.\r\nWhy wouldn\'t Anakin (as Darth Vader) later suspect Owen\'s farm as a possible hiding place for Luke?  Lucas had better make sure that\r\nAnakin never knows he has a child at all, because otherwise it\'s just silly to think that Vader wouldn\'t closely monitor Owen for\r\nsigns of treachery.\r\n<P>\r\nBut to some extent I\'m nitpicking here, so let\'s get back to what Georgie did right.  Number one is the action.  Episode II has \r\naction, real bad-mofo, lightsaber-swinging, high-speed spacecraft <i>action</i>, not just some lame pod-race.  And the light-saber\r\nbattles are far more involved and satisfying than the brief duels with Darth Maul and his anti-climactic death-by-falling from\r\nEpisode I.  The other part of <i>Phantom Menace</i>\'s crappy ending, the part where Anakin falls ass-backwards into blowing up a \r\nstarship, is gone here as well, being replaced by one that is much more action-packed, with the sort of down-note ending that made\r\n<i>Empire</i> so great.  \r\n<P>\r\nNumber two is the improved effects.  Episode I looked very fake at times, especially the outdoor battles on the plains of Naboo.\r\n<i>Attack</i> is much more realistic looking, and I rarely, if ever, found myself cursing some part that didn\'t look real enough.\r\nThe depiction of Yoda is especially improved.  He looked like a cartoon in <i>Phantom</i>, here he is much more realistic.\r\n<P>\r\nThe third improvement was a more muted, less irritating form of humor.  Lucas still felt the need to insert humorous moments in\r\nthe final battle scene.  But, unlike the atrocious Jar-Jar hijinks in Episode I, C-3PO\'s pratfalls in this film are far more\r\ntasteful and less disruptive to the mood of the film.\r\n<P>\r\nFourth, Lucas added some excellent spice with Anakin\'s flirtations with the Dark Side.  Although he\'s a bit whiny at times, we see\r\nenough flashes of anger and rage to convince us that the best is yet to come.  Also very cool were the subtle insertions of the\r\nImperial March theme at times when Anakin\'s wrath is at its peak.\r\n<P>\r\nLast but not least was the welcome addition of some tail.  Natalie Portman\'s assets are shown off relatively well in <i>Attack</i>,\r\nand her scenes with Christensen are surprisingly good.  \r\n<P>\r\nAll in all, <i>Attack of the Clones</i> is indeed worthy of being part of the <i>Star Wars</i> franchise, but it makes <i>Phantom Menace</i>\r\nlook that much worse.  Now that Lucas has shown that he is still capable of making a good film, he\'ll go back and completely re-film\r\nEpisode I to bring it up to snuff with the rest of the saga.  Here\'s hoping.\r\n', 'movie', 'Ewen McGregor, Natalie Portman, Hayden Christensen', '', 2002, 1),
(56, 'Windtalkers', '', 3, '2002-06-18', 6.5, '<img src=\'images/windtalkers.jpg\' width=\'146\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'Since September 11th, war movies have been at a premium, capitalizing on the surge of patriotism and pride.  Some, like <i>Black\r\nHawk Down</i>, have been superb.  Others, like <i>Behind Enemy Lines</i>, were the same old crap in new shoes.  <i>Windtalkers</i>\r\nfalls somewhere in between.\r\n<P>\r\nJohn Woo directs this based-on-true-events story of Navajo codetalkers in World War II\'s Pacific theater.  After having numerous\r\nmilitary codes broken by the Japanese, the Marines turned to a new system based on the Navajo language.  The code completely\r\nstumped the Japanese and was instrumental in their defeat.  But the success of the code made its protection much more important,\r\nand bodyguards were assigned to some codetalkers to ensure that the code would never fall into enemy hands.\r\n<P>\r\nNicholas Cage plays Sgt. Joe Enders, a Marine whose blind adherence to orders makes him the perfect man to saddle with the morally\r\ndifficult issue of preventing the capture of the code.  Cage\'s performance is predictably Cage-like.  He acts all brave and\r\nfearless in battle, and stumbles around drunk and melancholy at night.  At least here he doesn\'t use the crappy Southern accent \r\nthat plagued other roles, including <i>Con Air</i>.  I imagine it was Woo who pushed to have Cage for the role, and though I\'d\r\nhoped the decision would turn out to be a good one, it didn\'t.\r\n<P>\r\nI really hated the casting of Peter Stormare as Gunnery Sgt. Hjelmstad.  Stormare was the guy with the Russian accent in \r\n<i>Fargo</i>, the lead nihilist in <i>The Big Lebowski</i>, and the cosmonaut in <i>Armageddon</i>, among other roles.  But here \r\nI couldn\'t for the life of me figure out what accent he was trying to use.  It seemed to drift from Scottish to Russian to Southern \r\nin course of a single sentence.  The rest of his performance was fine, but I was so distracted by the accent that I found him more\r\nannoying than anything.\r\n<P>\r\nThe plot is pretty predictable and pedestrian.  It\'s typical of every partner movie, where the outsider comes in (in this case,\r\nthe Navajo radiomen Ben Yahzee and Charlie Whitehorse) and gets paired with the reluctant partner, Cage.  There\'s awkwardness\r\nand conflict at first, made worse by Cage\'s \"troubled\" character, but by the end they\'re the best of friends and willing to\r\nlay down their lives for each other.  And of course there\'s the redneck soldier who hates Injuns but gets saved by Yahzee and\r\nchanges his mind.  The writing credits go to John Rice and Joe Bateer, and I guarantee they did this one in their sleep.\r\n<P>\r\nThe action is pretty much the only strong point of the film.  The effects and fight choreography are done well for the most part,\r\nbut sometimes feel a bit contrived and gratuitous.  It sometimes seems as though it\'s there just for its own sake, rather than\r\nto really advance the story.  There\'s a detached feeling to it that isn\'t nearly as gripping as the likes of <i>Saving Private \r\nRyan</i> and <i>Black Hawk Down</i>.\r\n<P>\r\nIn the end, <i>Windtalkers</i> is good in that it brings attention to the greatly overlooked contributions of the Navajos in\r\nWorld War II, and does so in a reverent and non-exploitative manner.  But with the exception of the history lesson, you\'ve already\r\nseen this movie before, so you might as well wait for the rental.', 'movie', 'Nicholas Cage, Christian Slater', '', 2002, 1),
(57, 'The Mothman Prophecies', '', 2, '2002-07-03', 6, '<img src=\'images/mothman.jpg\' width=\'150\' height=\'218\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   If this movie wasn\'t based on actual events that happened in West Virginia \r\nit would totally suck.  However, because some of things sort of happened, it \r\nonly sucks.\r\n<P>\r\n   Richard Gere plays John Kline, a writer for the Washington Post.  He and \r\nhis wife get into a car accident where she \"sees\" something, then later \r\ndies.  Gere is broken up about the whole thing, but life goes on.  A year or \r\nso later Gere is sent to interview the Governor of Virginia.  He leaves his \r\nhouse in Maryland and ends up in Bumfuck, West Virgina, totally confused on \r\nhow he got there.  Horrific oddities ensue.\r\n<P>\r\n   I actually identified with Gere in this film.  I myself have gotten in my \r\ncar, ended up in West Virgina, and not known how.  Oddly enough I was in \r\ncollege all of those times, drunk and ended up at a strip club. No real \r\nhorrific oddities except the next morning I would always find a receipt in \r\nmy pocket for a massage and two hundred dollar tip.\r\n<P>\r\n   The rest of the movie went downhill.  Will Patton played a hillbilly \r\nand tried to shoot Gere and Laura Linney played a cop who hooks up with Gere \r\nat the end.  This leads beautifuly into the sequel where Linney uncovers \r\nshocking secerts about Gere\'s past in \"The Gerbil Man Prophecies\".\r\n<P>\r\n    As far as the DVD goes, nothing great.  Some behind the scenes stuff on \r\nhow they destroyed a bridge and a little about the true events.  Everyone \r\nhas probably seen both on A&E a month before the movie came out.  If you\'ve \r\nseen everything else, rent this, but Mothman isn\'t that great.', 'movie', 'Richard Gere, Laura Linney, and Will Patton', '', 2002, 1),
(58, 'Orange County', '', 2, '2002-07-03', 9, '<img src=\'images/orange_county.jpg\' width=\'150\' height=\'198\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   This is a better film than I thought it was going to be.  I thought it \r\nwas going to be just Jack Black being funny, which would have been great, \r\nbut <i>Orange County</i> and Colin Hanks surprised me.  <i>Orange County</i> had depth.\r\n<P>\r\n    Colin Hanks, son of Tom, plays Shaun Brumder, a high school senior who \r\nwants to study writing at Stanford.  He has the grades and the talent, but \r\nhe lacks help from everyone around him.  His guidance counselor sends in the \r\nwrong transcripts, his mother is a raging alcoholic, his father is a rich \r\nprick, and his brother burns down the admissions building.\r\n<P>\r\n   Hanks is persistent in following through on his dream and with the help \r\nof his brother Lance-- played by Jack Black-- and his girlfriend, Schuyler \r\nFisk, they go though mad-capped schemes to get him admitted for the fall.\r\n<P>\r\n    Black was very funny.  He didn\'t have the lead role, but he didn\'t need \r\nto.  He was a very funny supporting character like he was in <i>Saving \r\nSilverman</i> ( 2000 ).  Fisk was also great.  She was the supportive girlfriend \r\nwho didn\'t want to lose her boyfriend.\r\n<P>\r\n   But Hanks was the Diet Coke in this film.  He portrayed a man on his own \r\ntrying to acheive a dream, something that his father\'s characters never did. \r\n  Gump had Bubba, that Castaway guy had the volleyball, but not Shaun \r\nBrumder, he did this one on his own.\r\n<P>\r\n    As far as the DVD went, it had some great special featues, including \r\nabout a dozen trailers I hadn\'t seen featuring Black and some featuring a \r\nhigh school English class.  Very funny.\r\n<P>\r\n    This is definitely a very funny movie and who knows, you might just \r\nlearn something about yourself.', 'movie', 'Colin Hanks, Schuyler Fisk, and Jack Black', '', 2002, 1),
(59, 'The Royal Tenenbaums', '', 2, '2002-07-13', 9, '<img src=\'images/tenenbaums.jpg\' width=\'138\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '    I had a professor in college who was eccentric.  He would ride his bike \r\nto the buildings year around and would reuse the same brown paper bag he \r\nwould carry his lunch in.  One day I had lunch with him and noticed the \r\nbrown paper bag had dates on it.  When I asked him about it, he told me they \r\nwere from the book he was writing.  When I asked him when the book would be \r\nout he laughed and told me he\'s been working on it for ten years and \r\nprobably wouldn\'t be finished for another ten. Okay, that might not be so \r\ncrazy, but if my professor was a character in the film <i>the Royal Tenenbaums</i>, \r\nhe would be the normal guy.\r\n<P>\r\n   <i>The Royal Tenenbaums</i> is a dark comedy centered around a family of \r\nbrilliant wackos.  Gene Hackman plays Royal Tenenbaum, a low-life lawyer, \r\nwho is married to Angelica Huston, a complacent anthropologist. Together \r\nthey have three children, Ben Stiller, a financial guru, Gwyneth Paltrow, an \r\nadopted playwrite, and Luke Wilson, a washed-up professional tennis player.  \r\nWhen the children are still very young yet accomplished in their own \r\nrights, Gene Hackman separates from their mother.  The children are comically \r\nscarred in their own ways.\r\n<P>\r\n     The cast that makes up the Tenenbaums and their cohorts is amazing. Bill \r\nMurray, Danny Glover, and Owen Wilson are very funny in their subtle humor.\r\n<P>\r\n     This film is not for everyone. This film is a very dark ride.  The \r\nstory moves quickly as the children grow up and the wit is even quicker.  \r\nThis is not your typical Zoolander-esque film one would expect from seeing \r\nStiller and Owen Wilson in the credits.  The humor is very dry and highbrow \r\nto say the least. If you were a fan of <i>Rushmore</i> ( 1998 ) then I highly \r\nsuggest this film.\r\n', 'movie', 'Gene Hackman, Gwyneth Paltrow, Luke Wilson', '', 2001, 1),
(60, 'The Road to Perdition', '', 3, '2002-07-15', 8, '<img src=\'images/perdition.jpg\' width=\'158\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'Tom Hanks proves his worth as an actor once again in <i>The Road to Perdition</i>.  This guy just keeps turning\r\nout quality work.  With the notable exceptions of <i>You\'ve Got Mail in Seattle</i> and <i>Sleepless on AOL</i>,\r\nalong with <i>Joe vs. the Volcano</i>, he\'s pretty much owned the 90\'s and sits poised to continue his dominance\r\ninto the new millenium.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Perdition</i> finds Hanks as Mike Sullivan, bodyguard to John Rooney (Paul Newman), a regional manager\r\nfor the Capone Empire.  Mike is as loyal and trustworthy as any employee could ever hope to be; more so in fact,\r\nbecause of his love for Rooney and all he\'s done for the Sullivan family.  Rooney in turn looks upon Mike even\r\nmore favorably than his own son Connor, a ruthless and unstable headcase.\r\n<P>\r\nThings get complicated, however, when Mike\'s son witnesses a murder at the hands of Sullivan and Connor.\r\nDespite pledges of silence, Connor attempts to seal the secret six feet underground, sending Mike and son on\r\nthe road in search of asylum and revenge.  The conflict leaves Rooney in the difficult predicament of choosing\r\nbetween his own son and the son he wished he had.\r\n<P>\r\nAs I mentioned, the acting is superb all around, even the child actor playing Mike Jr. was solid.  The cinematography\r\nis excellent, and the colors lend a very antique noir feel to the film.  \r\n<P>\r\nI thought the story could use some work.  It was based on a graphic novel and revolved around some rather predictable\r\nelements.  Plotwise, nothing here is going to blow you away.  But if looked at from the standpoint of a character\r\nstudy-- focusing primarily on Sullivan and Rooney-- it can be quite fulfilling.\r\n<P>\r\nClaims have been made that <i>The Road to Perdition</i> is the best crime drama since <i>The Godfather</i>, but I\r\nthink that\'s a bit of a stretch.  Even so, it\'s an excellent showcase of terrific acting and a pretty solid period\r\npiece.  An admirable sophomore effort from Sam Mendes, director of <i>American Beauty.</i>\r\n', 'movie', 'Tom Hanks, Paul Newman, Jude Law', '', 2002, 1),
(61, 'Summer Catch', '', 1, '2002-07-14', 5, '<img src=\'images/summercatch.jpg\' width=\'149\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', 'You know, it\'s really amazing what horrible forms of torture one will\r\nsubject themselves to when there is nothing else on the TV late in the\r\nevening on a Sunday night.  Such is how I was exposed to <i>Summer Catch</i>, a\r\nschlocky, predictable, \"feel-good\" vomit-fest starring the worst actor of\r\nour generation, Freddie Prinze Jr.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nSummer Catch is the story of Ryan Dunne, a poor lawn-boy whose dream is to\r\nplay professional baseball.  Dunne qualifies for the Cape Cod summer league,\r\na semi-exclusive league which doubles as a haven for being \"discovered\" by\r\nMajor League scouts.  Early in the season, Dunne falls in love with Tenley\r\nParrish (Jessica Biel), the daughter of one of his rich lawn-care clients.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nThis is one baseball movie that has more cliches than Darryl Strawberry has\r\ndrug arrests.  The girl encourages the guy to achieve his dreams.  The rich\r\ndad tells the girl she can\'t see the poor guy.  The guy chokes in his big\r\ngame.  Then, the guy gets a second chance...with both baseball and the girl.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nI don\'t think I\'m really spoiling anything to reveal that the cliches all\r\nclimax into the ultimate cliche: everything works out fine for everyone\r\ninvolved and they all live happilly ever after.  Whoopity-doo.\r\n<P>\r\n\r\nThe only reasons that this movie got any points from me are for the\r\nfollowing reasons (one-point alotted for each):  (1) The movie featured\r\nbaseball, the best damn sport in the world.  (B) The movie featured Jessica\r\nBiel, who although she possesses marginal (at best) acting skills, is still\r\ntotally hot.  And, (3) the movie features a song from local Pittsburgh rock\r\nfavorites The Clarks -- a small excerpt during an early scene, and in its\r\nentirety over the closing credits.  I just wish for them their song would\r\nhave been used in a better movie.\r\n<P>\r\nSo, if this movie is ever on late on a Sunday night when there is nothing\r\nelse on TV, go to bed, watch SportsCenter (it\'s always on), or carve out\r\nyour eyes with a dull spoon.  Yes, even that is better for you than <i>Summer\r\nCatch</i>.\r\n', 'chick', 'Freddie Prinze Jr., Jessica Biel, Matthew Lillard', '', 2001, 1);
INSERT INTO `Article` (`ID_`, `Title`, `Subtitle`, `Author_ID_FK`, `Article_date`, `Rating`, `Image`, `Image_fullsize`, `Text`, `Type`, `Starring`, `Caption`, `Release_year`, `QA`) VALUES
(62, 'Austin Powers in Goldmember', '', 2, '2002-08-16', 5, '<img src=\'images/goldmember.jpg\' width=\'148\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '   The tagline for the new installment of everyone\'s Swinging Spy is\r\n\"The Secret Will Be Exposed\".  Here\'s the secret: this movie-- much like \r\nmany other sequels-- sucks. <i>Ghostbusters II</i>, <i>Ace Ventura II</i>, any James Bond \r\nfilm post-Sean Connery, and now <i>Austin Powers in Goldmember</i> can all be lumped \r\ntogether as bad ideas gone terribly right.\r\n<P>\r\n   Here\'s the story, Austin Powers, played by Mike Myers, captures Dr. Evil, \r\nplayed by Mike Myers, and then finds out his father has been kidnapped, \r\nplayed by Michael Caine.  Austin goes back in time and meets up with Foxxy \r\nCleopatra, played by Beyonce Knowles (of Destiny\'s Child fame and favorite \"quiet time thought\"\r\n for Greg), who informs Austin his father was \r\nkidnapped by a Dutch Hedonist named Goldmember, also played by Mike Myers.  \r\nGratuitous sexual situations and previously done comic devices ensue.\r\n<P>\r\n     Where did this film go wrong? Mike went to the well too many times.  \r\nThey did the whole Dr. Evil-interrupting-Scott Evil joke, the whole-Clint \r\nHoward-seeing-some-sexual-organ-on-his-radar-screen joke, \r\na-foreign-hot-chick-with-a-sexual-sounding-name-that-Austin-doesn\'t- \r\nget-at-first-joke, and the Dr. Evil-describing-his-plan-but-calls- \r\neverything-by-odd-names-and-Scott Evil-makes-fun-of-him joke.\r\n<P>\r\n      Another problem was the new characters.  In the first Austin Powers film \r\nit was Myers playing Austin and Dr. Evil.  Two totally new and very funny \r\ncharacters.  The second film brought us Fat Bastard and Mini Me.  Two \r\ndifferent characters that were also very funny.  By the third installment \r\nMyers produced Goldmember.  Goldmember was basically a cross of Dr. Evil and \r\nFat Bastard.  I sort of wished Austin would have done a Canadian character \r\nlike an evil French lumberjack or an evil Jewish talkshow host.\r\n<P>\r\n     The movie wasn\'t all bad though.  Verne Troyer returned as Mini Me, \r\nBeyonce Knowles did a great job spoofing a composite of Pam Grier characters \r\nfrom the 1970\'s and I thought Fred Savage\'s character, The Mole, was funny.  \r\nThe cameos were also very entertaining, which included Tom Cruise, Danny \r\nDeVito, the Osbournes and Gwyenth Paltrow.\r\n<P>\r\n      Ultimately, this film just tried too hard.  Too many characters bogged \r\ndown the script.  For example, Michael Caine\'s character, Nigel Powers, was \r\neven lost and sort of useless. I hope there will not be another Austin \r\nPowers film, but the ending hints towards yet another sequel.  I just hope \r\nthe next one will include two hosts of a public access television show which \r\nis filmed in Aurora, IL.\r\n', 'movie', 'Mike Myers, Beyonce Knowles, Michael Caine', '', 2002, 1),
(63, 'Dodgeball', NULL, 3, '2004-06-19', 7, '<img src=\"images/dodgeball.jpg\" alt=\"\" />', NULL, 'Once again, the forces of evil have descended upon a beloved neighborhood establishment and threaten to crush it beneath the iron claw of oppression.  The only salvation in sight is a highly improbable competition pitting the good guys vs. the bad in a winner-take-all slugfest.\r\n<P>\r\nThis time, the victim is Average Joe\'s gym, owned and operated by Peter La Fleur (Vince Vaughn).  The corporate juggernaut Globo Gym of White Goodman (Ben Stiller) has bought out La Fleur\'s second mortgage and plans to bulldoze Average Joe\'s to make way for a parking lot.  Unless the patrons of Joe\'s can raise $50,000 in a month, their hangout is history.  Luckily, they spot an ad in \"Obscure Sports Quarterly\" for a dodgeball tourney in Las Vegas, paying out exactly $50,000.  Hooray.\r\n<P>\r\nOkay, that\'s the setup, and you can guess all the rest of the plot.  The only question is whether the laughs along the way make the movie worthwhile.  The answer, for the most part, is yes.\r\n<P>\r\nThe characters are decent.  Average Joe\'s of course has its obligatory group of stock dweebs and uncoordinated losers (with one notable standout), while Globo Gym fields its team of big buff tough guys.  Vaughn\'s La Fleur is much more laid back than his characters in other movies like <i>Swingers</i> and <i>Made</i>.  A decision was obviously made that La Fleur was going to be just as normal as normal could possibly be, but the result is in some ways almost dull.  But he serves as an effective foil to Stiller\'s over-the-top Goodman, an egotistical masochist with bizarre fetishes and an ingrained sense of his divine right to anything he wants.\r\n<P>\r\nPersonally, I wasn\'t all that impressed with Stiller in this movie.  I don\'t think he plays over-the-top characters nearly as well as he plays the normal-but-neurotic guys of <i>There\'s Something About Mary</i> and <i>Meet the Parents</i>.  The Goodman character is funny, but just seemed to be missing something that would have really catapulted him to a gut-busting level.\r\n<P>\r\nThe actual dodgeball action in the movie is relatively thin and doesn\'t provide that many laughs.  The best parts are the training sequences with Average Joe\'s coach, Patches O\'Houlihan (Rip Torn), and the in-game play-by-play from Cotton McKnight (Gary Cole) and spaced-out color commentary of Pepper Brooks (Jason Bateman).  \r\n<P>\r\nI feel compelled to compare <i>Dodgeball</i> to another sports spoof, Trey Parker and Matt Stone\'s <i>BASEketball</i>, and I find that <i>Dodgeball</i> just doesn\'t measure up to the latter.  <i>BASEketball</i>\'s great commentary on the nature of professional sports, along with the sheer absurdity of the game itself and the great \"psych-outs\" put it on top.  Parker and Stone display a creativity that\'s just unmatched in this offering.\r\n<P>\r\nOverall, <i>Dodgeball</i> is an amusing diversion and well worth viewing, but never quite lives up to all the potential of its stars or its subject.', 'movie', 'Vince Vaughn, Ben Stiller, Christine Taylor', NULL, 2004, 1),
(64, 'Master and Commander', NULL, 3, '2004-06-30', 5, '<img src=\'images/master.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, '<i>Master and Commander: The Far Side of the World</i> could have been a great swashbuckling epic.  Indeed, it appears to be quite accurate in its portrayal of 19th century naval warfare.  Unfortunately, no matter how hard it tries, it just isn\'t very exciting or inspirational or awe-inspiring or any of the other things a great epic should be.\r\n<P>\r\nI think Hollywood basically just got tired of Russell Crowe going around to different bars and starting fights, so they decided to make a movie where he starts fights on a boat.  But then they remembered that Robin Williams already made <i>Popeye</i> into a movie and it didn\'t do very well at the box office, so Hollywood paid some snooty intellectuals to toss in some historical accuracy as well.\r\n<P>\r\nThe problem with <i>M and C</i> is that there\'s only enough story to support about 30 minutes worth of action.  So some stuff happens at the beginning, and some stuff happens at the end, but other than a few little incidents through the middle (including the obligatory \"big sea storm\" scene), not much happens.  Mostly it\'s just Russell Crowe dealing with the whiny ship\'s doctor about whether to go look at turtles or not.  Exciting stuff, that is.\r\n<P>\r\nMy recommendation is to wait until this one\'s on network tv, and then just watch the first and last half hours.  You won\'t miss a thing, and won\'t have to pay for it, either.', 'movie', 'Russell Crowe', NULL, 2003, 1),
(65, 'Spider-Man 2', NULL, 3, '2004-07-07', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/spiderman2.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, 'There are certain things that we\'ve come to expect from a Spider-Man movie:  web-fu, a cameo of Stan Lee helping someone dodge falling debris, and Kirsten Dunst\'s nipples poking through a wet shirt.  <i>Spider-Man 2</i> delivers on all three expectations and more, and not in an <i>Austin Powers 3</i> \"didn\'t-they-already-do-that?\" sense.   Meet the new Spidey, same as the old, but in a good way.\r\n<P>\r\nIn this installment, Oscorp sponsors the brilliant Doctor Octavius in his quest for controllable fusion.  It is a noble endeavor but one doomed to fail because this is an action movie.  Octavius apparently isn\'t content with sticking his arms in those rubber-sleeves-behind-glass that most scientists use; he\'s invented four massive AI-augmented mechanical tentacles that he can control through neural connections jacked directly into his spine.  Pretty sweet, and one wonders why Oscorp doesn\'t just make its millions in mechanical tentacle sales.  But hey, it\'s a comic book movie, we\'ll forgive them that minor point, too.  \r\n<P>\r\nOf course, accidents happen, chaos ensues, and the tentacles take over Doc Ock\'s mind and make him really grumpy.  Plus, Harry Osborn is still holding a big grudge for the death of his father.  Can Spider-Man save the day, protect his loved ones, and get his homework done on time?\r\n<P>\r\nDespite the fun I poke, the story is pretty solid.  Parts of the plot arc feel like they\'ve been boosted from <i>Superman 2</i>, but I\'m told that it\'s really based on the <i>Amazing Spiderman</i> comic, Issue #50, which was published in 1967.  It\'s a story that could be predictable, but the writers do a good job of teasing us with other possibilities and making the whole deal fun enough that we\'re on the edge of our seat no matter how it turns out.\r\n<P>\r\nThe special effects are pretty much comparable to the previous installment.  Doc Ock\'s tentacles reminded me a lot of the Sentinels from <i>The Matrix</i>, but were still quite believeable in their movements.  The CGI is apparent in the surreal sheen of the colors in many scenes, but it\'s easily overlooked and never as bad as the wooden zombie graphics of <i>The Matrix Reloaded</i>.\r\n<P>\r\nMy only real complaint is that there were way too many women screaming in B-movie fashion.  The operating room scene is especially laden with gratuitous screaming.  I suppose it\'s somewhat in line with the comic book motif of helpless women and such, but I found it distracting.\r\n<P>\r\nOverall, <i>Spider-Man 2</i> equals, if not surpasses, its predecessor.  Director Sam Raimi has the makings of a really great franchise on his hands, possibly as good as <i>Indiana Jones</i> or <i>Back to the Future</i>.  It\'s already surpassed the <i>Batman</i> series, despite that franchise\'s exceptionally strong episode with Jack Nicholson as the Joker.  Here\'s hoping that Raimi continues to get it right, because after all, with great power comes great responsibility.\r\n<P>\r\nOh, and something I\'ve always wondered:  who goes around cleaning up all those long web ropes that Spider-Man leaves hanging from all the buildings as he swings down the street?  Surely they\'re a bigger mess than dog crap on the sidewalk, so there\'s gotta be a law about cleaning it up.  Does Windex work for that?  If so, why don\'t Spidey\'s enemies use it against him?  Maybe he could have a big showdown with Mr. Clean and the Scrubbing Bubbles.  Are you listening, Stan Lee?  I want 10% royalties on this stuff...\r\n', 'movie', 'Tobey Maguire, Kirsten Dunst, Alfred Molina', NULL, 2004, 1),
(66, 'Anchorman', NULL, 3, '2004-07-12', 9, '<img src=\'images/anchorman.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, 'Will Ferrell turns out his best movie to date with <i>Anchorman</i>.  I haven\'t laughed this hard or this often since <i>South Park</i>.  Absurdity reigns supreme and the quotable lines just keep rolling.  If you miss this one, you\'re missing the funniest comedy in a long time.\r\n<P>\r\nMake no mistake, <i>Anchorman</i> is NOT \"The Daily Show\" on the big screen.  Some may have expected it to be, especially with \"Daily Show\" veteran Steve Carell on the cast.  The movie really just uses the newsroom as a stage for its antics.  Carell\'s character, moron weatherman Brick Tamland, will probably catch many viewers by surprise because of how different he is from his \"Daily Show\" persona, but he is extremely funny nonetheless.\r\n<P>\r\nThe film is filled with great cameos.  Vince Vaughn, Luke Wilson, Ben Stiller, Jack Black, Tim Robbins, and others turn in great bit parts.  Fred Willard is a bit underutilized as the news director, but Chris Parnell lends great support as his assistant.\r\n<P>\r\nI feel like this is a really lame review because there\'s not much else I can tell you without ruining all the jokes for you.  The \"jazz flute\" scene is probably the best burst of hilarity.  The \"rumble\" in the street is golden.  The \"playful banter\" between Burgundy and Corningstone during the newscast credits will leave you howling.  And my new automatic response at any point when I don\'t know what to say is now, \"I love lamp.\"\r\n<P>\r\nTrust me, this one is a must-see.', 'movie', 'Will Ferrell, Christina Applegate', NULL, 2004, 1),
(67, 'The Village', NULL, 1, '2004-07-31', 7.5, '<img src=\'images/village.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, 'As has been thoroughly chronicled here on TopFiveMovies, M. Night \r\nShyamalan is a cinematic force to be reckoned with.  The strength of his \r\nstories, the beauty of his pictures, and the surprising and clever \r\ntwists in his plots have quickly turned him into a top Hollywood filmmaker.\r\n<P>\r\nIn Shyamalan\'s latest film, <i>The Village</i>, his star has dimmed ever so \r\nslightly.\r\n<P>\r\nThe cast of <i>The Village</i> is nearly perfect, led by newcomer Bryce Howard \r\n(the daughter of Ron Howard) and filled out with great performances from \r\nJoaquin Phoenix, William Hurt, and a brilliant performance by Adrien \r\nBrody.  Miss Howard was absolutely riveting as Ivy Walker, a young blind \r\nwoman who becomes the unwitting centerpiece in the struggle between the \r\nvillagers and the strange mythic creatures inhabiting the forbidden \r\nwoods at the village\'s border.  Howard and co-star Joaquin Phoenix \r\nstrike up an uneasy, innocent, but strong relationship that serves as \r\nthe impetus for conflict, change, and resolution.\r\n<P>\r\nThe only weakness in the cast was Sigourney Weaver who struck me as just \r\nhappy to be in a good movie for a change - and not very believable as \r\nPhoenix\'s mother.  But I digress...\r\n<P>\r\nUnfortunately, the story let me down. \r\n<P>\r\nThe <i>idea</i> of the story was great.  A village of resolute but \r\nthreatened idealists squares off against forces real and imagined, \r\ninternal and external.  The initial execution was great.  The \r\nintroduction of the villagers and the establishment of the story were \r\ndone with grace and subtlety.  But, some of the standard Shyamalan \r\n\"twists\" came way too soon and/or were a bit too predictable - which \r\nrobbed the movie\'s second half of potential drama and tension and robbed \r\nthe ultimate revelation of its gravity.\r\n<P>\r\nIn all, <i>The Village</i> was rich and ultimately rewarding, if not a bit \r\nill-paced.\r\n<P>\r\n(Fire away, Shyamalites...)', 'movie', 'Bryce Dallas Howard, Joaquin Phoenix, William Hurt', NULL, 2004, 1),
(68, 'Harold & Kumar Go To White Castle', NULL, 3, '2004-08-09', 8, '<img src=\'images/haroldkumar.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, 'Harold and Kumar have a serious case of the munchies.  And this kind of craving can\'t be satisfied by any ordinary delivery or fast food.  No, this time it\'s gotta be White Castle.  If only they can find it.  Along the way they have to battle racist cops, \"extreme\" skater punks, dorky stalkers, and Neil Patrick Harris.  All in a day\'s work for two guys looking to score some herb, fill their bellies, and land a date with the hella-cute chick from down the hall.\r\n<P>\r\nGranted, there\'s not much in the way of a real plot to <i>Harold & Kumar Go to White Castle</i>.  It\'s mostly just to set the stage for their comic misadventures.  But that\'s okay, because their misadventures are quite comic indeed.  The laughs range from slapstick to satire, with a healthy dose of stoner humor and gross-out gags thrown in.  The movie is mostly in the mold of <i>American Pie</i> or <i>Road Trip</i> -- with more drug humor -- so if you liked either of those you will definitely like this one.\r\n<P>\r\nThe themes are pretty much the same as any R-Rated teen comedy:  the uptight nerdy guy struggles to overcome his doormat attitude with help from his more free-spirited buddy.  The difference with <i>Harold and Kumar</i> is that it casts actors of Korean and Indian descent in those roles.  Deciding whether or not this is one of those \"empowering minorities\" movies is an exercise best left to the intellectuals.  Meanwhile, we\'ll just sit here and listen to \"Extreme Mix Tape 5\" and say with certainty that at least Neil Patrick Harris\' wallet was empowered.\r\n<P>\r\nActing is all-around solid for a comedy, with an amusing cameo by Jamie Kennedy along with the well-known NPH appearance.  \r\n<P>\r\nAll in all, <i>Harold and Kumar</i> is a surprisingly hilarious comedy that should take its place in the pantheon of great ganja-related flicks.\r\n', 'movie', 'John Cho, Kal Penn, Neil Patrick Harris', NULL, 2004, 1),
(69, 'Chasing Liberty', NULL, 3, '2004-08-08', 5, '<img src=\'images/liberty.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, 'In <i>Chasing Liberty</i>, Mandy Moore plays the teenage daughter of the President.  She\'s tired of having her every move shadowed by the Secret Service, who of course just want to protect her but end up ruining her date with some tool. So in the middle of a concert in Prague, she runs away from the agents and hitches a ride with some guy on his scooter.  She convinces him to accompany her to some \"Love Parade\" in Berlin, but unbeknowst to her, he\'s also actually a Secret Service agent tasked with protecting her.\r\n<P>\r\nIf you can\'t see all the angles in this one, you\'re not too bright.  Of course he falls in love with her and can\'t decide between lust and duty.  Of course she finds out that he\'s really an agent planted by her father.  Of course fate tears them apart and puts them back together again.\r\n<P>\r\nThe most entertaining aspect of the movie is the side-story between two agents played by Jeremy Piven and Annabella Sciorra.  Piven spends most of his watch hitting on Sciorra (amusingly only using her last name, as \"all\" police partners do), she brushes him off.  Of course, they eventually fall in love, too.\r\n<P>\r\nMorals of the story:\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Teens, your parents love you and want what\'s best for you, and they\'re probably watching you even when you don\'t know it.</li>\r\n<li>Parents, the chaperone you send to watch your child may ironically be the very guy that deflowers her.</li>\r\n<li>Employees, if you sexually harass your co-worker long enough, eventually she\'ll grow to love you.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<P>\r\nOverall, it\'s a tolerable movie.  Mandy Moore is cute and even naked in one scene (though regrettably it\'s a long-distance rear shot that reveals nothing).  There\'s not too much sappiness and nothing ridiculously unrealistic happens.  You could do a lot worse than <i>Chasing Liberty</i>.', 'chick', 'Mandy Moore, Jeremy Piven, Mark Harmon', NULL, 2004, 1),
(70, 'Bubba Ho-tep', NULL, 3, '2004-08-11', 8.5, '<img src=\'images/bubba_hotep.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, 'The King is Alive.  After trading places with an impersonator shortly before his alleged \"death\", Elvis has spent the last few years in a retirement home in East Texas.  But giant cockroaches and unnatural deaths have been plaguing the residents, and it\'s up to Elvis and fellow patient JFK to find and destroy the Egyptian mummy who\'s responsible.\r\n<P>\r\nOf course it sounds ridiculous, and at times this low-budget flick from director Don Coscarelli looks a little ridiculous, too.  One would hardly expect great filmmaking from the guy who brought us the <i>Beastmaster</i> and <i>Phantasm</i> franchises.  But great filmmaking it is.  Coscarelli reminds us all that excellent writing and editing are still worth more than all the special effects money can buy.\r\n<P>\r\nElvis is played to perfection by Bruce Campbell.  The voice, the mannerisms, the attitude; they\'re all there and they\'re spot on.  Presley is an extremely sympathetic character, the former King reduced to watching TV, using a bedpan and walker, and rubbing salve on his infected growths.  This is a man who had it all.  Now he\'s got nothing more than a bed to sleep in.\r\n<P>\r\nOssie Davis is excellent as John F. Kennedy, now dyed black for his protection and more obsessed with conspiracies than Oliver Stone.  The interplay between Elvis and JFK is great and Coscarelli\'s dialogue is fantastic.  Clearly, Ossie\'s character is a loony who only believes himself to be Jack... or is he?  This ties into the real theme of the movie: identity.\r\n<P>\r\n<i>Bubba Ho-tep</i>\'s real story is the quest for meaning in a life near its end.  It\'s about finally becoming the hero you\'ve always pretended to be.  It\'s about coming to grips with your past and making the best future you can.  It\'s about taking responsibility for your mistakes, but taking pride in your life despite them.  And it\'s about no longer dwelling on everything you\'ve lost, but instead holding on to what you have.\r\n<P>\r\nSometimes it takes a movie with such ridiculous circumstances as <i>Bubba Ho-tep</i> to let us see through all the chaos of modern life and really examine these questions.  Will we, at the weakest time of our life, make peace with our past and fight for our souls?  Or will we roll over and give up, content to let the world suck our will to live and flush it down the crapper?  It\'s a question that each of us must answer for ourselves, but the beauty of <i>Bubba Ho-tep</i> is that it brings these questions to light without typical Hollywood schmaltz.\r\n<P>\r\nSo, Mr. Presley, for teaching us the proper way to leave the building, I say, \"Thank you.  Thank you very much.\"', 'movie', 'Bruce Campbell, Ossie Davis', NULL, 2002, 1),
(71, 'Secret Window', NULL, 3, '2004-08-24', 6.5, '<img src=\'images/secret_window.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, '<i>Secret Window</i> is a bland psychological thriller starring Johnny Depp as a writer named\r\nMort Rainey whose personal life is spiraling out of control.  After catching his wife Amy (Maria\r\nBello) in the arms of another man (Timothy Hutton), he retreats to a cabin on the lake with only\r\nhis dog, his computer, and occasional visits from a cleaning lady to keep him company.  He soon\r\ngets an unwelcome visitor in the form of John Shooter (played wonderfully by John Turturro), who\r\naccuses Rainey of stealing one of his stories.\r\n<P>\r\nThe majority of <i>Secret Window</i> is Rainey\'s attempt to prove his innocence to the increasingly\r\npsychotic and violent Shooter.  Although Rainey has been known to \"borrow\" from other writers on\r\noccasion, this time he is clearly in the right, and this particular story was semi-autobiographical,\r\nso it couldn\'t have been plagiarized.  Still, Shooter insists that the tale is his, except for what\r\nhe claims is a much better ending, and will not rest until Rainey republishes the story with Shooter\'s\r\nimproved conclusion.\r\n<P>\r\nThe plot of <i>Secret Window</i> is based on a book by Stephen King, who has covered the topic\r\nof obsessed fans several times before.  Knowing that King is involved, I would have expected a much\r\nmore thrilling movie, but it mostly falls flat.  The conclusion is refreshingly un-Hollywood-like,\r\nbut somewhat predictable.  There are some clever clues and foreshadowing that redeem the film a bit,\r\nand a few moments of dark humor, but ultimately it\'s just not that interesting.\r\n<P>\r\nOn the flip side, Turturro\'s unhinged performance as John Shooter is satisfying to watch, though he unfairly gets\r\nsqueezed out at the end of the movie by Depp\'s more melodramatic turn.  The dialogue and general\r\nwriting throughout is solid, and there are some interesting camera shots from director David Koepp,\r\nwho also directed <i>Stir of Echoes</i> and contributed to the screenplays of <i>Spider-Man</i>,\r\n<i>Panic Room</i>, <i>Carlito\'s Way</i>, <i>Jurassic Park</i>, and more.  The lack of a strong\r\nsuspenseful feeling in an otherwise good script indicates that perhaps Koepp ought to stick with\r\nthe writing and leaving the directing up to others.\r\n<P>\r\nAll in all, <i>Secret Window</i> is far from being a complete waste of time, especially for fans\r\nof Depp and Turturro, but don\'t expect to be scared out of your wits or blown away by amazing \r\noriginality, because you won\'t find it here.\r\n', 'movie', 'Johnny Depp, John Turturro, Maria Bello', NULL, 2004, 1),
(72, 'M. Night Shyamalan', 'Defending Night Against Misguided Viewers', 3, '2004-08-31', NULL, '<img src=\'images/shyamalan.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, '</p>\r\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nOn August 29, the user \"Skoffin\" posted a story on <a href=\"http://www.kuro5hin.org\" target=\"kuroshin\">kuro5hin.org</a> \r\n\r\ncalled <a href=\"http://www.kuro5hin.org/story/2004/8/26/131133/514\" target=\"kuroshin\">The Problem with M. Night \r\n\r\nShyamalan</a>.  <span style=\"color: firebrick; font-weight: bold;\">Both that article and the following essay are full of \r\n\r\n\"spoilers\", so if there \r\n\r\nare one or more movies by Night that you haven\'t seen, don\'t read either of these yet.</span>  Skoffin\'s thesis is that \r\n\r\nalthough Night is well-educated in the art of filmmaking, his stories contain flaws in the form of events that seem contrived \r\n\r\nor poorly chosed dialogue.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn short, Skoffin is far off base.  The validity of his thesis may be debatable but the support he offers for the idea is \r\n\r\nlaughably poor and implies his utter lack of ability to even partially comprehend the deeper meaning of any of Night\'s films.  \r\n\r\nThe author mentions that he\'s only seen some of the films once.  It\'s very difficult to properly dissect all the merits of \r\n\r\n<i>any</i> movie on only one viewing, and because of the many layers of style and thematic content of Night\'s films, \r\n\r\ndismissing his work after watching it once does a great disservice to both the director and the audience.\r\n</p>\r\n<h3>Unbreakable</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nSkoffin first attacks <i>Unbreakable</i> and its ending, especially Elijah\'s final statement, \"They called me Mr. Glass.\"  \r\n\r\nThis he pulls out of context not only of the scene, but the entire movie.  The entire statement is, \"I should have known \r\n\r\nbecause of the kids: They called me Mr. Glass.\"  Skoffin derides the quote as absurd, as if Elijah literally means that\r\nbeing called names by kids is what drove him to supervillainy.  That\'s not at all true.  Elijah is referring to the whole\r\ncollection of comic-book style indicators underlying the lives of himself and David Dunn.  He is saying that his destiny\r\nshould have been obvious to himself much earlier; that he, like the audience, did not pick up on all the classic comic\r\nbook indications.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nRemember Elijah\'s core belief: that comic books are true in a sense, that they simply exaggerate the stories of real, \r\n\r\neveryday heroes and villains.  Elijah and David are proof of this theory, and the evidence for it exists everywhere \r\n\r\nthroughout the movie.  Elijah and David are exact opposites, but tied by a thread (weakness to water), just like comic \r\nheroes and their opponents.  Elijah is always represented with the color purple; David with Green.  Note the symbolism of\r\nDavid\'s job as a security guard and the imagery of his poncho as a cape.  Elijah\'s first scene\r\nas an adult, where he is explaining the illustration in his gallery to the customer, is extremely interesting.  His\r\ndescription of typical villain characteristics fits himself exactly, and Night subtlely reinforces this by subsequently\r\nfocusing on Elijah\'s reflection in the glass (which, on yet another layer, is a great symbolic introduction to \"Mr. Glass\",\r\nand we saw the same thing earlier when he was reflected in the tv screen as a boy).  \r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nIn both <i>The Sixth Sense</i> and <i>Unbreakable</i>, Night does an extraordinary job of building a case for his ultimate \r\n\r\nconclusion that\'s so obvious, the audience never sees the forest through the trees.  Watch again the scene in <i>The Sixth\r\nSense</i> where Cole finally admits his visions of dead people.  He\'s staring at Malcolm the entire time and describes his\r\naffliction in a way that practically hits the audience with a sledgehammer to indicate that he\'s talking about Malcolm.\r\nBut the genius of Night is that he\'s able to hide all this in plain sight.  We\'ll see this same technique later in \r\n<i>Signs</i>, as the little girl Bo says, \"There\'s a monster outside my room, can I have a glass of water?\"  The audience \r\nassumes the line is a throwaway, written for humor and cuteness.  But ironically, Bo\'s apparent non-sequiter is exactly\r\nthe remedy needed for this particular monster.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd <i>Unbreakable</i> has far more to offer than just its comic-book style.  Night tempers his moments of secret \r\nobviousness with other techniques that tell the story by way of subtle detail.  As David walks out of the hospital for the\r\nfirst time, a casual glance leads one to believe it to be a moment of puzzled relief.  But look more closely, we see \r\nJoseph force his mother\'s and father\'s hands together.  They hold just long enough for him to turn away, then release\r\ntheir grasp.  The attentive viewer has just been given a very poignant look into this family\'s home life.  I find this\r\nrelationship between David and Audrey to be very moving and I can\'t help but dig into David\'s mind as he struggles with\r\nhis daily depression.  Here is a man who could have been a football star and had everything he could want, but he cast all\r\nthat aside for the sake of a love he no longer feels.  I think of the regret and loss that must haunt him daily.  And so\r\nthere\'s great relief when he learns to make use of those true talents again.  His life reacquires its sense of purpose,\r\nand his resentment for Audrey dissipates.  He\'s happy, hopeful, and ready to repair his personal life.  This is the true\r\ntriumph of David Dunn in <i>Unbreakable</i>.\r\n</p>\r\n<h3>Signs</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nSkoffin\'s next target in the article is <i>Signs</i>, where he admits seeing the movie only once, and it shows.  He\r\nis dead wrong about several basic facts of the movie:  first, he claims Graham\'s wife was trapped between cars (it was\r\na car and a tree).  Next, he claims the character of Merrill is Graham\'s oldest son (Merrill is Graham\'s brother), and\r\nthird he claims Graham remembers the conversation with his wife after being knocked out by the alien (the alien never\r\ntouched him; Graham just \"zoned out\").  None of these details invalidate his argument in itself, but they point\r\nto a pattern of sloppy viewing and inattentiveness that brings into question Skoffin\'s ability to make any sort of\r\nscholarly critique.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe first major bone of contention Skoffin has with <i>Signs</i> is the basement scene where Morgan is struggling with\r\nasthma.  Skoffin apparently only half watched the scene, because he states that Graham cures the asthma by hugging Morgan\r\ntightly, shaking him, and yelling at him.  Skoffin then derides the scene as unrealistic and ridiculous.  Nothing of the\r\nsort ever happened, though.  Morgan is simply lying in Graham\'s lap as the father comforts his son.  He has his hand\r\nlightly on Morgan\'s chest, guiding him, trying to get him to feel Graham\'s own breathing pace and calm down.  Graham does\r\nhave a few harsh words but these are directed at God for threatening to take another loved one from him.  Mostly Graham\'s\r\nspeech is comforting and calming.  Ironically, Skoffin prescribes exactly this sort of treatment for an asthma attack,\r\nand since it\'s what the film in fact depicts, he strikes out completely on this criticism.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nNext Skoffin attacks the idea of Graham\'s wife saying \"swing away\", claiming that it\'s a ridiculous line and a ludicrous\r\ncoincidence on which to hang the entire weight of the family\'s escape from the alien.  But the viewer needs to think back\r\nto the scene which really gets at the heart of the movie: Graham and Merrill sitting on the couch, discussing the difference\r\nbetween people who see miracles, and those who see only dumb luck.  Graham says, \r\n</p>\r\n<blockquote>\r\n\"See what you have to ask yourself is what kind of person are you? Are you the kind that sees signs, sees miracles? Or do you \r\n\r\nbelieve that people just get lucky? Or, look at the question this way: Is it possible that there are no coincidences?\"\r\n</blockquote>\r\n<p>\r\n<i>Signs</i> isn\'t just the story of a guy who regains his faith because a good thing happens, or because his family stuck\r\ntogether to make it through a crisis.  Graham is the recipient of incontrovertible proof that God exists and has guided\r\nevery single aspect of the Hess family to get them through this pivotal day in human history.  That is no small thing.\r\nIt\'s not something that hinges merely on Merrill\'s batting record; it includes Bo\'s water hangups, Morgan\'s asthma, even\r\nColleen\'s death.  Colleen and Dr. Reddy both state their belief that the crash must have been meant to be; if Reddy had \r\n\r\nfallen asleep at any other point in his drive, Colleen would be fine.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAs for the exact choice of words \"swing away\", Colleen meant them to be inspirational.  Note the other\r\nbits of advice:  Tell Morgan to keep playing, tell Bo that Morgan will watch out for her, and most importantly, tell\r\nGraham \"to see\".  See what?  Miracles, not dumb luck.  See the plan of God in their lives.  And finally, Merrill, forget\r\nabout that strikeout record.  I know you felt it was wrong not to swing, so always do what you think is right.  \"Swing away.\"\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nWhile I\'m on the subject of <i>Signs</i>, I\'ll address a couple of other points that some critics have made about the\r\naliens.  The first is, if these creatures are smart enough to travel across the galaxy, why can\'t they operate a doorknob?\r\nThe answer is simple: they can.  Every situation where they\'re blocked by a door also includes an additional brace against\r\nthat door.  In Dr. Reddy\'s house, the alien is locked in the pantry, and the door is blocked by a large table and some \r\nchairs.  It\'s also not hard to imagine that the pantry may not have an inner doorknob.  And when the Hess family is hiding in\r\nthe basement, the creatures try to force the door open and they even turn the knob, but Graham and Merrill use their body\r\nweight and a pick-axe to hold it shut.  After their initial attempt, the aliens no longer try to force the door open; they\r\nmerely make noises to distract the family from their bid to enter through the coal chute.  So it\'s not at all far-fetched\r\nthat doors hold the creatures back, especially since they don\'t seem to be physically very strong.  Morgan\'s book even \r\n\r\nsuggests their advanced brains would reduce the need for physical strength.  And other than some menacing posturing, the\r\nalien in the living room puts up very little resistance to Merrill\'s baseball bat attack.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe other major point that critics of <i>Signs</i> try to make is that no intelligent beings would invade a planet with so\r\nmuch water if they themselves were susceptible to it.  The answer to this is two-fold.  First, a radio playing in the\r\nbasement the next morning features a caller who insists that the creatures \"weren\'t here for our planet... they were here for\r\n<i>us</i>.\"  So the aliens were on a hunt of sorts, and it ought to be plainly obvious that if you want to catch a human, you\r\nhave to go to where the humans live, no matter how treacherous the territory.  Some extremely misguided critics have claimed \r\n\r\nthe water vapor present in normal air should have destroyed the aliens sooner, which is silly.  It\'s a matter of quantity and \r\nconcentration.  Think of all the substances we humans consume on a daily basis that would be toxic in greater amounts.  We\r\nsprinkle salt on all our food, but eat a pound of it at once and you\'ll be dead.  Our drinks are laced with caffeine, a\r\ndeadly poison in amounts of just a few grams, and yet we feed it to our children with few reservations.  The alien skin \r\nprobably operates similar to the way our liver processes alcohol: able to handle or perhaps even thrive on small amounts at a \r\n\r\nslow rate, but easily overwhelmed by overexposure.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nThe second part of the answer is that these\r\ncritics are making a major mistake in trying to ascribe human rationality onto these alien beings.  They\'re aliens, which\r\nmeans they are different.  Just because they\'re humanoid in shape doesn\'t mean they adhere to some arbitrary human ideas\r\nabout rationality and philosophy.  So while these aliens could\r\nconceivably have created some protective suits, perhaps they have religious beliefs that forbid it.  Or maybe they \r\nsubscribe to some \"warrior code\" that would bring them shame for using too great an advantage against their adversaries.  \r\n\r\nPerhaps they\'re like the creature from <i>Predator</i> that takes great satisfaction in the challenge of the hunt, or maybe \r\n\r\nalien environmentalists and animal ethicists have\r\ndeclared it illegal to capture lowly humans without giving them a fighting chance for survival.  Hell, we don\'t even know\r\nfor sure that the creatures we see really are smart enough for intergalactic travel; maybe these are just an inferior,\r\nsubservient class of alien bred to be foot soldiers for the more intelligent race that stays in the mother ship.  Perhaps \r\nit\'s more cost-effective for them to send 20 of these pawns than to send one water-resistant super-soldier.  Decisions\r\nbased on economics often appear to fly in the face of ordinary reason.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd any one of these explanations is just as plausible as any other, because we\'re talking about a completely alien race \r\n\r\nabout which we know virtually nothing.  So please, drop the anthropomorphism and accept that whatever motive these things had \r\n\r\nfor acting as they did is irrelevant to the story of Graham\'s realization of the power and miracles of God.  \r\n</p>\r\n<h3>The Village</h3>\r\n<p>\r\nOkay, back to Skoffin and his next target, this year\'s <i>The Village</i> (In the interest of full disclosure, I myself have\r\nonly seen the film once, so my defense may not be complete. But I intend to see it again soon and may amend this essay if\r\nprudent).  Skoffin is obviously very disappointed that the apparently supernatural and evil monsters in the film turn out\r\nto be harmless disguises.  He expresses deep resentment at seeing the creatures \"defanged\" and wishes for \"monsters just\r\nfor monsters\' sake.\"  Ridiculous.  Shyamalan has thrown a bit of a twist at his audience: instead of incorporating more\r\nsupernatural elements like his previous films, this one is entirely earthly.  The author\'s essay concludes by wishing Night\r\nwould \"break free of his storywriting style\"; well, here\'s somewhere that he did, and Skoffin wasn\'t happy.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSkoffin also attacks the ending of <i>The Village</i>, equating the revelation of its modern-day setting with the end of\r\n<i>Monty Python and the Holy Grail</i> where police officers arrest the knights.  This is absurd and completely misses the\r\npoint of the characters\' lifetime struggles.  <i>The Village</i> HAS to be set in modern times; if not, the audience can\'t\r\nfeel the same connection with the heartache of the Elders.  Think about the strength of will they must possess to stick\r\nwith their settlement through the difficult times.  Think of the tremendous sacrifices they make every time they lose a loved\r\none to some illness that would be trivial to cure with modern medicine.  If the Elders didn\'t have the option to give up \r\ntheir life of hardship for modern marvels that would save their children, then the magnitude of their courage and fortitude\r\nis deeply undermined.  We can appreciate the plights of early pioneers, but they didn\'t have a choice in the matter.  These\r\nElders, however, have to make the conscious decision on a regular basis to maintain their way of life at the expense of\r\nthose they hold dear.  Whether we agree with their choice or not, the implications of their emotional struggle are\r\npowerful indeed.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nSo sorry, Skoffin, the story of <i>The Village</i> is not about fighting monsters in the woods.  It\'s about learning that\r\nyou can\'t run away from hardships and heartaches.  As August Nicholson says, the Elders know now that those things are a\r\npart of life.  Ivy represents to them their original intentions: to run towards hope.  To seek out that glimmer of great \r\nthings and work feverishly to attain it, no matter what the cost.  Skoffin laments that \"\'The Village\' amounts to a sitcom \r\nwhere everything is back to normal at the end. Nothing is changed, no character is developed and the logic behind it all is \r\namazingly flawed.\"  But he is entirely wrong: the Elders have undergone a great shift in their attitude about the purpose\r\nof their settlement.  And while they will be maintaining the illusion of \"Those We Don\'t Speak Of\" for the time being, it is\r\nclear that the ruse cannot last.  Eventually the secret must be passed to a new generation, Ivy\'s generation, and it will\r\nbe up to them to make their own decision about how to seek hope in their lives.  The beauty of <i>The Village</i>\'s ending\r\nis that it\'s not an ending at all; it\'s a beginning.\r\n</p>\r\n<p>\r\nAnd that, I think, is one of the characteristic elements of Night\'s work that I enjoy so much.  The part of the story that\r\nwe see captured in his films is actually only a small fraction of the beginning of the tale, which can then continue on\r\nin our minds eye to new and interesting places.  <i>The Sixth Sense</i> passes the torch of counselor from Malcolm to Cole,\r\nwho we can imagine going on to help countless lost souls find their peace.  In <i>Unbreakable</i>, David Dunn will be\r\nlending a hand to other victims, using his talents to set wrongs right.  Graham of <i>Signs</i> returns to the priesthood\r\nwith renewed belief and desire to help others see miracles as he sees them.  These and <i>The Village</i> all offer this\r\nsame sense of hope in better days and great things to come.  This is the true message of M. Night Shyamalan.\r\n', 'other', NULL, NULL, NULL, 1),
(73, 'Batman Begins', NULL, 2, '2005-06-27', 8, '<img src=\"images/batmanbegins.jpg\" width=\"150\" height=\"222\" />', NULL, '<i>Batman Begins</i> lands in the odd world called \"summer films that are \r\nhighly hyped, but don\'t suck because they are wicked awesome.\"  It wasn\'t \r\nlike <i>Star Wars: Revenge of the Sith</i>, a prequel which (supposedly) tied up \r\nall the loose ends of the Holy Trilogy (Ben Kenobi doesn\'t remember R2 in \r\nNew Hope ? WTF ?)  or one of the big budget Marvel flicks.  <i>Batman Begins</i>\r\nis also (thank God) nothing remotely close to the last two Batman \r\ndisasters.  \r\n<P>\r\nThe film breaks down like this.  Bruce Wayne (Christian Bale) is an \r\nemotionally troubled man dealing with the death of his philanthropist parents \r\nsomewhere snowy and filled with Asian-looking people.  He meets up with \r\nthe mysterious Ducard (Liam Neeson), who does not look Asian, but \r\nteaches him the ways of the justice-seeking ninja.  Wayne returns to \r\nGotham to \" ...strike fear into the hearts of those who prey on those who \r\nare fearful.\"  While there, the movie focuses on him hanging out with his \r\nbutler (Sir Michael Caine), listening to Rachel Dawes (Katie Holmes) talk \r\nout of the side of her mouth about justice, and kicking butt.  Car chases and \r\nexploding neighborhoods ensue.\r\n<P>\r\nThe thing that totally makes this movie turn it out is the cast, the \r\nwriting, and the fact that the story was well done.  The cast is wicked \r\nawesome.  Christian Bale is arguably as good a Bruce Wayne / Batman as \r\nPittsburgh native Michael Keaton.  He played the playboy / brooding \r\nmilionare character well.  Morgan Freeman, Liam Neeson and Sir Michael \r\nCaine\'s characters were well written and well acted.  Above all this film \r\nhad a plot that focused on a long timeline yet moved quickly and thoroughly \r\nthrough all the details.  \r\n<P>\r\n   To sum it all up, <i>Batman Begins</i> is the way comic book movies should be. \r\n Great story, great characters and great thrills.\r\n', 'movie', 'Christian Bale, Katie Holmes, Sir Michael Caine', NULL, 2005, 1);
INSERT INTO `Author` (`ID_`, `Name`, `EMail`, `Image`, `Bio`, `Jobtitle`, `Userid`) VALUES
(1, 'Jesse', 'REDACTED', 'jesse.jpg', 'Please allow me to introduce myself, \r\nI\'m a man of wealth and taste...\r\n<P>\r\nI\'m also a man who writes reviews and commentaries and, i believe, writes \r\nthem pretty well.\r\n<P>\r\nI\'m a man who enjoys the simpler things in life: BMW\'s, fine wine, fine \r\nwomen, good lovin\'.\r\n<P>\r\nWhen I\'m not living the life I love (and loving the life I live), I\'m \r\ndonating my time and monetary resources to various research funds in an \r\neffort to make man\'s existance more pleasant and managable.\r\n<P>\r\nWhen I\'m not funding said research projects, I moonlight as an exotic \r\ndancer at a dance club called \"Mr. Bojangle\'s\".  (Rest assured, it is a \r\n<i>straight</i> establishment.)  Many a lady has known the pleasure of \r\nseeing my sculpted body shimmy and shake in front of them.\r\n<P>\r\nFinally, when time permits, I watch movies and review them for \r\nTopFiveMovies.com.  However, you ladies should know that often times when \r\nI\'m writing, I\'m only wearing a smile...\r\n<P>\r\nUntil the next time we meet, enjoy what you read and read what you enjoy...\r\n', 'Contributing writer', 'jesse'),
(2, 'Bruce', 'REDACTED', 'bruce.jpg', 'I grew up in the small town of Saegertown, PA which is just north of \r\nMeadville.  I hold a degree in Secondary Education Social Studies from \r\nYoungstown State University.  Currently I am a substitute teacher, which is \r\na better term for unemployed.\r\n<P>\r\nI have always been a fan of movies and a fan of putting things in order \r\nfrom greatest to least.  I also have the ability to count from the number \r\none to five in decending order.  Greg once heard of this unique gift \r\nand decided to test me on it.  He gave me five blocks of Spam, all in \r\ndifferent order.  Greg asked me to put the smallest first, then the next \r\nlargest and so on and so forth until I no longer had blocks of Spam.  In two \r\nhours I had finished his task.  He was amazed, then asked me if I could do \r\nthe same with movies.  I told him I could and a website was born.\r\n', 'Contributing writer, honorary founding member', 'bruce'),
(3, 'Greg', 'REDACTED', 'greg.jpg', 'Hey, I\'m Greg, and just about everything you\'ve read about me is true.  \r\n<P>\r\nSo instead of sitting here and rehashing the legendary accomplishments of my illustrious life,\r\nI\'ll stick to highlighting a few of the places I\'ve traveled:\r\n<P>Well I\'ve been up to Paris, and I\'ve slept in a park. \r\nWent down to Barcelona, someone broke in my car.\r\n<P>\r\nTook the train down to Athens, and I slept in a fountain. \r\nSome Swiss junkie in Turin, ripped me off for my cash. \r\n<P>\r\nThe CRS on the metro shook me down for a bribe;\r\nOn my knees for the sergeant when my passport arrived. \r\n<P>\r\nCalled my mom from a payphone, I said \"I\'m down to my last.\" \r\nShe said \"I sent you to college, now go call your dad.\" \r\nAnd the waitress that he married, well she hung up the phone. \r\nYou know she never did like me, but I can stand on my own. \r\n<P>\r\nSold my plasma in Amsterdam: spent it all in a night \r\nBuying drinks at the Melk Weg for a soldier in drag. \r\n<P>\r\nGot a tattoo in Berlin and a case of the crabs. \r\nA rose and a dagger on the palm of my hand. \r\n<P>\r\nYeah, I\'ll search the world over for my angel in black. \r\nYeah, search the world over for a Euro-trash Girl.\r\n', 'Founding member, Webmaster, Oriental Despot', 'greg'),
(4, 'Nick', 'REDACTED', 'nick.jpg', '<p>\r\nMy, my, my music hits me so hard<br />\r\nMakes me say \"Oh my Lord\"<br />\r\nThank you for blessing me<br />\r\nWith a mind to rhyme and two hype feet.<br />\r\nIt feels good, when you know you\'re down<br />\r\nA super dope homeboy from the Oaktown<br />\r\nAnd I\'m known as such<br />\r\nAnd this is a beat, uh, you can\'t touch<br /> \r\n</p><p>\r\nEvery time you see me<br />\r\nThe Nick\'s just so hype<br />\r\nI\'m dope on the floor and I\'m magic on the mic<br />\r\nNow why would I ever stop doing this?<br />\r\nWith others making records that just don\'t hit<br />\r\nI\'ve toured around the world, from London to the Bay<br />\r\nIt\'s \"Nick, go Nick, MC Nick, yo Nick\"<br />\r\nAnd the rest can go and play...<br />\r\n</p> \r\n', 'Midwest correspondent, \"The Courtier\"', 'nick'),
(6, 'Matt', 'REDACTED', 'matt.jpg', '<table cellspacing=\'2\' cellpadding=\'2\' width=\'100%\'>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td colspan=\'2\' class=\'authorName\'>VITAL STATS</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\' width=\'15%\'>Full name:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Matt</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Height:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>5\'9\"</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Weight:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>134lbs (soaking wet)</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Eyes:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>dark blue</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Hair: </td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>brown (dyed: green, blue, or blonde)</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Birthplace:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>New Castle, PA</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Current Residence: </td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Wasco, California</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td colspan=\'2\' class=\'authorName\'>&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td colspan=\'2\' class=\'authorName\'>FUN FACTS</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Expression:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>\"Dude\"</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Color:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Black</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Foods:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Most cereals, peanut butter & honey on white</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Films:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>The Star Wars Trilogy</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Shows:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Seinfeld, The Simpsons, Sportscenter</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Actors: </td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Kevin Spacey, Edward Norton, John Cusack</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Actresses:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Audrey Hepburn, Halle Berry</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Fears:</td>\r\n    <td class=\'bodyText\'>Registrars, Long pants, Winter jackets (or any jackets for that\r\nmatter)</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>\r\n', 'Westside correspondent, Miscreant', 'matt'),
(7, 'Brian', 'REDACTED', 'brian.jpg', '<table width=\'100%\' cellspacing=\'0\' cellpadding=\'4\'>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\' width=\'25%\'>Color:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Blue</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Food:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Crispix/Rice Chex</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Sports:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Football,Hockey,Basketball</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Album:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>tie; The Rising Tide (Sunny Day Real Estate)/1984 (Van Halen)</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Best Academic Performance:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'bottom\'>5th Grade, O\'Hara Elementary</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Actors:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Robert DeNiro, Jimmy Stewart, Brad Pitt, Edward Norton, Bruce Willis</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Actresses: </td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Grace Kelly </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Animal:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Ankliosaurus </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'top\'>Best Experience:</td>\r\n	<td class=\'bodyText\' valign=\'bottom\'>1997-1998 (Sophomore Year) </td>\r\n</tr>\r\n</table>', 'Founding member, Renaissance man', 'brian'),
(8, 'Ryan', 'REDACTED', 'ryan.jpg', '<p><i>(* name cleverly changed to protect professional reputation)</i></p>\r\n\r\nI had a great-great uncle named Cuffy in Brookville.  He was an institution in Brookville for years as an auto mechanic.  He also accumulated many antique vehicles.  Anyways, when I \r\nwas a junior in high school, there was a sort of tribute to him because he \r\nwas the oldest living member of the American Legion in PA.  He was also one \r\nof the few remaining WWI vets.  To put it simply, my basketball coach caught \r\none of the articles, and when I was moving slowly in practice the next day, \r\nhe yelled \"your old uncle Cuffy could move faster than that!\".  From those \r\nhumble roots, the nickname was born.  I am now known almost exclusively as \r\nCuffy to my high school friends, their parents, and my teachers.\r\n', 'Founding member, Space cadet', 'ryan'),
(9, 'The TopFiveMovies Staff', '', '', '', '', 'none');
INSERT INTO `Quote` (`ID_`, `Quote`) VALUES
(1, '\"The only true currency in this bankrupt world is what you share with someone else when you\'re uncool.\" -- Almost Famous'),
(2, '\"Forget it, Jake.  It\'s Chinatown.\"  --Chinatown'),
(3, '\"This town needs an enema!\"  --Batman'),
(4, '\"Politicians, ugly buildings, and whores all get respectable if they last long enough.\"  --Chinatown'),
(5, '\"Stanley, see this? This is this. This ain\'t something else, this is this!\"  -- the Deer Hunter'),
(6, '\"They\'re not gonna catch us.  We\'re on a mission from God.\"  -- the Blues Brothers'),
(7, '\"I say hurl.  If you blow chunks and she comes back, she\'s yours.  If you spew and she bolts, then it was never meant to be.\"  -- Wayne\'s World'),
(8, '\"Charlie don\'t surf!\"  -- Apocalypse Now'),
(9, '\"You\'re an errand boy, sent by grocery clerks, to collect a bill.\"  -- Apocalypse Now'),
(10, '\"Are you not entertained?\"  -- Gladiator'),
(11, '\"They\'ll talk to ya and talk to ya and talk to ya about individual freedom.  But they see a free individual, it\'s gonna scare \'em.\"  -- Easy Rider'),
(12, '\"The next woman takes me on\'s gonna light up like a pinball machine and pay off in silver dollars!\"  -- One Flew Over the Cuckoo\'s Nest'),
(13, '\"Never rub another man\'s rhubarb.\"  -- Batman'),
(14, '\"Strange women lying in ponds, distributing swords is no basis for a system of government!\"  -- Monty Python and the Holy Grail'),
(15, '\"Hell, I can get you a toe by 3 o\'clock this afternoon, <i>with</i> nail polish.\"  -- the Big Lebowski'),
(16, '\"The Dude abides.\"  -- the Big Lebowski'),
(17, '\"Oh no, Mrs. Robinson, I think you\'re the most attractive of all my parent\'s friends, I mean that.\"  -- the Graduate'),
(18, '\"Everybody\'s got \'Frampton Comes Alive\'.  If you lived in the suburbs you were issued it.  It came in the mail with samples of \'Tide\'.\"  -- Wayne\'s World 2'),
(19, '\"So there I am in Sri Lanka, formerly Ceylon, at about 3 o\'clock in the morning, looking for one thousand brown M&Ms to fill a brandy glass, or Ozzy wouldn\'t go on stage that night.\"  -- Wayne\'s World 2'),
(20, '\"Sleeping like this will add ten years to your life.  I learned it from Keith Richards when I toured with the Stones.  This may be the reason why Keith cannot be killed by conventional weapons.\"  -- Wayne\'s World 2'),
(21, '\"Ooh, I hated the Colonel, with his wee beady eyes.  \'Ooh, you\'re going to buy my chicken!\'\" -- So I Married An Axe Murderer'),
(22, '\"When I was insolent, I was placed in a burlap bag and beaten with reeds.  Pretty standard, really.\"  -- Dr. Evil, in Austin Powers'),
(23, '\"She should be good-looking, but we\'re willing to trade looks for a certain... \'morally casual\' attitude.\"  -- Animal House'),
(24, '\"The greatest trick the devil ever pulled was convincing the world he didn\'t exist.\"  -- the Usual Suspects'),
(25, '\"You know women, mate. Like monkeys, they are -- won\'t let go of one branch until they\'ve got hold of the next.\"  -- Mission Impossible 2'),
(26, '\"There is no spoon.\"  -- the Matrix'),
(27, '\"Never underestimate the power of denial.\"  -- American Beauty'),
(28, '\"We are such fans of your music and all of your records.  I\'m not speaking of yours personally, but the whole genre of the rock and roll.\"  -- This is Spinal Tap'),
(29, '\"\'The Statue of Liberty is kaput\' -- that\'s disconcerting.\"  -- Saving Private Ryan'),
(30, '\"It\'s only after you\'ve lost everything that you\'re free to do anything.\"  -- Fight Club'),
(31, '\"My rifle and myself are defenders of my country, we are the masters of my enemy, we are the saviors of my life.  So be it, until there is no enemy, but peace.  Amen.\" -- Full Metal Jacket'),
(32, '\"That movie has warped my fragile little mind.\"  -- South Park: Bigger, Longer, and Uncut'),
(33, '\"Just remember what the MPAA says: Horrific, deplorable violence is okay, as long as people don\'t say any naughty words!\"  -- South Park: Bigger, Longer, and Uncut'),
(34, '\"Do not have any attachments, do not have anything in your life you are not willing to walk out on in 30 seconds flat if you spot the heat around the corner.\" -- Heat'),
(35, '\"Adventure, excitement... a Jedi craves not these things.\"  -- the Empire Strikes Back'),
(36, '\"This city is about to face a disaster of biblical proportions.\"  -- Ghostbusters'),
(37, '\"Generally you don\'t see that kind of behavior in a major appliance.\"  -- Ghostbusters'),
(38, '\"Ray, when someone asks you if you\'re a god, you say YES!\"  -- Ghostbusters'),
(39, '\"Who\'s done more research than the good people at the American Tobacco Industry?  They say its harmless.  Why would they lie?  If you\'re dead, you can\'t smoke.\"  -- Kingpin'),
(40, '\"Well, what if there is no tomorrow? There wasn\'t one today.\"  -- Groundhog Day'),
(41, '\"I got to feeling like a machine, and that aint\' no way to feel.\"  -- Shaft'),
(42, '\"You don\'t know how lucky you are being a monkey.  Because consciousness is a terrible curse.\"  -- Being John Malkovich'),
(43, '\"It\'s a madhouse!  A MADHOUSE!\" -- Planet of the Apes'),
(44, '\"Get your stinking paws off me you damn dirty apes!\" -- Planet of the Apes'),
(45, '\"\'Who\'s trying to kill you, Mr. Donut-head Man?\' \'I don\'t know, but he better not.\'\" -- Wayne\'s World'),
(46, '\"Garth, marriage is punishment for shoplifting in some countries!\" -- Wayne\'s World'),
(47, '\"A gun rack?... a gun rack. I don\'t even own <u>a</u> gun, let alone many guns that would necessitate an entire rack. What am I gonna do... with a gun rack?\" -- Wayne\'s World'),
(48, '\"We\'re between lawyers right now. You see, our first lawyer screwed our affairs so badly.\" -- Wayne\'s World'),
(49, '\"Did you ever find Bugs Bunny attractive when he put on a dress and played a girl bunny?\" -- Wayne\'s World'),
(50, '\"No matter what anybody tells you, words and ideas can change the world.\" -- Dead Poets Society'),
(51, '\"Goddammit! I swear if you guys rip on me 13 or 14 more times... I\'m outta here!\" -- BASEketball'),
(52, '\"I\'m telling you, it\'s jobs.  First we get the jobs.  Then we get the khakis.  Then we get the chicks.\" -- BASEketball'),
(53, '\"I have to remind myself that some birds aren\'t meant to be caged.  Their feathers are just too bright.\" -- the Shawshank Redemption'),
(54, '\"We are the music makers.  We are the dreamers of dreams.\" -- Willie Wonka and the Chocolate Factory'),
(55, '\"I have nipples, Greg.  Can you milk me?\"  -- Meet the Parents'),
(56, '\"Never put passion before principle.\" -- The Karate Kid Part II'),
(57, '\"Life moves pretty fast.  If you don\'t stop and look around once in awhile, you could miss it.\" -- Ferris Bueller\'s Day Off'),
(58, '\"An eye for an eye just ends up leaving the whole world blind.\"  -- Gandhi'),
(59, '\"So, Lord Helmet, at last we meet again for the first time for the last time.\" -- Spaceballs'),
(60, '\"So, Lone Star, now you see that evil will always triumph because good is dumb.\" -- Spaceballs'),
(61, '\"Unfortunately there is one thing standing between me and that property - the rightful owners.\" -- Blazing Saddles'),
(62, '\"He\'s experienced about as much pain and suffering as anyone I\'ve encountered, give or take, and he still has Hell to look forward to.\" -- Se7en'),
(63, '\"Yeah, a landlord\'s dream: a paralyzed tenant with no tongue.\" \"Who pays the rent on time.\" -- Se7en'),
(64, '\"Ernest Hemingway once wrote, \'The world is a fine place and worth fighting for.\'  I agree with the second part.\" -- Se7en'),
(65, '\"...problems only get worse. Why is that? Because money\'s most powerful ability is to allow bad people to continue doing bad things at the expense of those who don\'t have it.\" -- Sneakers'),
(66, '\"You know I could have been in the NSA, but they found out my parents were married.\" -- Sneakers'),
(67, '\"Yeah I called her up, she gave me a bunch of crap about me not listening to her, or something, I don\'t know, I wasn\'t really paying attention.\" -- Dumb & Dumber'),
(68, '\"...if you was to put me with this here sniper rifle anywhere up to and including one mile from Adolf Hitler, with a clean line of sight... well, pack your bags, boys. War\'s over.\" -- Saving Private Ryan'),
(69, '\"It\'s like finding a needle in a stack of needles.\" -- Saving Private Ryan'),
(70, '\"Picture a girl who took a nose dive from the ugly tree and hit every branch on the way down.\" -- Saving Private Ryan'),
(71, '\"This is your life and it\'s ending one minute at a time.\" -- Fight Club'),
(72, '\"After fighting, everything else in your life has got the volume turned down.\" -- Fight Club'),
(73, '\"Welcome to the real world.\" -- The Matrix'),
(74, '\"You find out life\'s this game of inches, and so is football.\" -- Any Given Sunday'),
(75, '\"In any fight, it\'s the guy whose willing to die who\'s gonna win that inch, and I know that if I\'m going to have any life anymore it\'s because I\'m still willing to fight and die for that inch.\" -- Any Given Sunday'),
(76, '\"You can\'t handle the truth!\" -- A Few Good Men'),
(77, '\"Son, we live in a world that has walls. And those walls have to be guarded by men with guns.\" -- A Few Good Men'),
(78, '\"Because deep down, in places you don\'t talk about at parties, you want me on that wall. You need me on that wall.\" -- A Few Good Men'),
(79, '\"This field, this game, is a part of our past, Ray. It reminds us of all that once was good, and that could be again. Oh people will come, Ray. People will most definitely come.\"  -- Field of Dreams'),
(80, '\"For justice, we must go to Don Corleone.\" -- The Godfather'),
(81, '\"We\'re going to cut out their living guts and use them to grease the treads of our tanks.\" -- Patton'),
(82, '\"If I were the man I was five years ago I\'d take a flame-thrower to this place.\" -- Scent of a Woman'),
(83, '\"Remember, Red: Hope is a good thing, maybe the best of things and no good thing ever dies. I will be hoping that this letter finds you, and finds you well. Your friend, Andy.\"  -- the Shawshank Redemption'),
(84, '\"I\'m so excited I can barely sit still or hold a thought in my head.  I think it\'s the excitement only a free man can feel. A free man at the start of a long journey whose conclusion is uncertain.\" -- Shawshank Redemption'),
(85, '\"The point is, ladies and gentleman, is that Greed, for lack of a better word, is good. Greed is right. Greed works. Greed clarifies, cuts through and captures the essence of the evolutionary spirit.\" -- Wall Street'),
(86, '\"Look, look at it this way, you know uh, a man, a man takes a job, you know, and that job, I mean like that, and that it becomes what he is. You know like uh, you do a thing and that\'s what you are.\" -- Taxi Driver'),
(87, '\"I don’t like boats.\" -- Carlito’s Way'),
(88, '\"Back off man. I’m a scientist!\" -- Ghostbusters'),
(89, '\"If the ionization rate is constant for all ectoplasmic entities, we could really bust some heads... in a spiritual sense, of course.\" -- Ghostbusters'),
(90, '\"Strange game--the only winning move is not to play\" Joshua, WarGames'),
(91, '\"I am not Mr. Lebowski. <i>You’re</i> Mr. Lebowski. I’m The Dude. So that’s what you call me. You know, that, or His Dudeness, or Duder, or El Duderino, if you’re not into the whole brevity thing.\" -- The Big Lebowski'),
(92, '\"I <U>do</U> mind. The Dude minds. This will not stand. This aggression will not stand, man.\" -- The Big Lebowski'),
(93, '\"I can’t believe you loan me your car without telling me it had a blind spot!\" -- Biff Tannen, Back to the Future'),
(94, '\"Real life doesn\'t fit into little boxes that were drawn for it.\" -- Unbreakable'),
(95, '\"Kids today are amazing. I played winter ball down in Venezuela, they had kids half his age, every one of them speaking Spanish, and that\'s a hard language.\" -- Little Big League'),
(96, '\"Do you know how hard it is to manage?\" \"It\'s the American League, they got the DH, how hard could it be?\" -- Little Big League'),
(97, '\"Well, that sounds like a pretty good deal. But I think I may have a better one. How about, I give you the finger... and you give me my phone call.\" -- The Matrix'),
(98, '\"The Force is what gives a Jedi his power. It\'s an energy field created by all living things. It surrounds us and penetrates us. It binds the galaxy together.\" -- Star Wars: A New Hope'),
(99, '\"Who\'s the more foolish, the fool, or the fool who follows him?\" -- Star Wars: A New Hope'),
(100, '\"I\'m a firm believer in the philosophy of a ruling class, especially since I rule.\" -- Clerks'),
(101, '\"Yeah, I mean aside from the cheating, we were a great couple. I mean that\'s what high school was about, algebra, bad lunch, and infidelity.\" -- Clerks'),
(102, '\"Wait a minute, what is that anyway, something like 36? Does that include me?\" \"Ummm...37.\" \"I\'m 37?!\" -- Clerks'),
(103, '\"The first boy I ever kissed ended up in a coma for three weeks. I can still feel him inside my head.\" -- X-Men'),
(104, '\"If you think that Mick Jagger will still be doing the whole rock star thing at age fifty, well then you are sorely, sorely mistaken.\" -- Almost Famous'),
(105, '\"Don\'t let those swill merchants rewrite you.\" -- Almost Famous'),
(106, '\"I didn\'t invent the rainy day. I just own the best umbrella.\" -- Almost Famous');
INSERT INTO `Response` (`ID_`, `Response_date`, `AUTHOR_ID_FK`, `ARTICLE_ID_FK`, `TOP5_ID_FK`, `Text`) VALUES
(1, '2001-06-29', 3, 0, 15, 'Dude, you forgot about &quot;Life is Beautiful&quot;.  Certainly that has to rank somewhere above one or more Jackie Chan movies....'),
(12, '2001-06-29', 1, 5, 0, 'I think I\'m gonna be sick...'),
(13, '2001-06-30', 3, 5, 0, 'I can\'t believe this high rating.  Now anything decent has to be rated at least a 9 just to compete.  In one fell swoop, Nick has managed to emasculate himself AND subvert the entire ratings system as we know it.  I hope you\'re happy....'),
(15, '2001-06-30', 3, 0, 16, 'All this from a man who had a poster of Starship Troopers on his wall....'),
(14, '2001-06-30', 6, 0, 13, 'Very creative top 5 list, dude.  Also, I have to give you props on the Professional; it was actually a pretty cool flick.'),
(16, '2001-07-01', 6, 6, 0, 'Great review, dude.  But did anyone else notice how big John Travola\'s head looked?!  Geez, I haven\'t seen a head that big on film since &quot;Mask&quot;.'),
(17, '2001-07-02', 3, 0, 17, 'No \'Any Given Sunday\' ?'),
(18, '2001-07-03', 3, 0, 18, 'How about a \'Top 5 Hitchcock Cameos\', highlighting his best appearances in his own films?'),
(19, '2001-07-04', 3, 0, 21, 'How in blue HELL could you have left out the &quot;Scream 2&quot;/Bob Fish debacle??  The events surrounding that movie got us pulled over by the cops, for cryin\' out loud!  Such a gross oversight is inexcusable...'),
(20, '2001-07-06', 6, 7, 0, 'Sounds like someone Woods would like.'),
(22, '2001-07-07', 1, 9, 0, 'Hmm...  homosexual encounters and male nudity?  Sounds like a &quot;Woods Movie&quot; to me...'),
(23, '2001-07-11', 4, 0, 23, 'uh, ever hear of a little movie called &quot;Scarface&quot;? It should be either here or on the worst list--a glaring omission, dearest Bruce.'),
(24, '2001-07-11', 4, 0, 24, 'Once again, I must disagree.  &quot;And Justice for All&quot; and &quot;Sea of Love&quot; are hands down the worst Pacino movies...'),
(25, '2001-07-11', 4, 0, 18, 'A rather Grace Kelly-ocentric list, don\'t you think? (Brian\'s favorite actress is Grace Kelly according to his bio.  A fantastical coincidence, I think not).'),
(26, '2001-07-11', 4, 7, 0, 'Both &quot;serial mom&quot; and &quot;cry baby&quot; are outstanding movies! I especially liked (the formerly huge) Rikki Lake\'s stellar performance in &quot;cry baby.&quot;'),
(27, '2001-07-11', 8, 0, 24, 'While Heat has its flaws, it certainly is not one of his worst movies.  The dynamic exchanges between he and Robert DeNiro alone make it worth seeing.  Also, it has one of the best gunfight scenes in cinema history.      '),
(29, '2001-07-12', 3, 0, 27, 'Personally, I would have combined the two &quot;Back to the Future&quot; movies so I could include &quot;Bill and Ted\'s Excellent Adventure&quot; in the list.'),
(30, '2001-07-13', 3, 0, 28, 'Don\'t forget another killa with a significant other:  Frankenstein.  But I guess that\'s TOO old-school...'),
(31, '2001-07-13', 4, 0, 27, 'My guess as to the lyrics is &quot;Dream Theatre.&quot;  As for your list, the most glaring omission is &quot;Back to the Future III&quot; because of the movie\'s mishap giving birth to the &quot;Two Dolorian Theory.&quot;'),
(32, '2001-07-13', 8, 0, 27, 'What about &quot;Dark City&quot;?'),
(33, '2001-07-15', 1, 0, 27, 'Remember those wacky sunglasses that you could get at Pizza Hut as a Back to the Future tie-in?  Those were awesome!  I wish I still had mine...'),
(34, '2001-07-15', 6, 11, 0, 'Wow an 8.5!  I guess the next movie I see is a toss-up between The Score and My Dog Skip.'),
(35, '2001-07-18', 4, 0, 29, 'Great list!! How about James Brown in Rocky IV? '),
(36, '2001-07-19', 7, 0, 29, 'How could you overlook two biggies? Crossroads- Steve Vai playing the devil\'s musician Jack Butler, and Back to the Future 1- Huey Lewis playing the band auditioner.'),
(37, '2001-07-19', 7, 0, 16, 'Thin Red Line, worst film ever. C\'mon that\'s just flat out extreme. Joe vs. the Volcano should have been in there son.'),
(38, '2001-07-19', 6, 0, 29, 'Brian, I haven\'t seen Crossroads (sorry), but Huey Lewis\' part in Back to the Future, while cool, was so small, you could blink and miss it.'),
(39, '2001-07-19', 6, 0, 16, 'I stand by my statement that A Thin Red Line is the worst movie ever.  Believe me I wanted to put Boxing Helena or Lost Highway in that spot, but I hadn\'t seen many dark, strange, freaky movies like those with which to compare.  However, as far as war movies?  To compare to ATRL I\'d seen Saving Private Ryan.  Nuff said.'),
(40, '2001-07-20', 4, 0, 30, 'Meet Joe Black is a solid choice even though you only honorably mentioned it'),
(41, '2001-07-21', 8, 0, 32, '&quot;Pearl Harbor&quot; should be ranked.  It was as good as the movie was bad.'),
(42, '2001-07-23', 1, 0, 33, 'How can you leave out the original &quot;Planet of the Apes&quot;?  The all time best ending...  A true miscarriage of justice...'),
(43, '2001-07-25', 8, 0, 33, '&quot;Heat&quot; deserves some mention for the final scene where Pacino comforts DeNiro as he\'s dying.  Truly epic stuff.'),
(51, '2001-07-26', 4, 0, 33, 'Ryan, In response to your response, Heat deserves to be placed on the worst ending list.  Everyone knows that the right ending would have been DeNiro taking Pacino down.'),
(50, '2001-07-26', 4, 15, 0, 'Who\'s going to play Scooby? I don\'t know of any talking dogs who could handle the part...'),
(52, '2001-07-26', 4, 0, 35, 'Hey, copycat, you didn\'t think &quot;Primal Fear&quot; was shocking?'),
(53, '2001-07-26', 3, 15, 0, 'Geez, Nick, I figured you\'d suggest the doggy from &quot;My Dog Skip&quot;...'),
(54, '2001-07-26', 3, 0, 35, 'No, I didn\'t see &quot;Primal Fear&quot;.  I did see &quot;Fear&quot;, though.  Mmmm.... Alyssa Milano....'),
(55, '2001-07-29', 3, 0, 35, 'The new &quot;Planet of the Apes&quot; is a strong contender for this category as well, partly for the very end, and partly for its explanation of the planet\'s creation.'),
(56, '2001-07-30', 4, 0, 37, 'Time out!! Corey Haim in &quot;License to Drive&quot; put on an Oscar-caliber performance'),
(57, '2001-08-01', 6, 0, 37, 'Hey, what about Mark Hamill in anything but Star Wars?'),
(58, '2001-08-04', 6, 19, 0, 'I hope the ending isn\'t anything like another Marky-Mark film, &quot;Boogie Nights&quot; eeewww'),
(59, '2001-08-08', 3, 21, 0, 'How could Faraday have been rational AND paranoid?  The plot couldn\'t advance any other way.  What else needs explained?  Nothing, that\'s what.  The movie as a whole may have a couple flaws, but the ending is top notch.'),
(60, '2001-08-10', 3, 0, 38, 'No &quot;Dances with Wolves&quot;??'),
(61, '2001-08-14', 3, 23, 0, 'I liked this better the first time, when it starred Dana Carvey and was called &quot;Clean Slate&quot;.'),
(62, '2001-08-14', 4, 21, 0, 'I concur-- a most irrational ending that would only be plausible upon the occurrence of pigs flying...'),
(63, '2001-08-27', 3, 0, 39, 'Yes, Supe 4 was bad, way too preachy.  That\'s what you get for letting Christopher Reeve write the screenplay.  But how could you forget &quot;From Dusk Til Dawn 2&quot;, or &quot;Blues Brothers 2000&quot;?  The latter not only sucked balls, but also diminished the public memory of the original.  Plus it had NO Belushis in it.  What a rip.'),
(64, '2001-09-03', 3, 0, 40, 'How could you include &quot;Thirteen Days&quot; with all its &quot;creative liberties&quot;, but leave off &quot;Tora! Tora! Tora!&quot;, which was created with the cooperation of American and Japanese historians, and is dead accurate in practically every word of dialogue?  It seems Mr. Bruce has not done his homework...'),
(65, '2001-09-04', 1, 0, 43, 'WHAT?!?  Tom Selleck only number 4?!?  Bruce, you just may be the stupidest man on Earth...  Selleck\'s mustache was WAY more talented than the whole Peter Sellers.  Pathetic lack of respect for the greatest facial hair ever.'),
(66, '2001-11-16', 3, 0, 55, 'This list is a sham!  You have blatantly disregarded such great Christmas movies as &quot;Die Hard&quot; and &quot;Gremlins&quot;.  Sure, those are violent movies, but they\'re heartwarmingly violent.  This discrimination must be corrected at once.  I did like &quot;Trapped in Paradise&quot; though.  Dana Carvey is a stitch.'),
(67, '2001-11-28', 3, 0, 58, 'No, no, NO!!  &quot;One Flew Over the Cuckoo\'s Nest&quot; is hands-down the greatest film with Christopher Lloyd.  You suck, Bruce.'),
(68, '2001-12-13', 3, 34, 0, 'The Hulkster was also in that really bad TV series called &quot;Thunder in Paradise&quot;.  I think it was on TNT, or TBS, or one of those crap Turner networks.  It was like &quot;Knight Rider&quot;, except with a speedboat instead of a Trans Am.  It was hella-lame.'),
(69, '2002-01-02', 3, 37, 0, 'Too friggin\' long?  Shyeah, that\'s what SHE said!!'),
(70, '2002-01-02', 3, 0, 61, 'Dude, you forgot about &quot;Coyote Ugly&quot;.  That was clearly his best.  Heh, just kidding.  That movie sucked my sack.'),
(71, '2002-04-03', 3, 48, 0, 'Mmmmmm.... Jennifer Love Hewitt.....'),
(72, '2002-05-03', 1, 0, 61, 'What the hell?  No &quot;King Ralph&quot;?  Come on now...'),
(73, '2002-07-15', 3, 57, 0, 'Actually, I really liked this film.  I thought it was pretty solid, and very creepy.  And with scenes filmed in and around Pittsburgh, it gets bonus points.  Not that that helped &quot;Sudden Death&quot;, though...'),
(74, '2002-07-16', 3, 61, 0, 'Ever notice how every single movie starring Freddie Prinze Jr. (nice name... NOT) also stars Matthew Lillard?  It\'s really frickin\' sad.  C\'mon, Lilly.  Are you gonna be Freddie\'s monkey boy the rest of your life?  Freddie is no one\'s friend.  If Freddie were an ice cream flavor, he\'d be &quot;Pralines and Dick&quot;.'),
(75, '2002-07-16', 4, 0, 62, 'I love &quot;What About Bob?&quot;, but to not rank &quot;Caddyshack&quot; at #1 is just plain wrong. Bill Murray\'s performace in Caddyshack essentially defined his career. Shame! Shame!'),
(80, '2004-07-13', 6, 66, 0, 'I guess with <i>My Dog Skip</i> getting an 8.5 (thanks Ranj), you\'d have to give <i>Anchorman</i> at least a 9 even if you hated the flick.  Hmm... Muniz vs. Ferrell... need I say more?'),
(79, '2004-07-09', 1, 0, 65, 'Wait, wait, wait...  No '),
(81, '2004-07-13', 6, 33, 0, 'I like your point citing JB as a cult favorite.  It started from the beginning where Jack Black and Kyle Gass (Tenacious D) first appeared on the small screen on the cult smash <i>Mr. Show with Bob and David</i>.');
INSERT INTO `Top5` (`ID_`, `Author_ID_FK`, `Title`, `Item1`, `Item2`, `Item3`, `Item4`, `Item5`, `Article_date`, `Intro`, `Image`, `Caption`, `Image_fullsize`, `QA`, `Type`) VALUES
(1, 3, 'Top 5 Movies That Keep On Giving', 'the Blues Brothers', 'the Big Lebowski', 'Wayne\'s World', 'Animal House', 'South Park: Bigger, Longer, and Uncut', '2001-06-19', 'There are many movies you can watch a few times, and then they lose their charm.\r\nAnd then there are those special few that offer something new every time you see them,\r\nsomething different you didn\'t quite catch the first 10 times you watched.<P>\r\nHere are the Top 5 movies that offer great things to those who wait:', '<img src=\'images/blues_bros.jpg\' width=\'158\' height=\'240\'>', NULL, NULL, 1, 'top5'),
(2, 3, 'Top 5 Best Uses of Narration', 'GoodFellas', 'the Shawshank Redemption', 'Fight Club', 'A Bronx Tale', 'American Beauty', '2001-06-20', 'It may seem a bit juvenile, but sometimes there\'s just no better way\r\nto tell a story than through a narrator or some character that talks\r\nto the camera.  This list actually turned out to be much more difficult\r\nthan I anticipated, because of the vast number of excellent movies that\r\nuse narration.  By resolving to later revisit comedies in a category of\r\ntheir own, the task became marginally easier.\r\n<P>\r\nSo here are the best uses of narration in a non-comedy:', '<img src=\'images/goodfellas.jpg\' height=\'175\' width=\'112\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(3, 4, 'Top 5 Ralph Macchio Films', 'The Karate Kid, Part I', 'My Cousin Vinny', 'The Outsiders', 'The Karate Kid, Part II', 'Crossroads', '2001-06-21', '', '<img src=\'images/macchio.jpg\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(4, 1, 'Top 5 Movies with the Word \"Poo\" in the Title', 'Fernando Poo', 'Poo Lom', 'Poovae Poo Chooda Vaa', 'John Henry and the Inky-Poo', 'Samanthi Poo', '2001-06-22', '', '', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(5, 1, 'Top 5 Movie Soundtracks', 'Superfly', 'The Blues Brothers', 'Reservoir Dogs', 'Wayne\'s World', 'She\'s the One', '2001-06-22', 'We all know the elements that make up a truly great movie: character\r\ndevelopment, a great plot, realistic dialogue, awesome special effects,\r\netc. -- but sometimes a movie comes along where its soundtrack is the\r\npiece of the puzzle that puts it over the top.  So, I bring you the top 5\r\nexcellent movie soundtracks...\r\n', '<img src=\'images/superfly.jpg\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(6, 2, 'Top 5 Mafia Movies of All Time', 'The Godfather: Part II', 'Goodfellas', 'Angels With Dirty Faces', 'The Godfather', 'Donnie Brasco', '2001-06-24', '', '<img src=\'images/godfather2.jpg\' height=\'190\' width=\'104\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(7, 2, 'Top 5 Scenes of Movies to which Chris Larson has said, \"No way. Cool!\"', 'South Park: Bigger, Longer and Uncut <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Eric Cartman begins to shake and then begins to emit bolts of lightning\r\nfrom his finger tips and shocks Saddam Hussein.</div>\r\n', 'Fear and Loathing in Las Vegas <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Johnny Depp hits Benicio Del Toro in the head with an orange</div>', 'The Matrix <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Keanu Reeves enters the body of a bad guy then makes the bad guy explode</div>', 'Pulp Fiction <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Samuel L. Jackson shoots a guy on a couch and then delivers the line, \"Oh I\'m sorry, did I break your concentration?\"</div>', 'Mallrats <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Jason Lee gets hit in the head with a ladder</div>', '2001-06-24', '', '<img src=\'images/southpark.jpg\' height=\'190\' width=\'134\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(8, 2, 'Top 5 Independent Films with a Budget Under $30,000', 'Spanking the Monkey <div class=\'bodyText\'>(I\'m not exactly sure how much this movie cost to \r\nmake, but it couldn\'t have been that expensive and the title was pretty \r\nfunny)</div>', 'Reservoir Dogs <div class=\'bodyText\'>(the original was shot in black & white and the cast was \r\nmade up of Quentin Tarantino\'s friends)</div>', 'Slacker', 'Clerks', 'Night of the Living Dead', '2001-06-24', '', '<img src=\'images/spanking.jpg\' height=\'190\' width=\'104\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(9, 2, 'Top 5 Movies with Pro Wrestlers Playing Non-Wrestling Roles', 'The Princess Bride <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Andre The Giant</div>', 'The Mummy Returns <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Dwayne \"The Rock\" Johnson</div>', 'Suburban Commando <div class=\'bodyText\'>- \"Mean\" Mark Callous a.k.a. The Undertaker</div>', 'Jingle All the Way <div class=\'bodyText\'>- Paul \"The Big Show\" Wight</div>', 'The Bad Pack <div class=\'bodyText\'>- \"Rowdy\" Roddy Piper</div>', '2001-06-24', '', '<img src=\'images/princess.jpg\' height=\'190\' width=\'127\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(10, 4, 'Top 5 Mafia Movies of All Time', 'Godfather II', 'Godfather I', 'Goodfellas', 'A Bronx Tale', 'Analyze This\r\n', '2001-06-25', '', '<img src=\'images/godfather2.jpg\' height=\'190\' width=\'104\'>', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(11, 4, 'Top 5 Sports Movies', 'Breaking Away\r\n', 'Hoosiers', 'Rudy', 'The Natural\r\n', 'Little Big League', '2001-06-25', '', '', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(12, 8, 'Top 5 Rocky Movies', 'Rocky IV', 'Rocky III', 'Rocky', 'Rocky II', 'Rocky V', '2001-06-27', 'Since there are only 5 Rocky movies, each one must make this list by \r\ndefault.  The point of producing such an otherwise useless list then, is to \r\nidentify the best and worst of the Rocky movies.  As a younger generation \r\nRocky fan, I bring unique perspectives to the saga that serve to create a \r\nsomewhat controversial ranking.  \r\n<P>\r\nFirst, Rocky III and Rocky IV came out when \r\nI was at a very impressionable age.  Therefore, I grew up with Rocky and \r\nApollo on the same side, making it difficult for me to relate to the earlier \r\nRocky movies that focused on their rivalry.  Second, Rocky III is elevated \r\nin my mind by the presence of two of my childhood heroes, Hulk Hogan and Mr. \r\nT.  Third, as an unabashed Reaganite, I find the dramatization of the \"evil \r\nempire\" theme in Rocky IV irresistible.  These insights provide some \r\nbackground on my choices.', '<img src=\'images/rocky.jpg\' width=\'127\' height=\'198\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, '', 1, 'top5'),
(13, 7, 'Top 5 TopFive All-Hype Films', 'The Untouchables <div class=\'bodyText\'> - Ryan</div>', 'Boxing Helena <div class=\'bodyText\'> - Woods</div>', 'The Professional <div class=\'bodyText\'> - Brian</div>', 'FX 2 <div class=\'bodyText\'> - Brian</div>', 'Dark City <div class=\'bodyText\'> - Brian</div>', '2001-06-27', 'We have all been there. That annoying pest of a friend who nags all the time about having to see a certain movie. Finally you give him his day in the sun. You sit down with snacks and soda eager to view this film. You realize that the 2 hours could have been better spent elsewhere. This is in honor of those moments where a film was less than inspiring. I admit, I predominantly occupy this list...  but it is unanimously accepted TopFive folklore that the all-time movie-pick blunder goes to Cuffy. I have included the guilty party for all to see. \r\n', '<img src=\'images/untouchables.jpg\' width=\'149\' height=\'212\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(14, 6, 'Top 5 Historically Based Films', 'Schindler\'s List', 'Saving Private Ryan', 'Ben-Hur', 'Amistad', 'The 10 Commandments', '2001-06-29', 'First off, is there any doubt that Steven Spielberg is the greatest\r\ndirector of our time?  But that\'s another top 5 category.  Schindler\'s\r\nList has to be the most powerful, and accurate, for that matter,\r\nportrayal of an historical event I\'ve ever seen on film.  The thing\r\nthat separates it from the rest, is the amount of scenes that are just\r\nplain hard to watch.  I wouldn\'t wish some of the things in that movie\r\non my worst enemy, Woods.  Saving Private Ryan is also very\r\naccurate, it\'s biggest strength being the sense of patriotism it\r\nimparts upon every American who finishes watching it.  Another\r\nSpielfilm as well as two biblical epics finish out this list of\r\ntrue-life tales.', '<img src=\'images/schindlerslist.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(15, 6, 'Top 5 Foreign Films', 'Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon', 'Godzilla', 'El Mariachi', 'Zui Quan', 'Fei Lung Maang Jeung', '2001-06-29', 'All of these are good films, even if they happen to be foreign, well,\r\ntechnically, El Mariachi was filmed in Texas [I think], but it was in\r\nSpanish so I say it counts... but I digress.  I\'ve comprised this list\r\nof movies that could each be separated into their own categories. \r\nCrouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon:  Best Fight Scenes; Godzilla: \r\nCheesiest Fight Scenes; El Mariachi: Best Independent Film by a\r\nMexican-American for Under 7 Grand; and Zui Quan & Fei Lung Maang\r\nJeung: Best Fight Scenes/ Funniest Dubbed English in a Jackie Chan\r\nMovie.  Note:  For all you illiterate, English-speakers out there who\r\naren\'t reading this right now, you may want to stray away from 3, because they are in subtitles.', '<img src=\'images/crouchingtiger.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', 'Mandarin Chinese never tasted so good.', '', 1, 'top5'),
(16, 6, 'Top 5 Worst Films', 'The Thin Red Line', 'Boxing Helena', 'Lost Highway', 'Titanic', 'Congo', '2001-06-29', 'Here it is, folks... the worst films of all time.  Of course this is\r\nonly my own personal opinion, but, let\'s be honest, what else matters? \r\nI was swindled into seeing the first three films by my college buds\r\n(Brian:  The Thin Red Line and Lost Highway & Woods:  Boxing Helena -\r\njust for the record).  Somehow I felt dummer, yes dummer, after\r\nwatching these pieces of cinematic crap.  I was then later tricked into\r\nseeing the cash-cow, Titanic, by my so called \"friend\" Dave out here in\r\nCali.  (In his defense, however, he thought they would actually have a\r\nhistorical telling of the ship, not a fictional account of three\r\npassengers--go figure).  To round out the list, Congo, a monkey movie\r\nwith virtually no plot, just didn\'t cut it as Michael Crichton\'s much\r\nawaited book-to-movie project follow-up to Jurassic Park.', '<img src=\'images/thinredline.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', 'A thin line between loathe and hate', '', 1, 'top5'),
(17, 8, 'Top 5 Sports Movies', 'Hoosiers', 'Field of Dreams\r\n', 'Remember the Titans\r\n', 'Rudy\r\n', 'Rocky IV\r\n', '2001-07-02', 'For those of us who have been less than successful in the world of real \r\nathletics, the movies offer the perfect refuge.  For two hours we live \r\nvicariously through the silver screen; overcoming obstacles, beating the \r\nodds, and defeating formidable foes armed only with a Coke and popcorn.  And \r\nwe take it all in with a quiet confidence, knowing that in the end, the good \r\nguys will win, because its in the script.  So for those who want to feel \r\nthat jolt of adrenaline and thrill of victory with no physical exertion, \r\nhere are my top five sports movies.', '<img src=\'images/hoosiers.jpg\' width=\'144\' height=\'214\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(18, 7, 'Top 5 Hitchcock Movies', 'Psycho <font class=\'bodyText\'>(1960)</font>', 'North by Northwest <font class=\'bodyText\'>(1959)</font>', 'Rear Window <font class=\'bodyText\'>(1954)</font>', 'Dial M for Murder <font class=\'bodyText\'>(1954)</font>', 'Shadow of a Doubt <font class=\'bodyText\'>(1943)</font>', '2001-07-03', 'Compiling a Top 5 Hitchcocks causes me great pain. The man simply makes too many good ones. My favorite of his films generally come from the 1950s but each decade contains its jewels. Here you\'ll find no shortage of innovative shot-making, classy acting, tasty plots, masterful scores, and good ol\' fashion suspense.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/psycho.jpg\' width=\'166\' height=\'221\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(19, 7, 'Top 5 War Movies', 'The Thin Red Line', 'Saving Private Ryan', 'The Dirty Dozen', 'Apocalypse Now', 'Platoon', '2001-07-03', 'There are many quality war movies out there. For many it is the realism factor that defines a good war movie. My top selection touched on the psychological aspect better than any, though. Many will debate this and I will address in a future article. Platoon gets on this list just because of the sad and beautiful Adagio for Strings alone. And Apocalypse Now is just flat twisted.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/thinredline.jpg\' width=\'77\' height=\'140\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(20, 7, 'Top 5 Movie Tear-Jerkers', 'The Green Mile\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- tumor curing scene, execution at the end (I never faced a challenge quite like that one, it simply takes the cake)</div>', 'Rudy\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- The sack at the end of the game when his parents and brother are in the stands. . . being carried off the field </div>', 'Contact\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Jodie Foster meets her father again in some other dimension </div>', 'Carlito\'s Way\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- after Benny Blanco from the Bronx guns him down </div>', 'The Shawshank Redemption\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Brooks was here, the scene when Red finally gets out and finds the box hidden by Andy </div>', '2001-07-03', 'Probably the closest I\'ve come to being driven to tears occurred during these movies. I want to clarify the scenes that were responsible. Of course, being a guy, I had to fight them off hard. Without doubt, <i>The Green Mile</i> was the toughest effort. Just about every woman in the movie theater was sobbing throughout the film. Boy was my bottom lip sore after that one. Honorable mention: My Girl (death of Thomas J.).\r\n', '<img src=\'images/green_mile.jpg\' width=\'142\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(23, 2, 'Top 5 Best Al Pacino Movies', 'Dog Day Afternoon', 'Godfather: Part II', 'Scent of a Woman', 'Donnie Brasco <div class=\'bodyText\'>-- I almost put this one at number one for the same reason <i>Dog Day Afternoon</i> is number one.</div>\r\n', 'Godfather', '2001-07-04', 'Now before I start this, I know I\'ll suffer the slings and arrows of not \r\npicking the obvious choice for number one by placing it second and the other \r\nobvious number one by placing it third.  In my opinion <i>Dog Day Afternoon</i> is \r\ntotally over looked as his best film.  No, it wasn\'t an epic like <i>Godfather \r\nPart II</i> or did Pacino play an eccentric military man like <i>Scent of a Woman</i>.  \r\nInstead Pacino played a common man who was put under extraordinary \r\ncircumstances.', '<img src=\'images/dog_day.jpg\' width=\'163\' height=\'238\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(24, 2, 'Top 5 Worst Al Pacino Movies', 'Dick Tracy', 'Heat', 'Frankie and Johnny', 'City Hall', 'Devil\'s Advocate', '2001-07-04', 'For as good an actor as the man is, he\'s still come up with quite a few \r\nstinkers.  These films weren\'t his fault, but he\'s still guilty by \r\nassociation.', '<img src=\'images/dick.jpg\' width=\'132\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(25, 3, 'Top 5 Directors', 'Alfred Hitchcock', 'Stanley Kubrick', 'Steven Spielberg', 'Francis Ford Coppola', 'Martin Scorsese', '2001-07-05', 'This list was extremely difficult due to the vast number of high-caliber directors out there.  It could very easily have been\r\na Top Ten list, but the name of this site is Top FIVE Movies, so I guess I\'ve got to limit my choices.  <P>That being said, I still\r\nhave to dole out a few honorable mentions to David Lynch, John Landis, Oliver Stone, and M. Night Shyamalan, who will probably \r\nbreak into the Top 5 soon if his next few films display the same brilliance that the first couple have hinted at.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/hitchcock.jpg\' width=\'154\' height=\'200\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(26, 3, 'Top 5 Movie Tear-Jerkers', 'Death of a Salesman<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- The 1985 version, w/ Dustin Hoffman and John Malkovich.  This is the only one that actually broke me, for personal reasons I won\'t get into here.</div>', 'The Green Mile', 'Awakenings', 'Life is Beautiful', 'The Straight Story', '2001-07-07', '\"Do my tears surprise you?  Strong men also cry... <i>strong</i> men also cry.\"<BR><div align=\'right\' class=\'bodyText\'>-- <i>the Big Lebowski</i></div>\r\n<P>\r\nDespite the tough, manly exterior we all project, every one of us has one or two buttons that, when\r\npressed, somehow open up a tunnel into the pit of our being and expose some small kernel of emotion.\r\nOftentimes we are able to recover at the last possible moment, but sometimes, not.  These are the films\r\nthat try men\'s souls...\r\n', '<img src=\'images/death_salesman.jpg\' width=\'130\' height=\'206\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(27, 7, 'Top 5 Time Concepts', 'Back to the Future ', 'Back to the Future II', '12 Monkeys ', 'Frequency', 'Time Cop', '2001-07-12', '<I>So you run and you run to catch up with the sun but it\'s sinking <br>\r\nRacing around to come up behind you again <br>\r\nThe sun is the same in a relative way but you\'re older <br>\r\nShorter of breath and one day closer to death.</I> <br>\r\n<p>\r\nThe origin of this lyric comprises the gyst of this list. Where is it from?  BE HONEST, NO INTERNET. ', '<img src=\'images/back_to_future.jpg\' width=\'144\' height=\'217\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(28, 7, 'Top 5 Old-School Killas', 'Leatherface\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>--only cannibal of the bunch, very low IQ, maintains strong familial ties, non-athlete, main weapon: chainsaw</div>\r\n', 'Jason Vorhees\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- most resilient, most persistent, most menacing, main weapon: machete</div>\r\n', 'Michael Myers\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- prefers to strike on Halloween only, slickest dresser, best mask, main weapon: butcher knife</div>\r\n', 'Freddy Krueger\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- most witty, nastiest looking, best origin story, raised in single-mom household, main weapon: brown glove w/ shears </div>\r\n', 'Pinhead\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- most enigmatic, most cerebral, only one with significant other, main weapon: doesn\'t seem to prefer hand held tools and such</div>\r\n', '2001-07-13', 'There is nothing quite like a horror movie killer debate. Who\'s the strongest?  Most vicious? Shows the most mercy? Can Michael Myers beat up Jason? Does Pinhead seem a shade snobby?<P>Countless hours were spent with neighborhood kids breaking these homicidal freaks down- their strengths and weaknesses. Each with his unique personality and method but bound by a common thread: the ability to persevere despite adversity. These guys have been stabbed in the head, blown up, run over, and drowned, yet they manage to pick up where they left off each time. Props especially to Jason who made it through 8 movies.<P>This list is more of the informative nature as the other staffers, as far as I know, have little or no knowledge of this genre because they are too scared to watch.', '<img src=\'images/texas_chainsaw.jpg\' width=\'148\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(29, 6, 'Top 5 Cameos by a Musician', 'Alice Cooper\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>Wayne\'s World</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-as himself</div>\r\n\r\n', 'Flea\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>Back to the Future II and III </div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-as Douglas J. Needles</div>\r\n', 'James Brown\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>The Blues Brothers</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-as the Reverend Cleophus James</div>\r\n', 'KISS\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>Detroit Rock City</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-Gene Simmons, Paul Stanley, Ace Frehley, and Peter Criss: as\r\nthemselves</div>\r\n', 'Ozzy Osbourne\r\n<div class=\'quote\'>Little Nicky</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>-as himself</div>\r\n', '2001-07-17', 'Many times artists in the musical arena will see if they can replicate\r\ntheir success on the silver screen.  That being said, most of the time,\r\nthey play themselves, and it\'s a less than starring role.  Regardless,\r\nhere are some of the best cameos by musicians that I\'ve seen.', '<img src=\'images/alice_cooper.jpg\' width=\'149\' height=\'196\'>', 'We\'re not worthy!!!', '', 1, 'top5'),
(30, 8, 'Top 5 Movies You Like But Don\'t Want to Admit', 'Little Women<div class=\'bodyText\'>- a sweeping romantic epic, irresistable.</div>', 'Miss Congeniality<div class=\'bodyText\'>- A must-see starring America\'s Sweetheart and my \r\nfavorite actress, Sandra Bullock.</div>\r\n', 'Wedding Planner<div class=\'bodyText\'>- J. Lo.  Enough said.</div>', 'Trial and Error<div class=\'bodyText\'>- A standard romantic comedy livened up by the antics of \r\nMichael Richards.</div>\r\n', 'Romy & Michele\'s High School Reunion<div class=\'bodyText\'>- I honestly have no real \r\njustification for liking this movie, but I do.</div>\r\n<div class=\'headline\'>Honorable Mention: Meet Joe Black</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>- It was a long strange night at TopFive.  We wandered into the \r\nmovie store looking for an action flick.  It was Saturday night and the \r\nstore was picked over pretty good.  We panicked, lost our heads, and came \r\nout with this.</div>\r\n', '2001-07-19', 'OK.  Admit it.  You\'ve all been dragged to the theater or the video store by \r\nyour girlfriend to see another infamous chick flick.  You settle in, hoping \r\nto sneak a nap, when all of the sudden, out of the blue, a strange feminine \r\nforce seizes you.  You are helpless as your eyes gravitate to the screen, \r\nyour brain begins to sort out a rudimentary plot, you even begin laughing at \r\njokes, and then it hits you, you actually like the movie.  You don\'t tell \r\nanyone at first, hoping it was just a bad dream, but then you see it again.  \r\nYou laugh some more and cry some more and finally realize, you can\'t fight \r\nit, it\'s gonna happen.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/little_women.jpg\' width=\'167\' height=\'227\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(63, 3, 'Top 5 Movies with Ben Stiller', 'Meet the Parents', 'There\'s Something About Mary', 'The Royal Tenenbaums', 'Happy Gilmore', 'Dodgeball', '2004-06-20', 'Ben Stiller maintains his place as one of the smartest and funniest comics of the current generation.  Unfortunately, he\'s had some gutterballs along with the strikes.  Here are the best flicks in which he appears.', '<img src=\"images/meetparents.jpg\" alt=\"\" />', NULL, NULL, 1, 'top5'),
(31, 1, 'Top 5 Baseball Movies', 'Field of Dreams \r\n', 'Brewster\'s Millions \r\n', 'BASEketball  <div class=\'bodyText\'>(okay, this is a stretch for this category...  but a truly \r\nhilarious movie)</div>', 'Major League \r\n', 'Bull Durham ', '2001-07-19', 'In honor of my upcoming baseball road trip (The Bruce/Jesse BaseBrawl \r\n2001), I think it\'s only appropriate to name my Top 5 Basball Movies of all \r\ntime. ', '<img src=\'images/field_of_dreams.jpg\' width=\'159\' height=\'215\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(32, 6, 'Top 5 Trailers', 'Independence Day', 'Jurassic Park', 'Star Wars Episode I: The Phantom Menace', 'X-Men', 'Batman', '2001-07-20', 'I\'m really dating myself with this one.  I\'m sure there have been\r\nplenty of excellent trailers before these, but these are the ones that\r\nI have seen that blew me (and many audiences, I\'m sure) away.  By the\r\nway, does anyone know why they are called \"trailers\" even though they\r\ncome before the feature film?  ...but I digress.  Keep in mind, that\r\nthis list has nothing to do with how good the movie was, but how good\r\nthe trailer made it look.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/id4.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'201\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(33, 4, 'Top 5 Most Powerful Movie Endings', 'The Last of the Mohicans<div class=\'bodyText\'>The final sequence is full of action and bravery in the midst of some of the most powerful movie music.  The young lady\'s slow-motion suicide jump as tribute to her Indian love is unbearably courageous and romantic.</div>', 'The Shawshank Redemption<div class=\'bodyText\'>Some may be surprised by this one, but the final sequence of Red and Andy embracing on the beach as the camera fades away brings awesome closure to the movie and the theme of hope realized.</div>', 'The Karate Kid, Part I<div class=\'bodyText\'>My personal favorite; Daniel landing the winning kick doing the \"Crane Technique\" is a movie memory for all of us underdogs.</div>', 'Braveheart<div class=\'bodyText\'>\"Freedom!!\"  Principled courage under extreme torture is a definite winner.</div>', 'Vertigo<div class=\'bodyText\'>The movie doesn\'t even begin until the end.  Hitchcock shocks as the fates twist over and over until the final fall.</div><P><div class=\'quote\'>Honorable Mentions:</div> <div class=\'bodyText\'>The Usual Suspects, Primal Fear, Basic Instinct (all for shock value).</div>', '2001-07-23', 'There\'s simply nothing like a good ending to a movie.  The perfect end brings closure and feeling of complete satisfaction to the movie-watching experience.  In fact, movies are often made (and un-made) based solely upon the final sequence.  This list celebrates those flicks with tremendous endings-endings of drama, joy, courage, and surprise.  This list is by no means exhaustive for there are too many great endings that deserved mention but were arbitrarily left off due to the top five parameters.', '<img src=\'images/mohicans.jpg\' width=\'167\' height=\'213\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(35, 3, 'Top 5 Most Shocking Movie Endings', 'Arlington Road', 'Unbreakable', 'The Sixth Sense', 'Se7en', 'The Usual Suspects', '2001-07-24', 'Undoubtedly there are many classic movies that probably deserve to be in this list.  I\'m sure films like <i>Psycho</i>, \r\n<i>The Empire Strikes Back</i>, <i>The Crying Game</i>, and <i>Soylent Green</i> stunned theater-goers at the time \r\nof their release, but by the time I saw any of them, I already knew the ending.  So unfortunately, most such flicks \r\nwere less shocking to me personally because their outcome has already become part of the American culture.  \r\n<P>\r\nHaving said that, the following are the five films that blew me farthest out of my seat:', '<img src=\'images/arlington_rd.jpg\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(37, 3, 'Top 5 Actors to Avoid Like the Plague', 'Carrot Top<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Calling this guy an \"actor\" is an insult to the profession, but there\'s no doubt that watching any movie he\'s in will make you want to cut your face.</div>', 'Michael Dudikoff<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Star of many substandard action films such as \"American Ninja\" and \"Platoon Leader\".</div>', 'Corey Haim<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Thankfully, nobody\'s seen this guy since the 80\'s, which is pretty sad considering he actually is still making new movies.</div>', 'Brendan Fraser<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Does this one really need explaining?  One word: \"Monkeybone\".</div>', 'Pauly Shore<div class=\'bodyText\'>-- Eh, sometimes the guy\'s moderately amusing.  But he\'s still just a former MTV veejay.</div>', '2001-07-27', 'Sometimes you can tell a movie\'s going to suck the instant you see the opening credits.  And rest assured, if you ever see one of these names at the beginning of the movie, chances are you won\'t stick around to see it again at the end.\r\n<P>\r\nHonorable mention goes to Rosie O\'Donnell, who missed the list solely because she\'s not a man (then again, neither is Carrot Top).', '<img src=\'images/carrot_top.jpg\' width=\'142\' height=\'200\'>', 'Oh, the humanity...', '', 1, 'top5'),
(38, 1, 'Top 5 Kevin Costner Movies To Watch When You\'re Debating His Worth As An Actor', 'The Postman', 'Field of Dreams', '3000 Miles to Graceland', 'JFK', 'Waterworld', '2001-08-09', 'He\'s good.\r\n<p>\r\nHe sucks.\r\n<p>\r\nHe\'s good.\r\n<p>\r\nHe sucks.\r\n<p>\r\nIt\'s the age old debate.  Just when I start to like Kevin Costner as an actor, he puts out a bloated box-office bomb which makes me question my thinking.  He\'s made good films.  He\'s made bad films.  He\'s made downright gay films.  These are the ones to watch if you want to either (a) have a personal inner debate or (2) throw a Costner Sucks/Rules Block Party.  \r\n<p>\r\nRock on.', '<img src=\'images/headshot.gif\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(39, 2, 'Top 5 Worst Sequels of All Time', 'Superman IV <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1987)</div>\r\n', 'Caddy Shack 2 <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1988)</div>\r\n', 'Meatballs 2 <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1984)</div>', 'Problem Child 3 <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1995)</div>\r\n', 'Police Academy: Mission to Moscow <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1994)</div>\r\n', '2001-08-26', '   I\'ve been less than quiet in my opinion about sequels, particularly this \r\nsummer\'s stinkers.  The whole deal with sequels is to continue telling a \r\nstory about characters after one film ends.  The reality of sequels is for \r\nHollywood to whore out good ideas to make more money. This is no more \r\nevident than in these five movies that I hope no one saw other than me.', '<img src=\'images/superman4.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'220\'>', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(40, 2, 'Top 5 Historically Accurate Movies of All Time', 'Michael Collins <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1996)</div>', 'Thirteen Days <div class=\'bodyText\'>(2000)</div>', 'Gettysburg <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1994)</div>', 'Gandhi <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1982)</div>', 'Schindler\'s List <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1993)</div>', '2001-09-02', '   Being a historian I take the accuracy of historical films seriously.  To this day I can hardly watch Braveheart without shouting at the screen \"The \r\nScotts didn\'t wear blue war paint!\" and then be asked to leave the electronics section of Wal*Mart.  As far as my top fives go, you may notice Saving Private Ryan is missing.  Saving Private Ryan is the greatest war \r\nfilm and accurately depicts the storming of Normandy but the rest of the film was a fictitious story.  So without further delay, my top five picks.', '<img src=\'images/michael_collins.jpg\' width=\'159\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(41, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Westerns of All Time', 'The Good, the Bad and the Ugly <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1966) For all of you Actors Studio Guild Fans a.k.a Buono, il Brutto, il Cattiuo</div>', 'The Magnificent Seven <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1960 )</div>', 'Hondo <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1953)</div>', 'Tombstone <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1993)</div>', 'How the West Was Won <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1962)</div>', '2001-09-02', 'I\'m a big western fan.  I could never get into the Gene Autry singing \r\ncowboy stuff however.  The greatest westerns have to be the epic films,  \r\nfilms that make the west appear to be as big as it was in real life.', '<img src=\'images/good_bad_ugly.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(42, 2, 'Top 5 Definitive Corey Feldman Movies', 'Stand By Me <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1986)</div>', 'The Lost Boys <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1987)</div>', 'The Goonies <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1985)</div>', 'Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1990) He played the voice of Donatello</div>', 'The \'Burbs <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1989)</div>', '2001-09-02', 'Of all the actors in the world, Corey Feldman is one of them.', '<img src=\'images/stand_by_me.jpg\' width=\'166\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(43, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Actors Who Had Mustaches', 'Peter Sellers', 'Marlon Brando', 'Burt Reynolds', 'Tom Selleck', 'Raul Julia', '2001-09-03', 'Francis Ford Coppola once discussed his theory of choosing actors to play \r\ncertain roles.  He broke it into three basic categories.  One category he \r\ndid not use was the actors with mustaches catagory. Oftentimes actors, and \r\nmany actresses, don\'t look good with mustaches. But here are five that do.', '<img src=\'images/peter_sellers.jpg\' width=\'173\' height=\'213\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(44, 2, 'Top 5 Finest Mike Myers Films', 'Waynes World <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1992 )</div>', 'Austin Powers: International Man of Mystery <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1997 )</div>', 'So I Married an Axe Murderer <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1993 )</div>', '54 <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1998 )</div>', 'Shrek <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 2001 )</div>', '2001-09-16', '   I\'ve been a fan of Mike Myers since his days on Saturday Night Live.  No \r\nother performer from SNL has had the success that Myers has had in recent \r\nyears since leaving the show.  Here\'s his top five.', '<img src=\'images/waynes_world.jpg\' width=\'160\' height=\'208\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(45, 2, 'Top 5 Good Tom Hanks Films That Were Not Nominated for a Major Award', 'Joe vs The Volcano <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1990)</div>', 'A League of Their Own <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1992)</div>', 'Punchline <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1988)</div>', 'That Thing You Do <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1996)</div>', 'Splash <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1984)</div>', '2001-09-16', '   Tom Hanks is arguably the finest actor of our generation.  This doesn\'t \r\nneed to be said by a humble movie watcher such as myself.  Anyone could see \r\nhis collection of Oscars and Golden Globes on the mantle of his home if he\'d \r\nlet you.  Which he probably wouldn\'t.\r\n<P>\r\n(Just two side notes: I almost placed <i>A League of Their Own</i> as number one, \r\nbut then remembered Rosie O\'Donnell was in that film. <i>Joe vs the Volcano</i> \r\nwas made number one because Tom Hanks fought a volcano. \'Nuff said. \r\nSecondly, \"Bosom Buddies\" should be mentioned, a great televison program about \r\ncross-dressing that was over looked by the Emmys.)', '<img src=\'images/joe.jpg\' width=\'134\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(46, 2, 'Top 5 Worst SNL Movies of All Time', 'Stuart Saves His Family <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1995)</div>', 'It\'s Pat <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1994)</div>', 'Coneheads <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1993)</div>', 'Superstar <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1999)</div>', 'Night at the Roxbury <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1998)</div>', '2001-09-17', '    Saturday Night Live has given us some memorable characters who\'ve been \r\neven greater on the silver screen.  Jake and Elwood Blues, Wayne and Garth \r\nand yes, even Leon Phelps.  But there is a darker side.  If you haven\'t seen \r\nthese, consider yourself lucky.  Here are the five worst SNL character \r\nmovies of all time.', '<img src=\'images/stuart_saves.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'210\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(47, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Star Trek Motion Pictures', 'Star Trek II: Wrath of Khan <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1982 )</div>', 'Star Trek: Generations <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1994 )</div>', 'Star Trek V: The Final Frontier <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1989 )</div>', 'Star Trek VI: The Undiscovered Country <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1991 )</div>', 'Star Trek: First Contact <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1996 )</div>', '2001-10-02', '    Should these be called anything else? It\'s not like they are motionless \r\npictures.  Anyway, I had been thinking about this one for a while. Since TNN \r\nhas been showing all of the original cast Star Trek movies four times a week \r\nfor the past eight months and HBO has been showing the other three a great \r\ndeal, I\'m familiar with all of them.  The number one choice was easy.  This \r\nwas a great film no matter what.  A great hero, a great villian (played by \r\nRicardo Montalban without Tattoo) and one of the most talked-about endings in the science fiction world for years.', '<img src=\'images/startrek2.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(48, 2, 'Top 5 Funniest Sports Comedies', 'Caddyshack <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1980 )</div>', 'The Great White Hype <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1996 )</div>', 'Baseketball <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1998 )</div>', 'Major League <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1989 )</div>', 'Happy Gilmore <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1996 )</div>', '2001-10-04', '   Buster Keaton played shortstop for his high school baseball team.  One \r\nwould suspect that with Keaton\'s comic genius and his athletic ability he \r\nwould find these five movies dealing with sports to be the funniest.  I \r\nwould agree.  Just a side note, if you haven\'t seen <i>The Great White Hype</i> and \r\nyou\'re a fan of <i>Pulp Fiction</i> there\'s a subtle tie-in that I thought was \r\nfunny.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/caddyshack.jpg\' width=\'157\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(49, 2, 'Top 5 Comedic Duos of the Motion Pictures', 'Abbott & Costello', 'Laurel & Hardy', 'Richard Pryor and Gene Wilder', 'Jason Mewes and Kevin Smith <div class=\'bodyText\'>( Jay & Silent Bob )</div>', 'Matt Stone and Trey Parker', '2001-10-04', '  Getting an actor to do a movie is easy, making a comedy is more difficult. \r\n<P>\r\n  Getting two actors to be funny in one movie is possible, but getting the \r\nsame two actors to be funny in more than one movie is damn near impossible.  \r\nThe following five comedic duo\'s all deserve to be number one, but in my \r\nopinion Bud Abbott and Lou Costello perfected the funny guy/straight guy \r\nroutine that has shaped contemporary comedy.  Many of you might wonder where \r\nLewis and Martin are on my list.  I honestly never found Jerry Lewis,  the \r\nPauly Shore of the 40\'s, very funny.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/Societyb.jpg\' width=\'180\' height=\'183\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(50, 2, 'Top 5 Comedic Troupes of Motion Pictures', 'The Marx Brothers', 'The Three Stooges', 'Monty Python\'s Flying Circus', 'The Kids in the Hall', 'The Keystone Kops', '2001-10-04', '   If getting two actors together in more than one funny movie is damn near \r\nimpossible (see <a href=\'top5.php3?id=49\'>Top 5 Comedic Duos of the Motion Pictures</a>), then getting a bunch of \r\npeople <u>is</u> impossible.  But it has been done with wonderful results.\r\n<P>\r\n    Just a couple of notes, I know that not putting the Stooges first is \r\nblasphemy, but the Marx Brothers utilized both physical comedy and dialog \r\nbetter in my opinion.  The Kids in the Hall are better known for their \r\ntelevison show but they did make one film.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/marx.jpg\' width=\'179\' height=\'236\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(51, 3, 'Top 5 Perennial Psychos', 'Jack Nicholson\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\nThe absolute, hands-down, inarguable winner.  I could put Jack in all five spots in this list and still not be\r\noverstating my point.  <i>The Shining</i>, <i>One Flew Over the Cuckoo\'s Nest</i>, <i>Batman</i>, \r\n<i>As Good as it Gets</i>... this guy just doesn\'t stop.\r\n</div>', 'Christopher Walken\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>Just seeing him is usually enough to completely weird me out.</div>', 'Robert Blake\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\nIt\'s possible he may have carried his Hollywood persona a little too far into his personal life.  But if you were\r\nmarried to that woman, wouldn\'t you be crazy, too?  Anyways, nobody\'s better at playing the tortured soul, and\r\nno matter how many times I see him in <i>Lost Highway</i>, I still soil myself.\r\n</div>', 'Joe Pesci\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>\r\nPesci is the king of flying off the handle at the slightest provocation.  This guy is responsible for more\r\ndeaths-by-kicking than Chuck Norris and Jackie Chan combined.\r\n</div>', 'Steve Buscemi\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>Usually more quirky than creepy, the guy is insane nevertheless.</div>\r\n<div class=\'headline\'>Honorable Mention:  Brad Pitt</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'><i>12 Monkeys</i> and <i>Fight Club</i> showed this guy can be more than just a pretty face.</div>\r\n<div class=\'headline\'>Honorable Mention:  Roberto Benigni</div>\r\n<div class=\'bodyText\'>I don\'t know if this really counts, because he\'s not acting; the man truly is insane.  He\'s\r\ngot some wires crossed or something.</div>', '2001-10-04', 'A lot of actors and actresses seem to hate being type-cast.  Some apparently don\'t mind it.  And others are just\r\nso damn good at playing certain roles that it would be a crime if they stopped accepting them.  With that in\r\nmind, here are the guys that are the absolute kings of the hill when it comes to playing creeps, nutjobs, and\r\njust plain psychos.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/shining.jpg\' width=\'146\' height=\'230\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(52, 2, 'Top 5 Best John Cusack Films', 'High Fidelity', 'Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil', 'Being John Malkovich', 'Grosse Point Blank', 'Con-Air', '2001-10-12', '  John Cusack is the hip version of Tom Hanks.  Hanks plays the roles of \r\nsensitive lovers (<i>Sleepless in Seattle</i>) and heroic World War II soldier (<i>Saving Private Ryan</i>), \r\nCusack plays the oddball lover (<i>High Fidelity</i>) and \r\nthe just-trying-not-to-get-killed World War II soldier\r\n(<i>The Thin Red Line</i>).  Nonetheless he is a gifted actor with a hotty \r\nsister. Ok, I\'m only kidding, I only find her mildly attractive.', '<img src=\'images/high_fidelity.jpg\' width=\'141\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(53, 2, 'Top 5 Films Where Jeff Goldblum Says A Lot of Stuff', 'The Fly ', 'Jurassic Park', 'Powder', 'Independence Day', 'Holy Man', '2001-10-12', '   Simply put into laymans terms, Jeff Goldblum has the divine ability to \r\nuse words in sentences that are made up of a complex series of innate \r\nsounds.  These sounds dictate to the listener, visions of extraordinary, or \r\nshould I say, elaborate concepts.  Goldblum needs only to incorporate these \r\nwords, for a simpler term, into lines.  These lines, if you will, are \r\nprojected from his psyche which is now embodied in a character that he \r\nportrays in a film.  Goldblum divides the superego and ego of the character \r\nonly to reunite both into the id of the audience who is now spellbound by \r\nhis incoherent ramblings both in his native tongue and in Esperanto.  \r\nGoldblum manipulates the contagion, begining with an \"oooh\" and \"ah\" \r\nwhich turns into running and then screaming by any creature.', '<img src=\'images/fly.jpg\' width=\'138\' height=\'250\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(54, 2, 'Top 5 Finest Samuel L. Jackson Performances', 'Jackie Brown', 'Shaft', 'Pulp Fiction', 'The Negotiator', 'The Long Kiss Goodnight', '2001-10-25', '  Samuel L. Jackson is arguably the greatest African American actor of all \r\ntime.  In every film Jackson is in, he always portrays a persona that is \r\nunique.  In laymen\'s terms, he\'s cool.  In all of his films he\'s cool, dare I \r\nsay, cooler than Fonzie.\r\n<P>\r\n    This, by far, is the most difficult top five I\'ve ever done.  It\'s \r\nimpossible to limit Jackson\'s work to five best.  I\'m sure the film world \r\nwill call me a heretic by my rankings and leaving off the films <i>A Time to Kill</i> \r\n(1996) and <i>Eve\'s Bayou</i> (1997) but in my defense I\'ve never seen them.  My \r\nnumber one film, <i>Jackie Brown</i>, is number one because it was Jackson who \r\ncarried the film.  Otherwise <i>Jackie Brown</i> would have been a total disaster \r\nin my opinion.  Other honorable mentions would be <i>Unbreakable</i> (2000) and \r\n<i>The Great White Hype</i> (1997).', '<img src=\'images/jackie_brown.jpg\' width=\'146\' height=\'205\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(55, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Christmas Movies of All Time', 'Ernest Saves Christmas', 'It\'s A Wonderful Life', 'National Lampoon\'s Christmas Vacation', 'Trapped in Paradise', 'Home Alone', '2001-11-15', '  Christmas time is a special time for movies.  These five are the greatest \r\nand most heart warming of all time. I didn\'t put <i>It\'s A Wonderful Life</i> as \r\nnumber one, which might be blasphemy to some, but Ernest did save \r\nChristmas; Jimmy Stewart just saved a bank.', '<img src=\'images/ernest_saves_christmas.jpg\' width=\'121\' height=\'214\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(56, 2, 'Top 5 Worst Christmas Movies of All Time', 'Santa With Muscles', 'The Santa Clause', 'The Grinch Who Stole Christmas', 'I\'ll Be Home for Christmas ', 'The Nightmare Before Christmas', '2001-11-15', '   Some of the worst films ever deal with Christmas.  I think the evil \r\nHollywood Execs try and capitalize on the heartfelt sentiment of the season \r\nand exploit it.  Why can\'t they make bad holiday films around stupid \r\nholidays like Arbor Day.  The world may never know.\r\n<P>\r\n   Just as a side note, I do belive that my number one was the worst film \r\never in my opinion.  Hulk Hogan + Ed Beagley Jr. + Santa foiling burglars \r\ndoes not = Oscar.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/santa_muscles.jpg\' width=\'168\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(57, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Stanley Kubrick Films', 'Dr. Strangelove, or: How I Learned to Stop Worrying and Love the Bomb <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1964)</div>', 'Lolita <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1962)</div>', 'Full Metal Jacket <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1987)</div>', 'A Clockwork Orange <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1971)</div>', 'The Shining <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1980)</div>', '2001-11-15', '   Kubrick is one of those directors who did not make quantity films but \r\nquality films.  All of Kubrick\'s films have a dark side to them, a quality \r\nthat is unmistakeable.\r\n<P>\r\n  My number one and two films in my opinion have the greatest casts.  \r\nSlim Pickens, George C. Scott and of course the greatest actor with a \r\nmustache, Peter Sellers.', '<img src=\'images/strangelove.jpg\' width=\'156\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(58, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Christopher Lloyd Films', 'Back to the Future', 'Things To Do In Denver When You\'re Dead', 'The Addams Family', 'Dream Team', 'The Legend of the Lone Ranger', '2001-11-28', '   Christopher Lloyd is a mainstay in American cinema.  He\'s accomplished a \r\nwide range of films, from comedy to westerns, film noir to science fiction.  \r\nHe\'s an underrated actor and box office draw.\r\n<P>\r\n    A bit of clarification on my list is probably needed.  <i>Things to Do in \r\nDenver when your Dead</i> was a film starring Andy Garcia, Christopher Walken \r\nand Steve Buscemi, it\'s a great film.  <i>The Lone Ranger</i>, although not one of \r\nthe most critically acclaimed films, was very good due to Christopher Lloyd \r\nportraying a villain.  Plus it\'s one of the first films I can remember my \r\nparents taking me to see at the drive-in.', '<img src=\'images/back_to_future.jpg\' width=\'144\' height=\'217\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5');
INSERT INTO `Top5` (`ID_`, `Author_ID_FK`, `Title`, `Item1`, `Item2`, `Item3`, `Item4`, `Item5`, `Article_date`, `Intro`, `Image`, `Caption`, `Image_fullsize`, `QA`, `Type`) VALUES
(59, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest George Clooney Films', 'O Brother, Where Art Thou <div class=\'bodyText\'>(2000)</div>', 'Out of Sight <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1998)</div>', 'Perfect Storm <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1998)</div>', 'From Dusk Till Dawn <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1996)</div>', 'Three Kings <div class=\'bodyText\'>(1999)</div>', '2001-12-19', '   This week saw the opening of <i>Oceans\' Eleven</i>, a remake of a Rat Pack film.  \r\nWho plays the part of Frank? George Clooney.  How did he do? It\'s \r\ndebatable for two reasons.  The first is that Frank Sinatra never played a \r\ncharacter in a movie, he just acted the way he wanted to and the character \r\nacted around him.  Clooney can pull this off sometimes.  The second reason \r\nit\'s debatable on whether or not Clooney did the Sinatra film justice, is that I haven\'t \r\nseen it.\r\n<P>\r\n     Clooney, who was best known for his role as Booker on the sit-com \r\nRoseanne had done quite a bit afterwards.  He starred in <i>Attack of the \r\nKiller Tomatoes, part II</i> and played an undercover cop acting as a doctor on \r\nthe television series ER.  At least that\'s what I\'ve been told, I never saw that show \r\neither.  On with the top five.', '<img src=\'images/obrother.jpg\' width=\'147\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(60, 2, 'Top 5 Hitchcock Movies', 'Psycho <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1960 )</div>', 'Strangers on a Train <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1955 )</div>', 'Rear Window <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1954 )</div>', 'The Man Who Knew Too Much <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1956 )</div>', 'North by Northwest <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1959 )</div>', '2001-12-19', '   This top five was as difficult as the one I did on Samuel L. Jackson.  \r\nHitch is the director all the great directors want to be.  He was perfecting \r\nfilm techniques that weren\'t even imagined.  Every one of his films is a \r\nmasterpiece and deseves to be number one, but there is one that is first \r\namong equals.', '<img src=\'images/hitchcock2.jpg\' width=\'100\' height=\'134\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(61, 2, 'Top 5 John Goodman Films', 'The Big Lebowski <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1998 )</div>', 'O Brother, Where Art Thou <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 2000 )</div>', 'Arachnophobia <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1990 )</div>', 'One Night at McCool\'s <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 2001 )</div>', 'Fallen <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1998 )</div>', '2001-12-27', '   John Goodman is not a leading man, but he\'s probably one of the greatest \r\nco-stars in film history.  His roles in film aren\'t always the largest, but \r\nhe plays them to the fullest.  Goodman, by far, is the largest small role \r\nactor in Hollywood.\r\n<P>\r\n    Picking out five of Goodman\'s best films was tough.  I stayed away from \r\nthe cartoons, for example, <i>Monsters Inc</i>. Even though Goodman was good in \r\nthem the cartoons don\'t do him justice.  Goodman can say a great deal with \r\njust his facial expressions.  Far and away my favorite Goodman film is <i>The \r\nBig Lebowski</i> (1998).  Not only was it directed by the Coen Brothers, it \r\nhad one of my favorite actors in it, Steve Buscemi and it had one of my \r\nfavorite lines of all time in it spoken by Goodman:  \"You see Larry ! This \r\nis what you get when you fuck a stranger in the ass!\"  Classic.', '<img src=\'images/big_lebowski.jpg\' width=\'151\' height=\'215\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(62, 2, 'Top 5 Greatest Bill Murray Films', 'What About Bob <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1991 )</div>', 'Where the Buffalo Roam <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1980 )</div>', 'Ghostbusters <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1984 )</div>', 'Caddyshack <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1980 )</div>', 'Kingpin <div class=\'bodyText\'>( 1996 )</div>', '2001-12-27', '   Bill Murray is as versatile an actor as one can imagine.  He has played \r\neverything from a burned out gardener, a camp counselor, to a World War I \r\nsoldier in search of enlightenment.  In every role he has this type of \r\ncomedic arrogance that only he can pull off.  A brilliant, brilliant man.\r\n    Most of these films the layman has seen, except <i>Where the Buffalo Roam</i> ( 1980 ).  \r\n	In this film, Murray plays eccentric journalist Dr. Hunter S. \r\nThompson, a role Johnny Depp would play some fifteen years later.  If you \r\nhaven\'t seen this one, go rent it.  You will laugh, you may not get it, but \r\nyou will definitely laugh.\r\n', '<img src=\'images/whataboutbob.jpg\' width=\'153\' height=\'220\' alt=\'\' />', '', '', 1, 'top5'),
(64, 3, 'Top 5 Movies Starring Bruce Willis', 'Unbreakable', 'Pulp Fiction', 'Twelve Monkeys', 'Die Hard', 'The Sixth Sense', '2004-07-06', 'Bruce Willis is one of those actors whose agent seems to frequently go off on extended crack binges.  Bruce has done eight or ten great films, but maybe twice as many crappy ones.   Here are the ones to watch:', '<img src=\'images/unbreakable.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, NULL, 1, 'top5'),
(65, 3, 'Top 5 Movies Starring Brad Pitt', 'Se7en', 'Twelve Monkeys', 'Fight Club', 'Sleepers', 'Ocean\'s Eleven', '2004-07-06', 'Brad Pitt is a goddamned pretty-boy, but surprisingly enough, he\'s a pretty good actor.  His appeal to the ladies turns out to be a good thing for movie-watching guys, because you can often convince your woman to watch a good, violent Brad Pitt movie instead of having to gag your way through some crappy chick flick.  Yay, everybody\'s happy.  ', '<img src=\'images/seven.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, NULL, 1, 'top5'),
(66, 3, 'Top 5 Movies Starring Kevin Spacey', 'American Beauty', 'L.A. Confidential', 'Se7en', 'The Usual Suspects', 'The Life of David Gale', '2004-07-06', 'These were really hard to put in order, and you could argue pretty convincingly for any of the top four to be Number One.  Ultimately, I ordered them primarily based on the size of Spacey\'s role in the movie, but bumped <i>The Usual Suspects</i> a little lower because it\'s not quite as good in repeated viewings as it was the first time.  My guess is that <i>Midnight in the Garden of Good and Evil</i> should also be in this list, but unfortunately I haven\'t seen that one yet.', '<img src=\'images/american_beauty.jpg\' alt=\'\' />', NULL, NULL, 1, 'top5');
